(window.LINGUA_BOOK=window.LINGUA_BOOK||{}).condense=Object.assign(window.LINGUA_BOOK.condense||{},{"part-007-chapter-7-semantics":{"title":{"en":"Chapter 7: Semantics","zh":"第 7 章：语义学"},"summary":{"en":"Chapter 7 is about linguistic meaning, specifically semantics: how the meanings of words combine to form the meanings of sentences. It begins with lexical meaning, asking what is stored in the mental lexicon for a word and evaluating competing theories of lexical meaning. The rest of the chapter uses case studies across categories and languages to reveal the complexity of human semantic competence.","zh":"第 7 章讨论语言意义，尤其是语义学：词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。本章先考察词汇意义，即心理词库中为词储存的意义内容，并评述各种词汇意义理论的优缺点。其余各节通过跨词类、跨语言的意义个案研究，展现人类语义能力的复杂性。"},"points":[{"en":"Semantics studies linguistic meaning, especially how the meanings of words combine into sentence meanings.","zh":"语义学研究语言意义，尤其关注词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。"},{"en":"Lexical meaning concerns what is stored in the mental lexicon for a word and other small expressions.","zh":"词汇意义关注心理词库中为词和其他较小表达式所储存的意义内容。"},{"en":"Several competing theories of lexical meaning exist, and the chapter weighs the pros and cons of each.","zh":"关于词汇意义存在多种相互竞争的理论，本章逐一评估其优点与不足。"},{"en":"Case studies across categories and languages show how the meaning of one word combines with another.","zh":"跨词类、跨语言的个案研究展示一个词的意义如何与另一个词的意义组合。"},{"en":"The chapter aims to explain why the dictionary is not the ultimate authority on linguistic meaning.","zh":"本章力图说明，词典并不是语言意义的最终权威。"}],"terms":[{"en":"semantics","zh":"语义学","defEn":"The study of linguistic meaning, including how word meanings form sentence meanings.","defZh":"研究语言意义的学科，关注词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。"},{"en":"lexical meaning","zh":"词汇意义","defEn":"The meaning carried by words and other small expressions stored in the mental lexicon.","defZh":"词以及储存在心理词库中的其他较小表达式所承载的意义内容。"},{"en":"mental lexicon","zh":"心理词库","defEn":"The store of words and their meanings in a speaker's mind.","defZh":"语言使用者头脑中储存词语及其意义的知识库，即心理词库。"},{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The meaning of a word as stored in the minds of speakers.","defZh":"语言使用者头脑中储存的、关于某个词或表达式的意义内容。"},{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延/指称","defEn":"What a linguistic expression points to in the actual world.","defZh":"语言表达式在现实世界中实际指向的具体对象或事物，如草莓这种水果。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"Chapter 7 deals with linguistic meaning, and more precisely with semantics: how the meaning of words combines to form the meaning of sentences. It opens with lexical meaning, that is, what goes into the meaning of a word and of the other small expressions stored in your mental lexicon, and it surveys several theories of lexical meaning while weighing their advantages and disadvantages.","zh":"第 7 章讨论语言意义，更准确地说讨论语义学：词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。本章从词汇意义入手，即一个词以及心理词库中其他较小表达式的意义由什么构成，并评述若干种词汇意义理论，权衡其优缺点。"},{"en":"The second half of the chapter turns to case studies of linguistic meaning across categories and across languages, asking along the way what it means for the meaning of one word to combine with the meaning of another. Learners are expected to explain why the dictionary is not the ultimate authority on meaning, to analyse meaning critically from descriptive observations, to distinguish sense from denotation, and to use diagnostics for entailments, implicatures and presuppositions.","zh":"本章后半部分转向跨词类和跨语言的意义个案研究，并在过程中思考一个词的意义与另一个词的意义相结合究竟意味着什么。学习者应能说明词典为何不是意义的最终权威，能依据描写性观察批判地分析意义，能区分涵义与外延，并能运用诊断手段识别蕴涵、会话含义和预设。"},{"en":"Throughout, the chapter insists that linguistic meaning comes in many different kinds, and that each way of thinking about meaning yields important insight into how language users produce and understand meaning. The data examined across categories and languages are meant to foster appreciation of the complexity and diversity of human semantic competence.","zh":"全章始终强调，语言意义存在许多不同的类型，每一种思考意义的方式都能为理解语言使用者如何产出和理解意义提供重要洞见。通过考察跨词类、跨语言的材料，本章希望学习者体会人类语义能力的复杂性与多样性。"}]},"chapter-074-slug-7-1-linguistic-meaning":{"title":{"en":"7.1 Linguistic meaning","zh":"7.1 语言意义"},"summary":{"en":"The word meaning is used in everyday life in several ways, but not all of them count as linguistic meaning. A tea stalk that symbolises good luck, a Japanese imi that concerns the purpose of an action, and an insincere remark are excluded or postponed to pragmatics. Linguistic meaning is illustrated by ode'imin and strawberry, which mean the same thing because they point to the same fruit.","zh":"日常生活中 meaning 一词有多种用法，但并非都属于语言意义。象征好运的茶梗、表示行为目的的日语 imi，以及言不由衷的说法都被排除在外，或留待语用学讨论。真正的语言意义以 ode'imin 与 strawberry 为例：两者指同一事物，因而意义相同。"},"points":[{"en":"Semantics is the study of linguistic meaning, especially how word meanings combine into sentence meanings.","zh":"语义学研究语言意义，尤其关注词义如何组合成句义。"},{"en":"Everyday talk about tea stalks, purposes, or sincerity falls outside the linguistic meaning studied here.","zh":"关于茶梗象征、行为目的或言不由衷的日常说法，不属于此处研究的语言意义。"},{"en":"Meaning attaches to a linguistic expression such as ode'imin, not to the actual fruit it names.","zh":"意义附着于 ode'imin 这样的语言表达式，而非它所指称的实际水果。"},{"en":"Frege distinguished sense, the meaning stored in the mind, from denotation, what an expression points to.","zh":"弗雷格区分了涵义（头脑中储存的意义）与指称（表达式所指的对象）。"},{"en":"Because ode'imin and strawberry denote the same fruit, they can be said to mean the same thing.","zh":"由于 ode'imin 与 strawberry 指称同一种水果，二者可以说意义相同。"}],"terms":[{"en":"semantics","zh":"语义学","defEn":"The study of linguistic meaning, including how word meanings combine into sentence meanings.","defZh":"研究语言意义的学科，关注词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。"},{"en":"linguistic expression","zh":"语言表达式","defEn":"The words, phrases and sentences produced by language users.","defZh":"语言使用者产出和理解的词、短语与句子，例如 ode'imin 这样的表达式。"},{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The meaning of a word as stored in the minds of language users.","defZh":"语言使用者头脑中所储存的、关于某个词或表达式的意义内容。"},{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延/指称","defEn":"What a linguistic expression points to in the actual world.","defZh":"语言表达式在现实世界中所指向的对象，如 strawberry 指向草莓这种水果。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Ode’imin and strawberry mean the same thing.","noteEn":"Shows that two words from different languages can mean the same thing.","noteZh":"说明不同语言的两个词可以意义相同：奥吉布瓦语的 ode'imin 与英语的 strawberry 指同一事物。"},{"text":"‘There is no point in buying an expensive sewing machine if you don’t know how to use it’ (Japanese)","noteEn":"Here imi concerns the purpose of an action, so it is not linguistic meaning.","noteZh":"此处的 imi 关乎行为的目的，因此不是本书所讲的语言意义。"},{"text":"I said coffee is just as tasty as tea, but I didn’t mean it.","noteEn":"This use concerns sincerity and is left to Chapter 8 on pragmatics.","noteZh":"这种用法关乎说话人的诚意，留待第 8 章语用学讨论。"},{"text":"ode’imin (IPA: [odeimin]); strawberry (IPA: [stɹɔbɛɹi])","noteEn":"The two sound sequences whose attached meaning the chapter asks about.","noteZh":"本节所追问的、附着有意义的两个语音序列及其国际音标注音。"}],"body":[{"en":"The English word semantics comes from the Ancient Greek word meaning ‘significant’ (sēmantikós), which in turn comes from a word meaning ‘to show, signify’ (sēmaínein). Semantics is the study of linguistic meaning: how the meaning of words combines to form the meaning of sentences. Not every everyday use of mean or meaning belongs here. In Japanese culture a tea stalk floating vertically may “mean” good luck, yet the tea stalk is not a linguistic expression; and in the Japanese sentence about an expensive sewing machine, imi concerns the purpose of an action rather than language itself.","zh":"英语的 semantics 一词源自表示“有意义的”的古希腊语词 sēmantikós，后者又来自表示“显示、表示”的词 sēmaínein。语义学研究语言意义，即词的意义如何组合成句子的意义。日常生活中 mean 或 meaning 的用法并非都属于此。在日本文化中，竖立在绿茶中的茶梗或许“表示”好运，但茶梗并不是语言表达式；而在那句关于昂贵缝纫机的日语例句中，imi 指的是行为的目的，并非语言本身。"},{"en":"The insincerity reading of I said coffee is just as tasty as tea, but I didn’t mean it is certainly about language, since we are evaluating what someone said, but it is a matter of sincerity and will be treated in Chapter 8 on pragmatics. The example that best illustrates linguistic meaning is Ode’imin and strawberry mean the same thing. ode’imin is an Ojibwe (Anishinaabemowin) word and strawberry an English word, yet both carry content that lets speakers say yes or no when deciding whether a given object is an ode’imin or a strawberry.","zh":"“我说咖啡和茶一样好喝，但我并不是那个意思”这种言不由衷的解读当然与语言有关，因为我们是在评价某人所说的话，但它涉及诚意问题，将留到第 8 章语用学处理。最能体现语言意义的例子是 Ode’imin and strawberry mean the same thing。ode’imin 是奥吉布瓦语（Anishinaabemowin）词，strawberry 是英语词，但两者都带有某种内容，使说话人能够就某一对象是否算作 ode’imin 或 strawberry 作出肯定或否定的判断。"},{"en":"What this chapter asks is what knowledge the speaker stores about the expression ode’imin (IPA: [odeimin]) or strawberry (IPA: [stɹɔbɛɹi]) such that it points to that juicy red fruit with small seeds on the surface. Meaning attaches to the linguistic expression, not to the fruit in real life, and the same holds for signed languages such as American Sign Language. Since meaning can be viewed in two ways, Frege (1892) distinguished sense, the meaning stored in our heads, from denotation, what an expression points to: thinking in terms of denotation explains why ode’imin and strawberry “mean the same thing” and also helps us account for semantic patterns in language.","zh":"本节要追问的是：说话人头脑中关于 ode’imin（国际音标 [odeimin]）或 strawberry（国际音标 [stɹɔbɛɹi]）这一表达式储存了怎样的知识，使它指向那种表面带小籽、多汁的红色水果。意义附着于语言表达式，而不是现实中的水果；美国手语等手语也是如此。由于意义可以从两个角度理解，弗雷格（1892）区分了涵义（储存在我们头脑中的意义）与指称（表达式所指向的对象）：从指称角度思考，可以解释 ode’imin 与 strawberry 为何“意义相同”，也有助于说明语言中的语义规律。"}]},"chapter-075-slug-7-2-compositionality-why-not-just-syntax":{"title":{"en":"7.2 Compositionality: Why not just syntax?","zh":"7.2 组合性原则：为什么不能只谈句法？"},"summary":{"en":"A sentence you have never heard before is still comprehensible because the meanings of its morphemes are stored in your head and combine in a structured way. This is the principle of compositionality, and it applies to multi-morphemic words as well as to sentences. Yet syntax alone does not determine meaning, nor does meaning alone determine grammaticality, so syntax and semantics must be studied separately.","zh":"一个从未听过的句子之所以仍能理解，是因为其语素的意义储存在头脑中，并按一定结构组合起来，这就是组合性原则，它既适用于句子，也适用于多语素词。然而句法并不能完全决定意义，意义也不能单独决定是否合乎语法，因此句法与语义必须分别考察。"},"points":[{"en":"Compositionality holds that a complex expression's meaning comes from its parts and how they combine.","zh":"组合性原则认为，复杂表达式的意义来自其各部分的意义及组合方式。"},{"en":"A novel sentence about a raccoon is understood because each morpheme's meaning is stored and combined.","zh":"一个关于浣熊的全新句子之所以能被理解，是因为每个语素的意义都被储存并加以组合。"},{"en":"Syntactic constituency constrains semantic composition, and words forming a syntactic unit usually form a semantic unit.","zh":"句法成分制约语义组合，构成一个句法单位的词通常也构成一个语义单位。"},{"en":"Referential determiner phrases can be passivised, but predicative ones such as a scientist in (3) cannot.","zh":"指称性的限定词短语可以构成被动句，而 (3) 中 a scientist 这样的谓语性短语则不可以。"},{"en":"Before allows a clausal complement while during does not, even though both are compatible with event meanings.","zh":"before 允许小句补足语而 during 不允许，尽管两者都与事件意义相容。"}],"terms":[{"en":"compositionality","zh":"组合性原则","defEn":"The meaning of a complex unit results from its parts and how they combine.","defZh":"复杂语言单位的意义由其各部分的意义及这些部分的组合方式共同决定。"},{"en":"constituent","zh":"句法成分","defEn":"A syntactic unit or subunit within a larger structure.","defZh":"更大结构内部可以作为独立单位使用的句法成分，例如 that fluffy cat 或 the raccoon 等。"},{"en":"referential","zh":"指称性的","defEn":"Describing an expression that points to some existing entity.","defZh":"指表达式的意义在于指向现实世界中已经存在的某个具体实体。"},{"en":"predicative","zh":"谓语性的","defEn":"Describing an expression that expresses a property of the subject.","defZh":"指表达式表达主语所具有的某种属性，而不是指称某个实体。"},{"en":"determiner phrase","zh":"限定词短语","defEn":"A phrase headed by a determiner, such as a scientist.","defZh":"由限定词充当中心语的短语，例如英语中的 a scientist、the raccoon 这类形式的结构短语。"},{"en":"passive","zh":"被动","defEn":"A construction where the object of an active clause becomes the subject.","defZh":"主动句的宾语提升为主语句的句式，例如 A scientist was greeted by Vera，这类句子在英语中很常见。"}],"examples":[{"text":"The raccoon clothing store was doing a photo contest so I submitted a picture of a Toronto raccoon wearing a bright pink bandana with lime green polka dots on it.","noteEn":"A sentence you have likely never heard, yet you can understand it.","noteZh":"一个很可能从未听过的句子，但你仍然能够理解它的意义。"},{"text":"the word unhelpfulness is the combination of the meaning of un-, help, -ful, and -ness, put together as [ [ un- [ help -ful ] ] -ness ]","noteEn":"Compositionality applies to multi-morphemic words too, not only to sentences.","noteZh":"组合性原则不仅适用于句子，也适用于由多个语素构成的词。"},{"text":"Vera greeted a scientist. / Vera became a scientist. / A scientist was greeted by Vera. / *A scientist was become by Vera.","noteEn":"Same syntax, different meaning: only the referential determiner phrase passivises.","noteZh":"句法形式相同而意义不同：只有指称性的限定词短语才能构成被动句。"},{"text":"Alan sneezed before { he met with his student / he raced / he partied / he attended the event }. / *Alan sneezed during { he met with his student / he raced / he partied / he attended the event }.","noteEn":"Before takes a clausal complement, but during does not, so syntax matters too.","noteZh":"before 可以带小句补足语，during 则不能，说明句法同样起作用。"}],"body":[{"en":"The long sentence about the raccoon clothing store is probably one you have never heard before, yet you can comprehend it and know what it means. Each morpheme's meaning is stored in your head, and combining those meanings yields the meaning of the whole. This is the principle of compositionality: the meaning of a complex linguistic unit results from the individual meanings of its subparts and from how those subparts were combined. It governs sentences, and it equally governs multi-morphemic words such as unhelpfulness, whose meaning is the combination of un-, help, -ful and -ness arranged as [ [ un- [ help -ful ] ] -ness ].","zh":"那句关于浣熊服装店的长句你很可能从未听过，却仍能理解并知道它的意思。每个语素的意义都储存在你头脑中，把这些意义组合起来就得到整句的意义。这就是组合性原则：复杂语言单位的意义由其次级各部分的意义以及这些部分的组合方式产生。它既管句子，也同等地管多语素词，例如 unhelpfulness 的意义就是 un-、help、-ful 和 -ness 按 [ [ un- [ help -ful ] ] -ness ] 的方式组合的结果。"},{"en":"Compositionality concerns not only which pieces are combined but how they are combined, which connects it to constituency, or unithood, from Chapter 6. In That fluffy cat will chase the raccoon, that fluffy cat and the raccoon are constituents, and chase the raccoon is one too, whereas that fluffy cat will is not. Word meanings combine along the same structured path: the meaning of the and raccoon combine first, then with the meaning of chase, and so on. Syntax and semantics largely go hand in hand, since fluffy and cat form a noun phrase and also a semantic unit, with fluffy telling us what kind of cat is at issue.","zh":"组合性不仅涉及哪些成分被组合，也涉及它们如何被组合，这就与第 6 章的成分概念（单位性）联系起来。在 That fluffy cat will chase the raccoon 中，that fluffy cat 和 the raccoon 是句法成分，chase the raccoon 也是，而 that fluffy cat will 不是。词义也沿着同样的结构路径组合：先组合 the 与 raccoon 的意义，再与 chase 的意义组合，依此类推。句法与语义在很大程度上一同运作，因为 fluffy 与 cat 既构成名词短语，也构成一个语义单位，fluffy 告诉我们所谈的是哪一种猫。"},{"en":"The answer is no, not necessarily. Although a scientist is a determiner phrase in both Vera greeted a scientist and Vera became a scientist, it is referential in the first and predicative in the second, so only the first passivises: A scientist was greeted by Vera is grammatical, while *A scientist was become by Vera is not. Before and during are both semantically compatible with event expressions, and before even takes a full clause, but during does not, which is why *Alan sneezed during he met with his student is ungrammatical. Syntax matters for composition, yet semantics must be examined separately.","zh":"答案是不一定。尽管在 Vera greeted a scientist 和 Vera became a scientist 中，a scientist 都是限定词短语，但它在第一句中是指称性的，在第二句中则是谓语性的；因此只有第一句能变为被动句 A scientist was greeted by Vera，不合语法的 *A scientist was become by Vera 则不行。此外，before 和 during 在语义上都与指向事件的表达式相容，before 甚至可以带完整小句，during 却不行，所以 *Alan sneezed during he met with his student 不合语法。句法对句子如何组成固然重要，语义也必须单独加以考察。"}]},"chapter-076-slug-7-3-what-does-this-sentence-mean-entailments-vs-implicatures":{"title":{"en":"7.3 What does this sentence \"mean\"? Entailments vs. implicatures","zh":"7.3 这个句子“意味着”什么？蕴涵与会话含义"},"summary":{"en":"Sentence meaning can be approached through what follows necessarily from a sentence versus what is merely suggested. Entailments are guaranteed by the truth of the sentence and yield a contradiction when negated, while implicatures are context-based suggestions that can be cancelled. Presuppositions are a special kind of entailment that a sentence takes for granted and that survives negation and questioning.","zh":"要考察句子意义，需要区分必然推出的结论与仅仅被暗示的结论。蕴涵由句子为真所保证，否定后会形成矛盾；会话含义依赖语境，只是一种可能被取消的暗示。预设是一类特殊的蕴涵，是句子预先视为理所当然的内容，即使句子被否定或变成疑问句依然保留。"},"points":[{"en":"Entailment is a necessary meaning relation: if the sentence is true, the entailed sentence must be true too.","zh":"蕴涵是一种必然的意义关系：若原句为真，被蕴涵的句子也必定为真。"},{"en":"Negating an entailment and conjoining it with the original sentence produces a contradiction, a sentence that is never true.","zh":"把蕴涵句加以否定并与原句用“但是”连接，会得到矛盾句，即永远不可能为真的句子。"},{"en":"An implicature is a possible but not guaranteed conclusion, so it can be cancelled without producing a contradiction.","zh":"会话含义是可能但不受保证的结论，因此可以被取消而不会造成矛盾。"},{"en":"A presupposition is an entailment a sentence takes for granted, so it cannot be the target of negation or questioning.","zh":"预设是句子视为理所当然的蕴涵，因此不能成为主句否定或提问的对象。"},{"en":"Whether a piece of meaning survives negation and question tests diagnoses whether it is a presupposition.","zh":"某一意义成分能否在否定测试和疑问测试中保留下来，是判断它是否为预设的有效诊断手段。"}],"terms":[{"en":"entailment","zh":"蕴涵","defEn":"A meaning relation in which the truth of one sentence guarantees the truth of another.","defZh":"一种意义关系：一个句子为真必然保证另一个句子也为真。"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"A context-based suggestion of a sentence that is possible but not guaranteed to be true.","defZh":"基于语境产生的暗示，虽然可能为真，却并不被句子保证为真。"},{"en":"presupposition","zh":"预设","defEn":"An entailment that a sentence takes for granted as already true before it is uttered.","defZh":"句子在说出之前就已视为理所当然为真的那部分蕴涵内容。"},{"en":"contradiction","zh":"矛盾","defEn":"A sentence that can never be true, often used as a test for entailment.","defZh":"永远不可能为真的句子，常被用作检验蕴涵的手段。"}],"examples":[{"text":"The customer sighed, but it is not the case that the customer emitted a breath.","noteEn":"A contradiction, which shows that emitting a breath is an entailment of sighing.","noteZh":"这是矛盾句，说明“呼出一口气”是“叹气”的蕴涵。"},{"text":"The customer sighed, but it is not the case that the customer is angry.","noteEn":"Not a contradiction, so being angry is only an implicature of sighing.","noteZh":"这并非矛盾句，可见“生气”只是叹气带来的会话含义。"},{"text":"Did the customer sigh?","noteEn":"Under the question test the entailment about emitting a breath no longer holds.","noteZh":"在疑问测试下，关于呼出一口气的蕴涵不再成立。"}],"body":[{"en":"This section asks how word meanings combine into sentence meaning, and it begins from the observation that examining a word in isolation, such as the verb sigh, tells us very little. It is more informative to observe how a word is or is not used inside sentences. From sentence meaning, then, we can ask what conclusion is guaranteed and what conclusion is merely suggested. When one sentence entails another, the truth of the first forces the truth of the second.","zh":"本节要问的是词义如何组合成句子意义，出发点是一个观察：孤立地考察一个词，如动词 sigh（叹气），所能提供的信息很少。更有启发的做法是观察这个词在句子中如何被使用、又如何不能被使用。由此我们可以追问，从句子意义中能必然得出什么结论，又只是被暗示了什么结论。当一个句子蕴涵另一个句子时，前者的真会强制后者为真。"},{"en":"The contradiction test makes this relation visible: negate the candidate meaning and conjoin it with the original sentence using but. The result is a contradiction that can never be true, because sighing cannot occur without emitting a breath. For other conclusions, such as the customer being angry, the same test produces no contradiction, since the sigh may have come from relief instead. Such a cancellable, context-dependent suggestion is an implicature.","zh":"矛盾测试可以让这种关系显现出来：把候选意义加以否定，再用“但是”与原来的句子连接。结果是一个永远不可能为真的矛盾句，因为人不可能在不呼气的情况下叹气。换成别的结论，例如顾客在生气，同一测试并不产生矛盾，因为叹气也可能是出于宽慰。这种可以被取消、依赖语境的暗示就是会话含义。"},{"en":"The sentence about the restaurant that stopped charging extra for guacamole also commits the speaker to the claim that it used to charge extra. That commitment is an entailment but a special one, a presupposition: the sentence takes it for granted. Even a hearer who objects that this is not true can only contest whether the practice stopped, since both parties still assume the earlier practice. A presupposition therefore remains intact under negation and questioning, while an ordinary entailment disappears.","zh":"关于餐馆停止对鳄梨酱额外收费的句子，同时也让说话者承诺了“过去曾额外收费”这一内容。这固然是蕴涵，但属于一类特殊的蕴涵，即预设：句子把它当作理所当然的前提。即便听话人反驳说“那不是真的”，他所能争辩的也只是收费是否已经停止，因为双方仍然默认过去确实收过费。所以预设能在否定和提问下保持不变，而普通的蕴涵则会随之消失。"},{"en":"In sum, sentential meaning relations fall into two main kinds, entailments and implicatures, and entailments subdivide further into regular entailments and presuppositions. The definitions use the variables p and q simply as placeholders for sentences, a device borrowed from mathematics, where a variable such as r stands for a value that changes with the case being analysed.","zh":"总之，句子的意义关系主要分为两类：蕴涵与会话含义，而蕴涵又可再分为一般蕴涵和预设。定义中所用的变项 p 和 q 只是句子的占位符号，这一做法借自数学，正如数学中用变项 r 表示随所分析对象而变的数值。"}]},"chapter-077-slug-7-4-the-mental-lexicon":{"title":{"en":"7.4 The mental lexicon","zh":"7.4 心理词库"},"summary":{"en":"Language contains both regular, predictable patterns and irregular, arbitrary ones. The mental lexicon stores the irregularities that rules cannot capture, including free morphemes, affixes, idioms, non-compositional compounds, and irregular inflected forms. Each listeme has a lexical entry specifying its phonology, its syntax, and its sense, while compositional words and phrases are built by rules rather than listed.","zh":"语言中既有规则性强、可预测的模式，也有不规则、任意的部分。心理词库存储的是规则无法涵盖的不规则信息，包括自由语素、词缀、习语、非组合性复合词以及不规则屈折形式。每个词项都有一个词条，记录其音系、句法和涵义信息；而组合性的词和短语则由规则生成，不列入词库。"},"points":[{"en":"Regularity in language equals predictability, as when the English plural -s is realised as [ɨz] after sibilants.","zh":"语言中的规则性等同于可预测性，例如英语复数 -s 在咝音之后实现为 [ɨz]。"},{"en":"The link between sound or sign and meaning is arbitrary, so it must be memorised and stored in the lexicon.","zh":"声音或手势与意义之间的联系是任意的，必须靠记忆储存，因而存入词库。"},{"en":"Non-compositional meaning cannot be predicted from subparts, so free morphemes and affixes alike are listed.","zh":"非组合性意义无法由其组成部分预测，因此自由语素和词缀同样需要列入词库。"},{"en":"Idioms vary across language communities, and non-compositional compounds like hotdog are stored as listemes.","zh":"习语因语言社群而异，像 hotdog 这样的非组合性复合词也作为词项被储存。"},{"en":"Spelling and etymology are excluded from lexical knowledge because children use words without knowing either.","zh":"拼写和词源被排除在词汇知识之外，因为儿童在不了解二者的情况下也能使用词语。"},{"en":"Irregular forms such as went and geese may have their own lexical entries because they must be memorised.","zh":"went 和 geese 这类不规则形式可能需要独立的词条，因为它们必须靠记忆掌握。"}],"terms":[{"en":"lexicon","zh":"词库","defEn":"A language user's mental storage of linguistic information that rules cannot capture.","defZh":"语言使用者对无法由规则涵盖的语言信息所做的心理储存。"},{"en":"listeme","zh":"词项","defEn":"Anything that has a lexical entry in the lexicon, including morphemes, idioms, and some compounds.","defZh":"在词库中拥有词条的单位，涵盖语素、习语以及部分复合词。"},{"en":"non-compositional meaning","zh":"非组合性意义","defEn":"Meaning that cannot be predicted from the meanings of the expression's subparts.","defZh":"无法由该表达各组成部分的意义预测出来的意义。"},{"en":"compositional meaning","zh":"组合性意义","defEn":"Meaning that is predictable from the smaller parts put together by morphosyntactic rules.","defZh":"可以由较小成分经形态句法规则组合而预测出来的意义。"},{"en":"idiom","zh":"习语","defEn":"A meaningful sequence of words whose overall meaning is not compositional and must be memorised.","defZh":"有意义的词串，但其整体意义并非组合性的，必须逐一记住。"},{"en":"lexical entry","zh":"词条","defEn":"The listing for a listeme, containing its phonological, syntactic, and semantic information.","defZh":"词项在词库中的条目，记录其音系、句法与语义信息。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Loquesh [lowkwɛʃ]","noteEn":"A made-up beverage name whose regular English plural would be predicted as [lowkwɛʃɨz].","noteZh":"虚构的饮料名称，按英语规则其复数形式可预测为 [lowkwɛʃɨz]。"},{"text":"over the moon","noteEn":"An idiom meaning 'extremely happy' in spoken North American English.","noteZh":"北美英语口语中的习语，意为“极其高兴”。"},{"text":"hotdog","noteEn":"A non-compositional compound, since a hotdog is not a dog that is hot.","noteZh":"非组合性复合词，因为 hotdog 并不是一只热的狗。"},{"text":"TRAIN GONE SORRY","noteEn":"An American Sign Language idiom roughly meaning that you missed what was said.","noteZh":"美国手语习语，大意是“你错过了刚才说的话”。"}],"body":[{"en":"Earlier chapters surveyed regular patterns in language, and regularity can be tied to predictability. In English, for instance, the plural -s /z/ surfaces as [ɨz] after sibilant consonants. The rules a language user controls are part of that user's grammar, sometimes called the mental grammar. Not everything in language behaves this way: some information is irregular, and that is what the lexicon holds.","zh":"前面几章考察了语言中的规则模式，而规则性可以与可预测性联系起来。以英语为例，复数 -s /z/ 在咝音之后实现为 [ɨz]。语言使用者所掌握的这些规则是其语法的一部分，即心理语法。但语言中并非一切都如此运作，有些信息是不规则的，这正是词库所储存的内容。"},{"en":"The connection between a stream of sounds or signs and a meaning is arbitrary, as the morpheme goose shows: nothing in the sounds singles out a large waterbird, and other languages use unrelated forms such as nika in Ojibwe or oie in French. Because such correspondences must simply be memorised, they are stored in the lexicon as non-compositional. Free morphemes like goose, luck, and go, and affixes like -s, -ly, un-, and -er, all pattern this way.","zh":"一串声音或手势与意义之间的联系是任意的，语素 goose 就是例证：这些声音中没有任何部分单独指向这种长颈大型水鸟，而其他语言使用的是毫不相干的词形，如奥吉布瓦语的 nika、法语的 oie。正因这类音义对应关系只能靠记忆，它们才作为非组合性内容存入词库。goose、luck、go 这样的自由语素，以及 -s、-ly、un-、-er 这类词缀，都属于这种情况。"},{"en":"Idioms likewise belong to the lexicon, since the meaning of over the moon cannot be assembled from over, the, and moon; in North American English it means being extremely happy. Idioms differ across communities, so piece of cake means 'easy' in North American English but not in Japanese. Non-compositional compounds like hotdog are stored for the same reason, whereas compositional compounds like dog leash are probably not.","zh":"习语同样属于词库，因为 over the moon 的意义无法由 over、the 和 moon 组装出来；在北美英语中它表示极其高兴。习语因社群而异：piece of cake 在北美英语中表示“容易”，用日语却说不出这个意思；日语使用 asameshi-mae，法语则用 les carottes sont cuites。出于同样的理由，hotdog 这类非组合性复合词被列入词库，而 dog leash 这样的组合性复合词很可能不列入。"},{"en":"Any item with a lexical entry can be called a listeme, an umbrella term covering morphemes, idioms, and some compounds. Its entry records pronunciation such as /guws/, syntactic category such as noun, and the sense of the expression. Words like skater, unluckily, and reclassify are not listed, because their meanings follow predictably from their parts. Spelling and etymology are also left out, since five-year-olds converse accurately without either. Individual lexicons differ even inside one community, so a judgement reported here may not match every speaker's idiolect.","zh":"凡是在词库中有词条的单位都可称为词项，这一统称涵盖语素、习语和部分复合词。词条记录发音（如 /guws/）、句法类别（如名词）以及该表达的涵义。skater、unluckily、reclassify 这类词并不列入词库，因为其意义可以由 skate 与 -er、un-、luck、-y、-ly、re-、class、-ify 等部分预测出来。拼写和词源也不在词汇知识之列，因为五岁儿童并不了解这两者，却已能准确地交谈。即便在同一语言社群内部，各人的词库也存在差异，因此这里报告的判断未必符合每位说话者的个人方言。"}]},"chapter-078-slug-7-5-lexical-meaning":{"title":{"en":"7.5 The nature of lexical meaning","zh":"7.5 词汇意义的本质"},"summary":{"en":"The nature of lexical meaning remains debated. One approach treats a word's sense as a definition separate from concepts, listing necessary and sufficient conditions, while another identifies the meaning with the concept itself, sometimes in a maximalist form. Prototype effects, fuzzy category boundaries, and experimental results all suggest that concepts are structured by prototypicality and that word meaning involves more than minimal conditions.","zh":"词汇意义的本质至今仍有争论。一种观点把词义视为独立于概念的“定义”，列出必要且充分的条件；另一种观点则把意义等同于概念本身，其中包括极端版本。原型效应、模糊的范畴边界以及实验结果都表明，概念按原型性组织起来，进入词义的远不只是最低限度的条件。"},"points":[{"en":"World knowledge belongs to general cognition, while lexical meaning concerns an expression's link to a group of concepts.","zh":"世界知识属于一般认知，而词汇意义关注的是语言表达与某一组概念之间的联系。"},{"en":"A definition-based approach would list only necessary and sufficient conditions, such as a deer that is adult and female for doe.","zh":"定义式观点只列出必要且充分的条件，例如 doe 指成年且雌性的鹿。"},{"en":"Category boundaries are fuzzy, as the cup and bowl task shows, so minimal conditions cannot fully account for meaning.","zh":"范畴边界是模糊的，杯子与碗的分类任务即为例证，因此最低条件无法充分解释意义。"},{"en":"Conceptual Semantics holds that only phonology and syntax reside in the lexicon, with meaning supplied by conceptual representation.","zh":"概念语义学认为词库中只存放音系和句法信息，意义由概念表征提供。"},{"en":"Prototype theory organises concepts around a central prototype, which explains why typical members come to mind first.","zh":"原型理论把概念围绕中心原型组织起来，这解释了为何典型成员最先被想起。"},{"en":"Word meaning is shaped by lived experience, as studies of combat-related words with veterans who have PTSD indicate.","zh":"词义受到亲身经历的塑造，有关创伤后应激障碍退伍军人与战斗相关词语的研究即说明了这一点。"}],"terms":[{"en":"world knowledge","zh":"世界知识","defEn":"The general cognition about the world that a person accumulates through life experience.","defZh":"个人在生活经历中积累起来的、关于世界的一般认知。"},{"en":"concept","zh":"概念","defEn":"A building block of thought; an abstract understanding of what something is.","defZh":"思维的构成单位，即对某事物为何物的抽象理解。"},{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The lexical meaning of an expression, understood as its connection to a group of concepts.","defZh":"语言表达的词汇意义，即它与某一组概念之间的联系。"},{"en":"necessary and sufficient conditions","zh":"必要且充分条件","defEn":"The minimal set of conditions that together define a category and separate it from others.","defZh":"共同界定某一范畴并将它与其他范畴区分开来的最小条件集合。"},{"en":"prototype","zh":"原型","defEn":"A very central member of a category, around which the concept is organised.","defZh":"范畴中处于中心地位的成员，整个概念围绕它组织起来。"},{"en":"Generative Lexicon Theory","zh":"生成词库理论","defEn":"An approach in which lexical meaning retrieves conceptual information such as purpose and origin.","defZh":"一种认为词汇意义会提取对象用途、来源等概念信息的理论。"}],"examples":[{"text":"I drank the tea","noteEn":"A well-formed sentence used to contrast with the odd phrasing I drank the chair.","noteZh":"合乎语法的句子，用来与 I drank the chair 这类奇怪的搭配对照。"},{"text":"I drank the chair","noteEn":"It sounds distinctly odd, showing that concepts about states of matter matter to grammar.","noteZh":"它听起来明显别扭，说明有关物质状态的概念会影响语法。"},{"text":"the purpose of x is to seat one person","noteEn":"One item on the checklist of necessary and sufficient conditions for chair.","noteZh":"chair 的必要且充分条件清单中的一条。"},{"text":"I started a meal","noteEn":"It can mean 'I started eating a meal' or 'I started preparing a meal.'","noteZh":"它既可表示“我开始吃饭”，也可表示“我开始做饭”。"}],"body":[{"en":"What lexical meaning is remains under debate, and the English word chair helps to see why. A language user knows much about chairs from experience, which is world knowledge and belongs to general cognition; the concept of chair is a subset of that knowledge. The expression chair is connected with a group of concepts, and that connection is what lexical meaning, or sense, is claimed to be.","zh":"词汇意义究竟是什么，至今仍有争论，英语词 chair 有助于看清其中的困难。语言使用者凭经验知道大量关于椅子的信息，这属于世界知识，是一般认知的一部分；而“椅子”这一概念只是这些知识的一个子集。语言表达 chair 与一组概念相连，词汇意义即所谓涵义，指的正是这种联系。"},{"en":"If lexical meaning is separate from concepts, an entry would carry a definition alongside its phonology and syntax, as in earlier work by Katz and colleagues. Under this view only necessary and sufficient conditions would count, and x is a chair would entail a short checklist: the object seats one person, has a seat, and has a back. The conditions are necessary because each excludes wrong cases, and jointly sufficient because they are the minimum needed for the distinction.","zh":"如果词汇意义与概念彼此分离，那么词条除音系和句法信息外还会带有某种“定义”，Katz 及其同事的早期研究即采取这种做法。按此观点，只有必要且充分的条件才算词义，而命题“x 是椅子”所蕴涵的内容只是一份简短清单：该物体供一人就座、有座面、有靠背。这些条件之所以必要，是因为每一条都排除错误情形；合起来之所以充分，是因为它们已是区分椅子与非椅子的最少条件。"},{"en":"Experiments suggest that category boundaries are not sharp, which limits this checklist approach. In the object-sorting task inspired by Labov, people agree readily that one item is a cup and another a bowl but hesitate over the middle ones, and their verdicts shift depending on what the object contains, such as mac and cheese. A competing theory holds that lexical meanings simply are concepts, and in its maximalist version, associated with Jackendoff's Conceptual Semantics, the meaning of chair is everything one knows about chairs, with only phonology and syntax in the lexicon.","zh":"实验表明范畴边界并不清晰，这就限制了清单式的解释。在受 Labov 启发的物体分类任务中，人们对某一个是杯子、另一个是碗容易达成一致，对中间几个却犹豫不决，判断还会随容器中所盛之物改变，例如里面是通心粉。与之竞争的理论认为词汇意义本身就是概念；其极端版本与 Jackendoff 的概念语义学相关联，主张 chair 的意义就是人们关于椅子所知道的一切，词库中本来只存放音系和句法信息。"},{"en":"Neither approach explains prototype effects. People judging cup or bowl have intuitions about a typical cup, and when asked to imagine a chair most picture a prototypical one rather than an unusual one; asked to name bird species, speakers list robin and sparrow rather than penguin. Rosch's work suggests that concepts are organised by prototypicality, so the prototype sits at the conceptual core and less central facts lie farther out. Meaning is also shaped by lived experience and carries effects beyond literal content.","zh":"两种观点本身都无法解释原型效应。判断杯子还是碗的人对典型杯子有直觉；被要求想象一把椅子时，多数人想到的是原型椅子而非罕见的椅子；被要求列举鸟的种类时，说话者也倾向于说 robin、sparrow 而不是 penguin。Rosch 的研究表明概念按原型性组织，原型处于概念核心，不太中心的属性则距离较远。词义同样受到亲身经历的塑造，它所携带的影响远超字面内容。"}]},"chapter-079-slug-7-6-events-and-thematic-roles":{"title":{"en":"7.6 Events and thematic roles","zh":"7.6 事件与题元角色"},"summary":{"en":"This section examines how verbs encode meaning about eventualities, that is, events versus states, and about the participants those eventualities involve. It reviews the thematic roles introduced in the syntax chapter, including agent, causer, theme, instrument, location, experiencer, and goal, and shows how these roles form part of a verb's lexical meaning. It then uses the sentence The chef lifted the egg with the spatula to show that these role specifications are conjoined entailments.","zh":"本节考察动词如何编码关于事件与状态的意义，以及这些事件性所涉及的参与者。文中回顾了句法章介绍的题元角色，包括施事、致事、受事、工具、处所、经历者和目标，并说明这些角色属于动词的词义信息。最后以 The chef lifted the egg with the spatula 为例，说明题元角色的规定以合取方式构成句子的蕴涵。"},"points":[{"en":"Linguists look for semantic patterns to explain why some word combinations are well-formed and others are not.","zh":"语言学家通过寻找语义模式，来解释某些词语组合为何合乎语法、另一些却不合语法。"},{"en":"The asterisk marks descriptive ill-formedness, while the pound sign marks expressions that are syntactically fine but infelicitous in context.","zh":"星号标示描述层面的不合语法，井号则标示句法合格但在特定语境中不恰当的表达式。"},{"en":"Intransitive, transitive, and ditransitive verbs differ in how many arguments they take.","zh":"不及物动词、及物动词和双及物动词的区别在于它们所带的论元数目不同。"},{"en":"Events are dynamic and can answer the question What happened, whereas states are non-dynamic and generally cannot.","zh":"事件是动态的，可以回答“发生了什么”；状态是非动态的，一般不能这样回答。"},{"en":"Thematic roles specified in a verb's lexical meaning include agent, causer, theme, instrument, location, experiencer, and goal.","zh":"动词词义中规定的题元角色包括施事、致事、受事、工具、处所、经历者和目标。"},{"en":"The meaning of lift conjoins an event variable with statements that its arguments bear particular thematic roles.","zh":"lift 的意义把一个事件变项与若干陈述合取起来，这些陈述说明其论元承担特定的题元角色。"}],"terms":[{"en":"eventuality","zh":"事件性（事件与状态的统称）","defEn":"The umbrella term covering both dynamic events and non-dynamic states.","defZh":"一个统称性术语，既包括动态的事件，也包括非动态的状态。"},{"en":"thematic role","zh":"题元角色","defEn":"The role that a participant plays in the eventuality described by a predicate.","defZh":"论元在谓语所描述的事件或状态中所扮演的角色，如施事、受事、工具等。"},{"en":"agent","zh":"施事","defEn":"An animate actor who does things on purpose.","defZh":"有生命的施动者，即有意识地施行某一动作的参与者。"},{"en":"causer","zh":"致事","defEn":"A usually inanimate participant that causes things to happen without acting on purpose.","defZh":"通常是无生命的参与者，它使某事发生，但并非有意识地施为。"},{"en":"theme","zh":"受事","defEn":"The participant affected by the event, which may be changed by it.","defZh":"受事件影响的参与者，它可能因该事件而发生改变或被移动。"},{"en":"event semantics","zh":"事件语义学","defEn":"A semantic representation that makes reference to events and their participants.","defZh":"一种语义表征方式，它在意义描写中明确提及事件本身及其参与者。"}],"examples":[{"text":"(1) *Karen made scarf the.","noteEn":"Syntactically ill-formed because the determiner must precede the noun phrase in English.","noteZh":"该句句法不合语法，因为英语中限定词必须位于名词短语之前。"},{"text":"Context: Tamara put her hands together quietly to meditate. Tamara clapped.","noteEn":"Syntactically well-formed but infelicitous, showing that clap means more than joining the hands.","noteZh":"句子句法合格但在该语境中不恰当，说明 clap 的意义不止于双手合拢。"},{"text":"(4) Dan blinked. / (5) Ida imitated Raj. / (6) Cristina gave Alan a lab report.","noteEn":"Examples of an intransitive, a transitive, and a ditransitive verb respectively.","noteZh":"三者依次为不及物动词、及物动词和双及物动词的例子。"},{"text":"(17) The chef lifted the egg with the spatula.","noteEn":"Entails a lifting event with a theme, an instrument, and an agent.","noteZh":"该句蕴涵一个抬举事件，其中包含受事、工具和施事三个角色。"}],"body":[{"en":"This section turns from general questions about meaning to concrete semantic patterns, asking why some combinations of words are well-formed while others are not. Linguists mark descriptive ill-formedness with the asterisk and use the pound sign for sentences that are syntactically fine but infelicitous in a given context. Such contrasts are data: by forming a hypothesis about a word's meaning and testing it against contexts, we can revise our hypotheses, as with the verb clap. The upcoming sections are organised by syntactic category, with verbs treated here, nouns in 7.7, and adjectives in 7.8 and 7.9.","zh":"本节从意义的一般问题转向具体的语义模式，追问为什么有些词语组合合乎语法，而另一些不合语法。语言学家用星号标示描述层面的不合语法，用井号标示句法合格但在特定语境中不恰当的句子。这些对比本身就是语料：先就词义提出假设，再用语境加以检验并修正，例如对 clap 一词的分析。以下各节按句法类别展开：本节讨论动词，7.7 讨论名词，7.8 和 7.9 讨论形容词。"},{"en":"A verb's lexical information specifies how many arguments it takes and what roles those arguments bear. Intransitive, transitive, and ditransitive verbs illustrate the first point, while the second is captured by thematic roles such as agent, causer, theme, instrument, location, experiencer, and goal. Predicates describe eventualities, a cover term for dynamic events and non-dynamic states. Events can answer the question What happened, whereas states such as Leila knew the student cannot. Subject and object, however, are structural notions, so the subject is not always the doer.","zh":"动词的词义信息既规定它带几个论元，也规定这些论元承担什么角色。不及物、及物和双及物动词说明前一点，题元角色则说明后一点，包括施事、致事、受事、工具、处所、经历者和目标。谓语描述事件性，这一统称既涵盖动态的事件，也涵盖非动态的状态。事件可以回答“发生了什么”，而 Leila knew the student 这类状态句一般不能。需要注意的是，主语和宾语都是结构概念，主语并不总是动作的施行者。"},{"en":"Thematic roles also explain entailment patterns. The chef lifted the egg with the spatula entails that there was a lifting event, that the egg was its theme, the spatula its instrument, and the chef its agent. This resembles the entailments of a sentence coordinated with and, so the lexical meaning of lift can be represented conjunctively: there is an event e, e is a lifting event, and the arguments saturate the theme, instrument, and agent positions. As each argument merges in the syntax, its meaning fills the corresponding variable, yielding the full meaning and the entailments of the sentence.","zh":"题元角色还能解释蕴涵模式。The chef lifted the egg with the spatula 蕴涵：存在一个抬举事件，鸡蛋是该事件的受事，锅铲是工具，厨师是施事。这与用 and 连接的并列句的蕴涵方式相似，因此 lift 的词义可以用合取的方式表示：存在事件 e，e 是一个抬举事件，且各论元依次填充受事、工具和施事的变量位置。随着句法合并逐步进行，各论元的意义填入相应变项，最终得到整句的意义及其蕴涵。"}]},"chapter-080-slug-7-7-countability":{"title":{"en":"7.7 Countability","zh":"7.7 可数性"},"summary":{"en":"This section examines the lexical distinction between count nouns and mass nouns. Count nouns can be pluralised, take numerals, and combine with many, while mass nouns resist pluralisation, reject numerals, and take much. It shows that countability is a lexical property that can nevertheless shift with context, and that which nouns are count or mass varies across languages such as Halkomelem, French, and Japanese, with Japanese relying on classifiers.","zh":"本节考察名词意义中可数与不可数的区分。可数名词可以复数化、与数词连用并受 many 修饰，不可数名词则不能复数化，通常受 much 修饰。文中说明可数性是词库中的词汇属性，但也会随语境发生转换；同时指出名词的可数性因语言而异，如哈尔科梅勒姆语、法语和日语，其中日语依靠量词系统来计数。"},"points":[{"en":"Count nouns can be pluralised, combine with numerals, and appear with the determiner many.","zh":"可数名词可以复数化、与数词连用，并可与限定词 many 搭配。"},{"en":"Mass nouns resist pluralisation, reject numerals, and take much rather than many.","zh":"不可数名词不能复数化，不能与数词连用，且用 much 而非 many。"},{"en":"Count nouns are bounded, since each separate entity has a perceptible boundary, whereas mass nouns are unbounded.","zh":"可数名词是有界的，每个独立实体都有可感知的边界；不可数名词则是无界的。"},{"en":"Mass nouns can be used in count ways to denote kinds or containers, and count nouns can be used in mass ways.","zh":"不可数名词可用作可数，表示种类或容器；可数名词也可用作不可数。"},{"en":"Whether a noun is count or mass varies by language, and Japanese marks counting and plurality through classifiers.","zh":"名词的可数性因语言而异，日语则通过量词来体现计数与复数含义。"},{"en":"Acceptability judgments must consider context, since a sentence's oddness may come from world knowledge rather than semantics.","zh":"作出可接受性判断时必须考虑语境，因为句子读起来怪异可能源于世界知识而非语义。"}],"terms":[{"en":"count noun","zh":"可数名词","defEn":"A noun that can be pluralised, take numerals, and combine with many.","defZh":"可以复数化、能与数词连用，并可与限定词 many 搭配的名词。"},{"en":"mass noun","zh":"不可数名词（物质名词）","defEn":"A noun that resists pluralisation, rejects numerals, and takes much.","defZh":"不能复数化、不能与数词连用，并通常受 much 修饰的名词，如 dirt、rice。"},{"en":"bounded","zh":"有界","defEn":"Describing entities with perceptible boundaries, as with countable objects.","defZh":"指实体具有可感知的边界，可数名词所表示的对象即属此类。"},{"en":"unbounded","zh":"无界","defEn":"Describing homogeneous substances that lack perceptible boundaries, as with mass nouns.","defZh":"指同质物质缺乏可感知的边界，不可数名词所表示的对象即属此类。"},{"en":"classifier","zh":"量词","defEn":"A morpheme that attaches to numerals and turns mass nouns into bounded units.","defZh":"附着于数词之上的语素，使不可数名词成为可以计数的有界单位。"},{"en":"kind interpretation","zh":"种类解读","defEn":"The reading in which a pluralised mass noun denotes kinds of a substance.","defZh":"复数化的不可数名词表示某物质的若干种类的解读方式。"}],"examples":[{"text":"(4b) That is a lot of dirt.","noteEn":"The grammatical mass counterpart of the ill-formed a lot of dirts.","noteZh":"与不合语法的 a lot of dirts 相对的、合乎语法的不可数用法。"},{"text":"(7b) Can we have two waters? (count, 'two glasses of water')","noteEn":"A mass noun used countably to count containers rather than substances.","noteZh":"不可数名词用作可数的例子，计数的对象是容器而不是物质本身。"},{"text":"(10) The pumpkin truck crashed on the highway and there was so much pumpkin everywhere. (mass)","noteEn":"A basically count noun used in a mass way once the pumpkins are smashed.","noteZh":"本为可数名词的 pumpkin 在南瓜被压碎后用作不可数，指散落的物质。"},{"text":"(14a) kami 3-mai kudasai 'Please give me 3 pieces of paper' (Classifier: thin sheets)","noteEn":"Shows the Japanese classifier -mai used for thin, flat objects.","noteZh":"说明日语量词 -mai 用于纸张等薄而扁平的物体。"}],"body":[{"en":"Nominal meaning has a fundamental property: whether the entity denoted is countable. Descriptively, count nouns in English can be pluralised, can follow numerals, and can be modified by many, as in these shirts, three chairs, and many cups. Mass nouns such as dirt and rice behave differently: they resist pluralisation, reject numerals, and combine with much instead of many. Conceptually, count nouns are bounded, since adding another cup yields two distinct entities with perceptible boundaries, while mass nouns are unbounded, since adding dirt to a pile leaves one larger pile.","zh":"名词意义中有一个基本属性：所表示的实体是否可数。从描述上看，英语的可数名词可以复数化、可以跟数词连用，也可以受 many 修饰，如 these shirts、three chairs 和 many cups。dirt、rice 这类不可数名词则不同：它们不能复数化，不能与数词连用，并与 much 而非 many 搭配。从概念上看，可数名词是有界的，再放一个杯子就得到两个边界可感知的独立实体；不可数名词是无界的，往一堆土上再加土，仍然只是一堆更大的土。"},{"en":"The distinction is lexical, but it is not rigid. Pluralised mass nouns can receive a kind interpretation, as when three muds means three kinds of mud, or they can count containers, as when two waters means two glasses of water. Conversely, basically count nouns like pumpkin can be used in a mass way once the pumpkins are smashed across a highway. Countability also varies across languages: Halkomelem readily pluralises fog, French cheveu means a strand of hair while cheveux means a mass of hair, and Japanese has no productive plural marking, interpreting koppu as either bring cups or bring a cup.","zh":"这一区分属于词库信息，但并非一成不变。复数化的不可数名词可以获得种类解读，如 three muds 表示三种泥；也可以用来计量容器，如 two waters 表示两杯水。反过来，本属可数名词的 pumpkin 在南瓜被撞碎、撒满公路之后也可以用作不可数。可数性还因语言而异：哈尔科梅勒姆语中 fog 可以自由复数化，法语的 cheveu 指一根头发，而复数形式的 cheveux 指一头头发，日语则没有能产的复数标记，koppu 既可理解为 bring cups，也可理解为 bring a cup。"},{"en":"Japanese counts things through a rich classifier system, since expressions like 2 enpitsu are ungrammatical. Classifiers attach to numerals and divide nouns into semantically based classes: -mai for thin flat objects such as paper, -hon for long thin cylindrical objects such as pencils, -dai for mechanical objects such as cars, -hiki for small quadripedal mammals, -wa for animals with wings, and the elsewhere classifiers -tsu and -ko. Finally, context matters for acceptability judgments: I swam in vanilla extract sounds odd out of the blue, but is perfectly natural in a factory context, and the same emoji can be felicitous or infelicitous depending on the situation.","zh":"日语依靠丰富的量词系统来计数，因为像 2 enpitsu 这样的说法不合语法。量词附着在数词上，把名词按语义划分成若干类别：-mai 用于纸张等薄而扁平的物体，-hon 用于铅笔等细长的柱状物体，-dai 用于汽车等有可感知部件的机械制品，-hiki 用于较小的四足哺乳动物，-wa 用于有翼动物，-tsu 和 -ko 则是通用量词。最后，语境对可接受性判断十分重要：I swam in vanilla extract 乍看怪异，但在香草精工厂的语境中十分自然，同一个表情符号在不同情境中也可能恰当或不恰当。"}]},"chapter-081-slug-7-8-individual-vs-stage-level-predicates":{"title":{"en":"7.8 Individual- vs. stage-level predicates","zh":"7.8 个体层面谓语与阶段层面谓语"},"summary":{"en":"This short section distinguishes two kinds of adjectival state. Individual-level predicates such as tall, Canadian, and talented hold of an individual persistently and are unbounded, while stage-level predicates such as excited, bored, and sick hold only during a certain period and are bounded. Spanish marks the contrast overtly with the copulas ser and estar, and the same notion of boundedness links this distinction to count and mass nouns.","zh":"本节区分形容词所表示的两类状态。tall、Canadian、talented 等个体层面谓语持久地适用于个体，是无界的；excited、bored、sick 等阶段层面谓语只在某一特定时期成立，是有界的。西班牙语用系词 ser 和 estar 明确标示这一对立，而这一区分与可数、不可数名词共享有界性这一概念。"},"points":[{"en":"Individual-level predicates such as tall, Canadian, and talented hold of individuals persistently and are unbounded.","zh":"tall、Canadian、talented 等个体层面谓语持久地适用于个体，属于无界谓语。"},{"en":"Stage-level predicates such as excited, bored, and sick are true only during a certain period and are bounded.","zh":"excited、bored、sick 等阶段层面谓语只在特定时期成立，属于有界谓语。"},{"en":"English uses be for all adjectives, but Spanish selects ser or estar depending on the predicate type.","zh":"英语中各类形容词一律用 be，西班牙语则依据谓语类型选用 ser 或 estar。"},{"en":"Some adjectives are ambivalent, receiving individual-level or stage-level readings depending on context.","zh":"有些形容词属于两可谓语，可依语境获得个体层面或阶段层面的解读。"}],"terms":[{"en":"individual-level predicate","zh":"个体层面谓语","defEn":"A predicate that is true of an individual as a whole and persists over time.","defZh":"整体地适用于个体、并持久成立而不受时间限制的谓语。"},{"en":"stage-level predicate","zh":"阶段层面谓语","defEn":"A predicate true of an individual only during a certain period or stage.","defZh":"只在某一特定时期或阶段适用于个体的谓语，如表示暂时状态的形容词。"},{"en":"copula","zh":"系词","defEn":"The verb be in English, or ser and estar in Spanish, used with predicative adjectives.","defZh":"与谓语性形容词连用的动词，如英语的 be 以及西班牙语的 ser 和 estar。"},{"en":"ambivalent predicate","zh":"两可谓语","defEn":"An adjective that can take either an individual-level or a stage-level reading.","defZh":"既可以作个体层面解读、也可以作阶段层面解读的形容词。"},{"en":"boundedness","zh":"有界性","defEn":"The conceptual property of having a beginning point and an ending point.","defZh":"具有起点与终点的概念属性，可用于区分阶段层面谓语与不可数名词等。"}],"examples":[{"text":"(1a) ?When Ash is { tall / Canadian / talented }, I get worried.","noteEn":"Sounds odd because these individual-level predicates are persistent rather than temporary.","noteZh":"该句略显怪异，因为这些个体层面谓语表示持久属性，而非暂时状态。"},{"text":"(1b) When Ash is { excited / bored / sick }, I get worried.","noteEn":"Felicitous because stage-level predicates may hold for a limited interval.","noteZh":"该句自然，因为阶段层面谓语可以只在有限的时间段内成立。"},{"text":"(2a) Juan { es / *está } { leal / inmoral / inteligente / odioso }.","noteEn":"These Spanish adjectives are individual-level and allow only the copula ser.","noteZh":"这些西班牙语形容词属个体层面谓语，只能与系词 ser 连用。"},{"text":"(2b) Eva { *es / está } { contenta / enferma / perpleja / sola }.","noteEn":"These Spanish adjectives are stage-level and require the copula estar.","noteZh":"这些西班牙语形容词属阶段层面谓语，只能与系词 estar 连用。"}],"body":[{"en":"One interesting property of adjectival meaning is whether the state the adjective denotes has a temporal limit. Predicates like tall, Canadian, and talented generally hold of individuals persistently, which is why putting them in a when clause sounds slightly odd, since the when clause suggests a temporary eventuality. Such adjectives are individual-level predicates, true of individuals as a whole, and they invite the inference that the property will continue to hold unless otherwise specified. By contrast, excited, bored, and sick are stage-level predicates, true of an individual only during a certain period or at certain stages, so when clauses are felicitous with them and no inference of indefinite continuation arises.","zh":"形容词意义的一个有趣特点是，它所指的状态是否有时间限制。tall、Canadian、talented 这类谓语通常持久地适用于个体，因此把它们放进 when 从句会显得有点奇怪，因为该从句暗示事件性是暂时的。这类形容词是个体层面谓语，整体地适用于个体，并引发这样的推理：除非另有说明，该属性将持续成立。相比之下，excited、bored、sick 属于阶段层面谓语，只在某一时期或某些阶段适用于个体，因此 when 从句与它们连用很自然，也不会产生属性无限延续的推理。"},{"en":"English uses the copula be for adjectives of both kinds, as in Ash is tall and Ash is bored. Some languages encode the distinction in the copula itself. In Spanish, ser is used with individual-level adjectives such as leal, inmoral, inteligente, and odioso, while estar is used with stage-level adjectives such as contenta, enferma, perpleja, and sola. Some adjectives, including alegre, inquieto, and nervioso, are ambivalent predicates: they may receive an individual-level or a stage-level interpretation depending on context, at least in Spanish.","zh":"英语中两类形容词都用系词 be，如 Ash is tall 和 Ash is bored。有些语言则用系词本身来编码这一区分。西班牙语中，leal、inmoral、inteligente、odioso 等个体层面形容词只能与 ser 连用，而 contenta、enferma、perpleja、sola 等阶段层面形容词只能与 estar 连用；在例句中，ser 的屈折形式是 es，estar 的屈折形式是 está。alegre、inquieto、nervioso 等形容词属于两可谓语，至少就西班牙语而言，它们可依语境获得个体层面或阶段层面的解读。"},{"en":"Conceptually, stage-level predicates can be thought of as bounded, with a beginning point and an ending point, while individual-level predicates are unbounded and not contained within a particular interval of time. This is the same notion of boundedness used to characterise count and mass nouns in the previous section, which suggests that lexical meaning is cross-categorically sensitive to whether something has bounds and limits.","zh":"从概念上看，阶段层面谓语可以视为有界的，有起点也有终点；个体层面谓语则是无界的，不局限于某一时间区间之内。这与上一节用来刻画可数与不可数名词的有界性概念相同：前者说的是时间上的界限，后者说的是实体上的界限。由此可见，词汇意义在不同词类中都对“是否有边界和限度”这一概念敏感，有界性因此成为跨词类的语义参数。"}]},"chapter-082-slug-7-9-degrees":{"title":{"en":"7.9 Degrees","zh":"7.9 程度"},"summary":{"en":"Adjectives like scary, warm and tall are gradable: they accept modification by very, appear in comparatives, and can combine with a measure phrase. Such adjectives carry a degree argument whose value lies on a scale. The section shows degrees made visible in Italian Sign Language, comparison without degree morphemes in Washo and Motu, and the contrast between implicit and explicit comparison.","zh":"scary、warm、tall 这类形容词是程度形容词：它们可以受 very 修饰，可以出现在比较结构中，也能与度量短语组合。这类形容词的词汇语义中含有一个程度论元，其取值沿某一量表确定。本节展示意大利手语中可见的程度、瓦肖语和莫图语不用程度语素的比较方式，并区分隐性比较与显性比较。"},"points":[{"en":"Gradable adjectives accept very, occur in comparatives, and take measure phrases when a conventional unit of measurement exists.","zh":"程度形容词可受 very 修饰，能用于比较结构，并且在存在约定度量单位时可接受度量短语。"},{"en":"A degree analysis treats tall as meaning 'x is tall to a degree d', where d is measured along an abstract scale.","zh":"程度分析把 tall 的意义处理为“x 达到程度 d 的 tall”，其中 d 沿一条抽象量表取值，量表如同量尺。"},{"en":"Every gradable adjective has an associated scale, even when the language has no conventional unit such as cm or °C.","zh":"每个程度形容词都关联一条量表，即使该语言没有 cm、°C 这类约定单位，量表也依然存在。"},{"en":"In Italian Sign Language the degree argument of tall is overt, so a pronoun can point to Pietro's degree locus.","zh":"在意大利手语中，tall 的程度论元是显性的，因此指向代词可以指代 Pietro 所达到的程度位置。"},{"en":"Non-gradable adjectives such as ceramic and wooden classify rather than measure, though context can coerce a gradable reading.","zh":"ceramic、wooden 等非程度形容词表示分类而非计量，但语境可以迫使它们获得可分级的程度解读。"},{"en":"Washo and Motu compare without degree morphemes by implicit comparison, whereas English usually compares degrees explicitly with -er.","zh":"瓦肖语和莫图语不用程度语素，而以隐性比较表达比较义；英语则通常用 -er 显性比较两个程度。"}],"terms":[{"en":"gradable adjective","zh":"程度形容词","defEn":"An adjective whose meaning includes a degree, allowing very-modification and comparative use.","defZh":"语义中含有程度成分、可受 very 修饰并能用于比较结构的形容词，如 tall、warm、scary。"},{"en":"degree","zh":"程度","defEn":"A value on a scale that measures how much of a property holds.","defZh":"量表上的一个具体取值，用来衡量某一属性在多大程度上成立。"},{"en":"scale","zh":"量表","defEn":"An abstract measuring stick along which the degrees of a gradable adjective are ordered.","defZh":"一条抽象的量尺，程度形容词的各个程度值沿其排列，如长度量表、温度量表。"},{"en":"non-gradable adjective","zh":"非程度形容词","defEn":"An adjective that resists degree modification and comparatives because it classifies rather than measures.","defZh":"不能受程度修饰、也不能用于比较结构的形容词，因为它只表示分类而非计量。"},{"en":"implicit comparison","zh":"隐性比较","defEn":"Comparison made by manipulating context so that a predicate holds of one entity but not another.","defZh":"通过调整语境使谓语只对其中一个比较对象成立，从而间接表达比较意义。"},{"en":"explicit comparison","zh":"显性比较","defEn":"Comparison that directly compares two degrees or measurements, as English -er does.","defZh":"直接比较两个具体的程度值或测量值，如英语比较级 -er 所表达的比较。"}],"examples":[{"text":"That's a very scary story.","noteEn":"Very modifies the gradable adjective scary, showing that its meaning includes a degree.","noteZh":"very 能修饰程度形容词 scary，说明 scary 的语义中含有程度与量表成分。"},{"text":"It's warmer today than yesterday.","noteEn":"A comparative construction: today's degree of warmth is higher than yesterday's.","noteZh":"这是比较结构：今天温暖所达到的程度高于昨天所达到的程度。"},{"text":"That person is 6ft tall.","noteEn":"A measure phrase specifies the exact degree of tallness on the height scale.","noteZh":"度量短语具体指明该身高在身高量表上达到的程度值。"},{"text":"t’é:liwhu delkáykayiʔ k’éʔi daʔmóʔmoʔ delkáykayiʔé:s","noteEn":"Washo expresses 'The man is taller than the woman' by saying the man is tall and the woman is not.","noteZh":"瓦肖语用“男人高、女人不高”来表达“男人比女人高”，即隐性比较。"}],"body":[{"en":"Many adjectives let speakers say how much of a property an individual has. Scary, warm and tall belong to this class, the gradable adjectives: they can be modified by very, as in That's a very scary story, they appear in comparatives such as It's warmer today than yesterday, and, where a unit of measurement exists, they combine with a measure phrase, as in That person is 6ft tall.","zh":"许多形容词允许说话人说明某一属性在多大程度上成立。scary、warm、tall 就属于这一类，称为程度形容词：它们可以受 very 修饰，如 That's a very scary story；可以出现在比较结构中，如 It's warmer today than yesterday；当存在度量单位时，还可以与度量短语组合，如 That person is 6ft tall。"},{"en":"One analysis builds a degree into the lexical semantics of these adjectives. Tall does not simply mean 'x is tall'; it means 'x is tall to a degree d', with d on a scale, an abstract measuring stick. Tallness is measured along a length scale and warmth along a temperature scale. A measure phrase pins down a degree; very says it counts as high in the context; a comparative ranks two entities' degrees on one scale. Even scary, which has no conventional unit, has a scale: we can rate how scary a film was from 1 to 10.","zh":"一种分析把程度纳入这些形容词的词汇语义。tall 并不只表示“x 高”，而表示“x 达到程度 d 的 tall”，其中 d 落在一条量表上——一条抽象的量尺。高矮沿长度量表衡量，冷暖沿温度量表衡量。度量短语确定一个程度值；very 表示该程度在语境中被视为高；比较结构把两个实体放在同一量表上并排出它们的程度高低。即使 scary 没有约定单位，它同样有量表，因为我们可以按 1 到 10 分评价一部电影有多吓人。"},{"en":"Across languages, degrees are not always hidden. In Italian Sign Language certain adjectives make them visible: TALL introduces a locus s for the standard of tallness and a locus d for Pietro's degree, and a pointing pronoun can refer back to d, so the degree argument surfaces overtly. Washo and Motu have no morphemes for -er or more; they compare implicitly, saying that the man is tall but the woman is not, or that Mary is tall and Frank short. English can compare degrees explicitly, but also implicitly, as in Compared to Anne-Michelle, Marcin is tall.","zh":"在不同语言中，程度并不总是隐而不显。在意大利手语里，某些形容词让程度变得可见：TALL 这一手势引入两个位置，s 表示算得上高的最低程度（即 tall 的标准），d 表示 Pietro 所达到的程度；指别代词可以回指 d，于是程度论元以显性成分出现。瓦肖语和莫图语没有与 -er 或 more 对应的语素，它们采用隐性比较：说男人高而女人不高，或说 Mary 高而 Frank 矮。英语既能显性比较程度，也能使用隐性比较，如 Compared to Anne-Michelle, Marcin is tall。"},{"en":"Not every adjective has a degree. Ceramic and wooden resist very and comparatives, because they classify things into categories: something is either wooden or not. Yet speakers readily supply scales: That mosquito is very alive can be rescued by measuring visible vigour. Whether an adjective carries a degree argument is a lexical specification that varies across languages.","zh":"并非所有形容词都有程度。ceramic 和 wooden 不能受 very 修饰，也不能用于比较结构，因为它们把事物分入类别：要么是木制的，要么不是。不过语言使用者很善于临时补充量表，That mosquito is very alive 可以借助看起来多有活力而变得可接受。形容词是否带有程度论元，属于词汇层面的规定，并且会因语言而异。"}]},"chapter-083-slug-7-10-why-not-the-dictionary":{"title":{"en":"7.10 Why not the dictionary?","zh":"7.10 为什么不能依赖词典？"},"summary":{"en":"Dictionaries are often treated as the ultimate authority on language, yet they are written by fallible humans and may even contain invented entries such as Dord and mountweazels. Many genuine words, including tubist and beigeness, are left out for reasons of efficiency, and definitions capture only part of a word's meaning. Language users, not lexicographers, create meaning.","zh":"人们常把词典视为语言的最高权威，但词典由会犯错的人编写，甚至含有 Dord、mountweazel 这类虚构词条。出于效率考虑，tubist、beigeness 等真实存在的词也可能未被收录，而词典释义只呈现词义的一部分。创造意义的是语言使用者，词典编纂者只是记录了其中一部分用法，因此词典永远无法成为语言的完整记录。"},"points":[{"en":"Lexicographers are human and make errors: the meaningless entry Dord entered Merriam-Webster because of a misread note.","zh":"词典编纂者也是人，会出错：Merriam-Webster 因误读一条批注而收入了毫无意义的词条 Dord。"},{"en":"Some entries are deliberately invented, called mountweazels, in order to catch publishers who copy a dictionary.","zh":"有些词条是为识别抄袭者而故意编造的，称为 mountweazel，即防抄袭的假词条。"},{"en":"Because lexicographers choose what to include, productive derived words like tubist or unenthusiasticness may be absent for efficiency.","zh":"由于收录范围由编纂者决定，出于效率考虑，tubist、unenthusiasticness 这类能产的派生词可能不被收录。"},{"en":"Definitions are partial: using museum for 'person who works at a museum' is descriptively valid but is not listed.","zh":"释义只是部分的：用 museum 指在博物馆工作的人在描述上完全成立，却不见于词典的义项。"},{"en":"What makes a word real is use in the relevant community, not presence in a dictionary; Johnson's dictionary pursued prescriptive aims.","zh":"一个词是否真实，取决于相关语言社群是否实际使用，而非是否见于词典；约翰逊的词典带有规定语言的意图。"},{"en":"Sentential minimal pairs such as Japanese eda-o otta and #sara-o otta let linguists test hypotheses about lexical meaning.","zh":"日语 eda-o otta 与 #sara-o otta 这类句子层面的最小对立对，可用来检验关于词汇意义的假设。"}],"terms":[{"en":"lexicographer","zh":"词典编纂者","defEn":"A person who writes or compiles the entries of a dictionary.","defZh":"编写和编纂词典词条的人，其工作受到人类知识与编纂效率的限制。"},{"en":"mountweazel","zh":"蒙威泽尔词条（mountweazel）","defEn":"A fictitious entry placed in a dictionary or reference work to detect plagiarism.","defZh":"词典或工具书中故意编造的假词条，用来发现未经许可的抄袭行为。"},{"en":"derivational morpheme","zh":"派生语素","defEn":"An affix that builds a new word, often with a predictable meaning, like -ist or -ness.","defZh":"能够构成新词、往往还会改变词类的词缀，其意义通常可以预测，如 -ist、-ness。"},{"en":"minimal pair","zh":"最小对立对","defEn":"A pair of expressions that are identical except for exactly one parameter.","defZh":"除一个参数外其余部分完全相同的两个表达式，用以检验该差异造成的影响。"},{"en":"prescriptive","zh":"规定性的","defEn":"Concerned with prescribing how language should be used rather than describing how it is used.","defZh":"主张规定语言应当如何使用的立场，与描写实际用法的取向相对。"},{"en":"descriptive","zh":"描述性的","defEn":"Based on how people actually use language rather than on rules about how they ought to.","defZh":"以语言使用者的实际用法为依据，而不是依据应当如何使用的语言规范。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Dord","noteEn":"A ghost entry in Merriam-Webster that was wrongly taken to mean 'density'.","noteZh":"Merriam-Webster 中因误读批注而出现的假词条，曾被当成表示密度的词。"},{"text":"Purpleness is, but not beigeness","noteEn":"Shows that -ness nouns are formed freely, yet dictionaries include only some of them.","noteZh":"说明 -ness 名词可以自由构成，但词典只收录其中的一部分形式。"},{"text":"I visited a museum in Toronto today.","noteEn":"Here museum has its ordinary sense of a place where art and other things are exhibited.","noteZh":"此处 museum 用的是通常意义，指展出艺术品等物品的场所。"},{"text":"#sara-o otta","noteEn":"Japanese sentence meaning 'She broke a plate'; compared with eda-o otta, it shows that otta restricts its object.","noteZh":"意为“她打破了盘子”的日语句子；与 eda-o otta 对比可见 otta 对宾语有所限制。"}],"body":[{"en":"Many people treat the dictionary as the ultimate authority on language, insisting that something is not a word unless it appears there. That reasoning is flawed, because lexicographers are human and humans err. In the early 1930s a consultant wrote 'D or d' beside the entry for density in Merriam-Webster, and a lexicographer printed Dord as a word meaning 'density'; the error survived until 1947. Dictionaries may also contain deliberate fictions called mountweazels, such as the 1975 entry on the imaginary photographer Lillian Virginia Mountweazel.","zh":"有一种流行的误解，认为词典是语言的最高权威，人们有时还会以词典里没有为由断言某个说法不算词。这种推理并不成立。词典编纂者也是人，而人会犯错。20 世纪 30 年代初，一位顾问在 Merriam-Webster 的 density 词条旁写下 D or d，某位编纂者却误把 Dord 当作表示密度的词印入词典，这个错误直到 1947 年才改正。词典中还可能刻意安置假词条，即 mountweazel：1975 年就收录了虚构摄影师 Lillian Virginia Mountweazel 的条目，用来发现抄袭者。"},{"en":"Dictionaries also omit genuine words. The suffix -ist attaches productively to instrument nouns, giving guitarist, violinist and oboist, yet tubist has no entry in the Oxford English Dictionary, even though the National Academy Orchestra of Canada used the word in 2021. Similarly, unenthusiastic and purpleness are listed, but unenthusiasticness and beigeness are not. Listing every derived form would be uneconomical, so omission reflects efficiency, not invalidity; different dictionaries also serve different purposes.","zh":"词典也不可能收录所有词。-ist 这类能产后缀可以自由附着在乐器名词后表示演奏者，如 guitarist、violinist、oboist。然而 tubist 在 Oxford English Dictionary 中并没有词条，尽管加拿大国家管弦乐团 2021 年曾使用这个词。同样，unenthusiastic 被收录，unenthusiasticness 却没有；purpleness 被收录，beigeness 却没有。把所有派生形式都列出并不经济，因此未收录通常出于效率考虑，而非否定这个词；何况各类词典用途不同。"},{"en":"Even a listed word is not fully defined. In The museum said they were closing indefinitely, museum does not denote the building but roughly 'person who works at the museum', a meaning absent from the Oxford English Dictionary. The use is nonetheless descriptively valid, because people do use it that way. A word can carry many context-dependent meanings that no lexicographer can record. A dictionary is a reference work and a partial record of usage, giving a general picture and enough information to guess how a word might be used.","zh":"即使是已收录的词，释义也不完整。在 The museum said they were closing indefinitely 中，museum 并不指建筑物，而大致相当于在博物馆工作的人，这一意义并未见于 Oxford English Dictionary。但这种用法在描述上是成立的，因为人们确实这样使用。一个词可以有许多依赖语境的意义，任何编纂者都无法全部记录下来。词典是一种参考工具，是对语言用法的部分记录：它给出大致图景，以及足以推测用法的信息。"},{"en":"Dictionaries were never meant to record everything. James Murray, an editor of the first Oxford English Dictionary, noted that the vocabulary of a living language is not a fixed quantity circumscribed by definite limits. Because language keeps changing, the number of real words is never finite, and lexicographers record only part of the vocabulary. Linguists therefore observe use, testing hypotheses with sentential minimal pairs such as the Japanese eda-o otta and #sara-o otta.","zh":"词典从来就不打算记录一切。第一版 Oxford English Dictionary 的编者詹姆斯·默里指出，一种广泛使用、高度发达的活语言的词汇并不是被确定界限所限定的固定量。语言始终在变化，因此真实词的数量永远不是有限的，编纂者只记录了词汇的一部分。要探究词义，语言学家转而观察实际用法，并用句子层面的最小对立对检验假设，例如日语的 eda-o otta 与 #sara-o otta。"}]},"chapter-084-slug-7-11-denotation":{"title":{"en":"7.11 Denotation","zh":"7.11 外延"},"summary":{"en":"This section contrasts sense, the semantic information stored in the mental lexicon, with denotation, what a linguistic expression points to. One-place predicates denote sets, proper names denote unique individuals or references, and declarative sentences denote truth values. Since world knowledge is often lacking, sentence denotations are usually given as truth conditions, and denotation is technically relativised to our cognitive representation of the world.","zh":"本节对比涵义与指称：涵义是存放在心理词库中的语义信息，指称则是语言表达式所指向的对象。一元谓语指称集合，专有名词指称唯一的个体即其指称对象，陈述句则指称真值。由于我们往往缺乏相关世界知识，句子的外延通常以真值条件给出；严格说来，指称是相对于我们对世界的认知表征而言的。"},"points":[{"en":"Sense is the semantic information stored in the mental lexicon, while denotation is what an expression points to.","zh":"涵义是存放在心理词库中的语义信息，指称则是表达式所指向的对象。"},{"en":"One-place predicates, including some nouns, adjectives and intransitive verbs, denote sets of things in the world.","zh":"一元谓语（包括部分名词、形容词和不及物动词）指称现实世界中事物所构成的集合。"},{"en":"Proper names denote a unique individual, called the expression's reference, and are written as bolded constants such as n.","zh":"专有名词指称唯一的个体，即该表达式的指称对象，通常写作加粗的常项如 n。"},{"en":"Constants have fixed values while variables are placeholders; early alphabet letters conventionally serve as constants.","zh":"常项的值固定不变，变项则是可以变化的占位符号；习惯上字母表靠前的字母用作常项。"},{"en":"Only sentences have truth values, written T or F, as the test It is true/false that shows.","zh":"只有句子才具有真值，写作 T 或 F，这可由 It is true/false that 这一句式加以检验，名词和短语则不行。"},{"en":"A sentence's denotation is its truth value, computed as truth condition plus world knowledge; otherwise we state the truth condition.","zh":"句子的外延就是其真值，由真值条件加上世界知识得出；若缺乏世界知识，则只写出真值条件。"}],"terms":[{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The semantic information a word expresses and that speakers store in the mental lexicon.","defZh":"词语所表达的语义信息，也是说话人存放在心理词库中的内容。"},{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延（指称）","defEn":"What a linguistic expression points to in the actual world, or in our cognitive representation of it.","defZh":"语言表达式在现实世界（严格说是我们对现实世界的认知表征）中所指向的对象。"},{"en":"reference","zh":"指称对象","defEn":"The unique individual that an expression such as a proper name denotes.","defZh":"专有名词等表达式所指称的那个唯一确定的个体对象，又称该表达式的指称。"},{"en":"truth value","zh":"真值","defEn":"The abstract value true or false that a declarative sentence has.","defZh":"陈述句所具有的抽象取值，即要么为真、要么为假，通常分别写作 T 与 F。"},{"en":"truth condition","zh":"真值条件","defEn":"The circumstances under which a sentence would receive a particular truth value.","defZh":"使一个句子获得某一特定真值（真或假）所需要满足的具体情形或条件。"},{"en":"constant","zh":"常项","defEn":"A symbol with a fixed value, such as bolded n for the individual Nathan Sanders.","defZh":"取值固定不变、始终代表同一个体的符号，例如用加粗的 n 表示语言学家 Nathan Sanders。"}],"examples":[{"text":"The building to the north of the bank","noteEn":"A phrase with its own sense that nonetheless denotes the post office in the town.","noteZh":"该短语有自身的涵义，却与另一短语指称同一座邮局。"},{"text":"My house","noteEn":"Same sense for every speaker, but its denotation depends on who utters it.","noteZh":"无论由谁说出，其涵义相同，但指称对象随说话人而改变。"},{"text":"⟦sour⟧ = the set of all sour things in the actual world","noteEn":"One-place predicates denote sets of things rather than a single individual.","noteZh":"一元谓语的外延是事物构成的集合，而不是某一个个别的对象。"},{"text":"⟦My neighbour’s cat’s liver weighs 326 grams⟧ = T if my neighbour’s cat’s liver weighs 326 grams in the actual world, F otherwise","noteEn":"When world knowledge is unavailable, a sentence's denotation is stated as its truth condition.","noteZh":"当缺乏相关世界知识时，句子的外延就以真值条件的形式给出。"}],"body":[{"en":"Earlier in the chapter, meaning was analysed as sense: the semantic information a word expresses, stored in the mental lexicon. Sense is hard to pin down. It is probably not a list of necessary and sufficient conditions, it seems tied to concepts, and predicates specify how many arguments they take and what roles those arguments carry. Nouns specify countability, adjectives specify whether they are stage-level or individual-level, and some adjectives carry a degree argument. Lexical semanticists therefore ask general questions about semantic classes and lexical organisation.","zh":"本章前面主要从涵义的角度分析意义，即词语所表达、存放于心理词库中的信息。涵义很难精确刻画：它很可能不是一组充分必要条件，而与概念相关联；谓语还规定自己带几个论元、这些论元充当什么角色。名词规定可数性，形容词规定属于阶段层面还是个体层面，有些形容词还带有程度论元。因此词汇语义学家更关心语义类别与词条组织这类一般问题，而不是孤立地确定某一个词的意义。"},{"en":"Denotation offers another angle. The denotation of an expression is what it points to in the actual world: sour points to all sour things, such as lemons, limes, grapefruit, vinegar and pickles, and pencil points to the set of all pencils. Strictly speaking, it points to our cognitive representation of the actual world, because unicorn and Mario denote nothing in reality yet are clearly meaningful.","zh":"指称提供了另一个分析角度。表达式的外延就是它在现实世界中所指向的对象：sour 指向所有酸的东西，如 lemons、limes、grapefruit、vinegar、pickles；pencil 指向所有铅笔构成的集合。严格说来，指称是表达式在我们的认知表征中所指向的对象，因为 unicorn、Mario 在现实中并不存在，却显然有意义。"},{"en":"Sense and denotation are related: the sense of a word is what you use to decide what it points to. A map of a town makes the difference clear. The building to the north of the bank and the building to the east of the library contain different words and therefore different senses, yet both denote the same post office. My house, by contrast, has one sense for every speaker but its denotation changes with the speaker.","zh":"涵义与指称相互关联：正是借助一个词的涵义，我们才能判断它指向什么。用一张城镇地图可以清楚看出二者的差别。The building to the north of the bank 与 The building to the east of the library 用词不同，涵义因而不同，但二者指称同一座邮局。相反，My house 对每个说话人来说涵义相同，其指称却随说话人而改变。"},{"en":"For one-place predicates, denotation is a set: ⟦pencil⟧ is the set of all pencils and ⟦snore⟧ the set of all things that snore. A proper name denotes a particular individual, abbreviated by a bolded constant such as n; unlike variables, constants cannot vary. Only sentences have truth values, T or F, as the construction It is true/false that shows. The denotation of Nathan Sanders is bald is therefore T, or a truth condition if we lack the relevant world knowledge.","zh":"对一元谓语而言，外延是一个集合：⟦pencil⟧ 是所有铅笔的集合，⟦snore⟧ 是所有打呼噜之物的集合。专有名词指称某个特定个体，可缩写为加粗常项如 n；与变项不同，常项的值不能改变。只有句子才有真值 T 或 F，这可由 It is true/false that 这一结构检验。因此 Nathan Sanders is bald 的外延是 T；若缺少相关世界知识，则改以真值条件的形式表达。"}]},"chapter-085-slug-7-12-set-theory":{"title":{"en":"7.12 Introduction to set theory","zh":"7.12 集合论简介"},"summary":{"en":"Denotation can be modelled with sets: a common noun denotes the set of things it applies to, a name denotes an individual, and a sentence denotes a truth value. Membership, subset, proper subset, superset, cardinality, intersection, and union provide notation for these relations. Set theory also explains quantificational determiners such as two and every, whose meaning depends on how the sets in a sentence overlap or contain one another.","zh":"指称可以用集合来刻画：普通名词指称它所适用的所有事物构成的集合，专有名词指称某个个体，句子则指称真值。成员、子集、真子集、超集、基数、交集与并集等概念为这些关系提供了形式化记号。集合论还能解释 two、every 等量化限定词的意义，因为它们的理解依赖于句中各集合之间的包含与重叠关系。"},"points":[{"en":"The denotation of cat is the set of all cats in the actual world; members of a set are its elements.","zh":"cat 的指称是现实世界中所有猫构成的集合；集合中的事物称为该集合的成员或元素。"},{"en":"A name denotes the individual it refers to, so Panks denotes that particular cat in the actual world.","zh":"专有名词指称它所指向的个体，因此 Panks 指称现实世界中的那只猫。"},{"en":"A sentence denotes a truth value: Panks is a cat is true just in case Panks is in the cat set.","zh":"句子指称真值：Panks is a cat 为真，当且仅当 Panks 属于猫的集合。"},{"en":"A is a subset of B when all members of A are also members of B.","zh":"若 A 的所有成员都是 B 的成员，则 A 是 B 的子集，记为 A⊆B。"},{"en":"Quantificational determiners like two and every are understood through intersection and subset relations among sets.","zh":"two 与 every 等量化限定词的意义，可以通过集合之间的交集与子集关系来理解。"}],"terms":[{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延/指称","defEn":"The set of things in the actual world that a linguistic expression applies to.","defZh":"语言表达式在现实世界中所指称的对象集合，与心理词库中储存的涵义相对。"},{"en":"set","zh":"集合","defEn":"A collection of objects, conventionally named with a capital letter.","defZh":"由若干对象组成的整体，在集合论中通常用大写字母表示，如集合 A。"},{"en":"member","zh":"成员","defEn":"An object that belongs to a set; also called an element.","defZh":"属于某个集合的对象，也称元素；b ∈ A 读作 b 是 A 的成员。"},{"en":"subset","zh":"子集","defEn":"A set whose members are all contained in another set.","defZh":"其所有成员都包含在另一个集合之中的集合，符号为 ⊆。"},{"en":"proper subset","zh":"真子集","defEn":"A subset that leaves out at least one member of the larger set.","defZh":"真子集要求较大集合中至少有一个成员不属于该子集，符号为 ⊂。"},{"en":"intersection","zh":"交集","defEn":"The set of objects that are members of both A and B.","defZh":"同时属于 A 和 B 的所有对象构成的集合，写作 A∩B。"}],"examples":[{"text":"⟦cat⟧ = the set of all cats in the actual world","noteEn":"Double brackets express denotation; here cat denotes the whole set of cats.","noteZh":"双括号表示指称，此处说明 cat 指称现实世界中所有猫构成的集合。"},{"text":"Panks is a cat","noteEn":"True because the individual Panks is a member of the set of cats.","noteZh":"该句为真，因为个体 Panks 是猫集合的成员。"},{"text":"Karti is a cat","noteEn":"Denotes F, since the dog Karti is not in the set of cats.","noteZh":"该句指称假，因为狗 Karti 不属于猫的集合。"},{"text":"Every cat is grey","noteEn":"Means that everything in the cat set is also in the grey set.","noteZh":"意为猫集合中的所有事物也都属于灰色事物的集合。"}],"body":[{"en":"Set theory provides a precise way of talking about denotation. The denotation of the noun cat is the set of all cats in the actual world, and anything belonging to a set is called a member, or element, of it. Double brackets mark denotation, so cat denotes that set, whereas a name such as Panks denotes the individual it refers to. Sentences denote truth values, restatable as truth conditions: Panks is a cat is true just in case the individual Panks is a member of the set of all cats, and false otherwise. Because Panks really is a cat, that sentence denotes T; the parallel sentence about the dog Karti denotes F.","zh":"集合论为讨论指称提供了精确的工具。名词 cat 的指称是现实世界中所有猫构成的集合，而属于某个集合的对象称为该集合的成员（或元素）。依照惯例，双括号表示“指称”，因此 ⟦cat⟧ 就是那个集合，而专有名词 Panks 的指称则是它所指向的那个个体。句子指称真值，这也可以表述为真值条件：Panks is a cat 为真，当且仅当个体 Panks 是猫集合的成员，否则为假。由于 Panks 确实是猫，该句指称真；而关于狗 Karti 的平行句子则指称假，因为 Karti 不在猫的集合之中。"},{"en":"Sets may contain smaller sets. Suppose Panks, Waffy, and Lilly are Siberian Forest Cats and Sugar and Brezel are not; then the set containing just Panks, Waffy, and Lilly is a subset of the set of cats. Formally, A is a subset of B when every member of A is also a member of B, written A⊆B, and B is then a superset of A, written B⊇A. A proper subset, symbolised ⊂, requires in addition that at least one member of B is not a member of A, as with the Siberian Cats and cats. The reversed symbol ⊃ marks a proper superset. Capital letters conventionally stand for sets, and related notation covers cardinality, intersection, and union.","zh":"集合之中还可以有更小的集合。假设 Panks、Waffy 与 Lilly 是西伯利亚森林猫，而 Sugar 与 Brezel 不是，那么仅含 Panks、Waffy、Lilly 的集合就是猫集合的子集。形式化地说，若 A 的所有成员都是 B 的成员，则 A 是 B 的子集，写作 A⊆B；此时也称 B 是 A 的超集，写作 B⊇A。真子集用符号 ⊂ 表示，它额外要求 B 中至少有一个成员不属于 A，例如西伯利亚猫集合之于猫集合。颠倒过来的符号 ⊃ 表示真超集。大写字母通常用来表示集合，其他记号还涉及基数、交集与并集。"},{"en":"Set theory also illuminates quantificational words, which concern quantity, such as the English determiners every, some, most, many, few, two, and five. To interpret Two cats are grey, take the set of cats C and the set of grey things G and form their intersection C∩G, the set of grey cats; two asks how many members that intersection has. Every cat is grey instead says that everything in C is also in G. For later sections, grasping subset and superset conceptually matters more than formal notation.","zh":"集合论也有助于理解与数量有关的量化词语。英语中的量化限定词包括 every、some、most、many、few、two、five 等。要理解 Two cats are grey，可以取猫的集合 C 与灰色事物的集合 G，二者的交集 C∩G 就是灰色的猫；two 追问的正是这个交集里有多少成员。而 Every cat is grey 是说 C 中的一切都也属于 G。对于后面的小节来说，从概念上把握子集与超集比掌握形式记号更为重要。"}]},"chapter-086-slug-7-13-negative-polarity":{"title":{"en":"7.13 Negative polarity items","zh":"7.13 否定极性项"},"summary":{"en":"Negative polarity items are expressions such as anything, at all, anywhere, ever, and any, which appear only in particular licensing contexts. Although negation with not or no is the clearest licenser, the verb doubt and the antecedent of a conditional also license them. What these contexts share is downward entailment: a sentence with a proper superset entails the corresponding sentence with a proper subset. Upward-entailing contexts do not license negative polarity items.","zh":"否定极性项是 anything、at all、anywhere、ever、any 这类只出现在特定允准语境中的表达式。带 not 或 no 的否定是最明显的允准语境，但 doubt 以及条件句的先行语同样可以允准它们。这些语境的共同点是向下蕴涵：含真超集的句子蕴涵含真子集的对应句子。向上蕴涵语境则不能允准否定极性项。"},"points":[{"en":"Negative polarity items such as anything and ever appear only in certain negative grammatical contexts, called licensing contexts.","zh":"anything、ever 等否定极性项只出现在特定的否定性语法语境中，这类语境称为允准语境。"},{"en":"Negation must be structurally higher than the negative polarity item in the tree; fronting the item makes the sentence ungrammatical.","zh":"否定成分在树形图中必须结构上高于否定极性项；若把该成分前置，句子便不合乎语法。"},{"en":"Licensing is not limited to literal negation: embedding under doubt and conditional antecedents also license these items.","zh":"允准并不限于字面否定：嵌入 doubt 之下以及条件句先行语之中的否定极性项同样得到允准。"},{"en":"A context is downward-entailing when the sentence with a proper superset entails the sentence with a proper subset.","zh":"若含真超集的句子蕴涵含真子集的句子，该语境就是向下蕴涵语境。"},{"en":"Upward-entailing contexts reverse the entailment pattern, so they do not license negative polarity items.","zh":"向上蕴涵语境的蕴涵方向恰好相反，即由子集句蕴涵超集句，因此不能允准否定极性项。"}],"terms":[{"en":"negative polarity item (NPI)","zh":"否定极性项","defEn":"An expression licensed only in certain negative or downward-entailing contexts.","defZh":"只在特定否定性或向下蕴涵语境中才能出现的表达式，如 anything、ever。"},{"en":"licensing context","zh":"允准语境","defEn":"The grammatical environment that permits a negative polarity item to appear.","defZh":"允许否定极性项出现的语法环境，例如带 not 的否定句或条件句先行语。"},{"en":"downward-entailing context","zh":"向下蕴涵语境","defEn":"A context in which a sentence with a proper superset entails the one with a proper subset.","defZh":"由含真超集的句子蕴涵含真子集句子的语境，例如 They did not ____。"},{"en":"upward-entailing context","zh":"向上蕴涵语境","defEn":"A context in which the sentence with a proper subset entails the one with a proper superset.","defZh":"由含真子集的句子蕴涵含真超集句子的语境，否定极性项在此不被允准。"},{"en":"entailment","zh":"蕴涵","defEn":"A necessary meaning relation: if one sentence is true, another must be true.","defZh":"一种必然的意义关系：若一个句子为真，另一个句子也必然为真。"},{"en":"conditional","zh":"条件句","defEn":"A sentence of the form if X, then Y, whose if-clause can license NPIs.","defZh":"形如“如果 X，那么 Y”的句子，其 if 小句可以是否定极性项的允准语境。"}],"examples":[{"text":"I did not pet any cats.","noteEn":"The negation not is present, so the NPI any cats is grammatical.","noteZh":"句中存在否定词 not，因此否定极性项 any cats 合乎语法。"},{"text":"* Any cats, I did not pet.","noteEn":"Fronting the NPI places it above negation, so it is ungrammatical.","noteZh":"把否定极性项前置后它位于否定之上，因此句子不合乎语法。"},{"text":"They did not pet Siberian Forest Cats.","noteEn":"This is the proper subset version used in the downward-entailing test for that context.","noteZh":"这是向下蕴涵测试中把 cats 换成真子集后的对应句子。"},{"text":"If you buy orange pumpkins, then you get free candy.","noteEn":"The conditional antecedent is downward-entailing, so an NPI is licensed there.","noteZh":"条件句先行语具有向下蕴涵性质，因此其中可以出现否定极性项 any。"}],"body":[{"en":"When meaning is treated in terms of denotation, several patterns become explainable. The expressions anything, at all, anywhere, ever, and any are negative polarity items (NPIs): expressions that appear only in certain negative grammatical contexts, their licensing contexts. The clearest case is a sentence containing the negation not or no, but merely containing negation is not sufficient. Syntactically, negation must sit in a structurally higher position than the NPI in the tree, so I did not pet any cats is well formed while the fronted Any cats, I did not pet is ungrammatical. NPIs are also licensed under the verb doubt and in the antecedent of a conditional, the if-clause.","zh":"当我们从指称的角度理解语言表达的意义时，一些有趣的语言现象就能得到解释。anything、at all、anywhere、ever、any 这类表达式称为否定极性项（NPI），它们只出现在某些“否定性”语法语境之中，这种语境称为允准语境。最直接的情形是句子中含有否定词 not 或 no，但仅仅含有否定还不够。从句法上看，否定成分在树形图中必须处于比否定极性项更高的结构位置，因此 I did not pet any cats 合乎语法，而把否定极性项前置的 Any cats, I did not pet 则不合语法。否定极性项还可以在动词 doubt 之下以及条件句的先行语（if 小句）中得到允准。"},{"en":"That characterisation is downward entailment. To test a context, first insert a phrase containing something that denotes a set, as in They did not pet cats. Second, replace that set with a proper subset of it, giving They did not pet Siberian Forest Cats. Third, check the entailment relation between the two sentences. Here the sentence with the proper superset entails the sentence with the proper subset: if they did not pet cats, then they did not pet Siberian Forest Cats, but not conversely, since they might have petted a Siamese cat. A context producing this entailment pattern is downward-entailing, and this property seems to be what licenses NPIs.","zh":"这种允准语境的性质可以用向下蕴涵来刻画。检验分为三步：第一步，在语境中填入含集合指称的短语，如 They did not pet cats；第二步，把该集合换成它的真子集，得到 They did not pet Siberian Forest Cats；第三步，检查两句之间的蕴涵关系。这里含真超集的句子蕴涵含真子集的句子：若他们没有摸过猫，就必然没有摸过西伯利亚森林猫；反过来则不成立，因为他们可能摸过暹罗猫。产生这种蕴涵方向的语境就是向下蕴涵语境，而它似乎正是允准否定极性项的条件。"},{"en":"A context without negation behaves differently. From They petted cats, the proper subset sentence They petted Siberian Forest Cats does not follow; instead the subset sentence entails the superset sentence, so this context is upward-entailing, and NPIs are not licensed in it. The conditional antecedent behaves like negation: if buying pumpkins earns free candy, then buying orange pumpkins also earns free candy, so the superset sentence entails the subset sentence, and the environment is downward-entailing. Thinking in terms of denotations and sets therefore proves genuinely useful.","zh":"不含否定的语境表现不同。由 They petted cats 推不出 They petted Siberian Forest Cats；相反，是含真子集的句子蕴涵含真超集的句子，因此这是向上蕴涵语境，否定极性项在其中不被允准。条件句先行语则与否定相似：如果买南瓜就能得到免费糖果，那么买橙色南瓜也必然能得到免费糖果，于是含真超集的句子蕴涵含真子集的句子，该环境属于向下蕴涵。可见，从指称和集合的角度思考意义确实很有用。"}]},"chapter-087-slug-7-summary":{"title":{"en":"7.14 Summary","zh":"7.14 小结"},"summary":{"en":"This chapter surveyed several kinds of linguistic meaning: entailment versus implicature, sense versus denotation, lexical versus sentential meaning, and compositional versus non-compositional meaning. The main lesson is that asking what an expression means is a complicated question, because meaning has many layers, each of which sheds light on how language users produce and understand it.","zh":"本章考察了若干种语言意义：蕴涵与会话含义、涵义与指称、词汇意义与句子意义、组合性意义与非组合性意义。核心结论是，“这到底是什么意思”是一个复杂的问题：语言意义具有多个层面，每个层面都为语言使用者如何产生和理解意义提供了重要洞见。"},"points":[{"en":"Linguistic meaning comes in several kinds, including entailment versus implicature and sense versus denotation.","zh":"语言意义有多种类型，包括蕴涵与会话含义之别、涵义与指称之别。"},{"en":"We also distinguished lexical from sentential meaning and compositional from non-compositional meaning.","zh":"本章还区分了词汇意义与句子意义，以及组合性意义与非组合性意义。"},{"en":"Because meaning has many layers, asking what an expression means is a complicated question.","zh":"由于意义具有多个层面，“某个表达是什么意思”是一个复杂的问题。"}],"terms":[{"en":"entailment","zh":"蕴涵","defEn":"A necessary meaning relation between sentences: if one is true, the other must be.","defZh":"句子之间必然的意义关系：若一句为真，另一句也必定为真。"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"Meaning suggested by an utterance but not literally entailed by it.","defZh":"话语所暗示、但并不由字面内容所必然蕴涵的那一层意义。"},{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The meaning stored in the mental lexicon, as opposed to denotation.","defZh":"储存在心理词库之中的那层意义，与指称（外延）相对。"},{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延/指称","defEn":"The set of things in the world that an expression applies to.","defZh":"表达式在现实世界中所适用的对象所构成的集合，如 cat 指称所有猫。"},{"en":"compositionality","zh":"组合性原则","defEn":"The meaning of a whole is built from the meanings of its parts.","defZh":"整体表达式的意义由其组成部分的意义及组合方式共同决定。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"The chapter examined different kinds of linguistic meaning. It contrasted entailments with implicature, sense with denotation, lexical with sentential meaning, and compositional with non-compositional meaning. Each contrast isolates a distinct layer of interpretation that a theory of meaning has to account for.","zh":"本章考察了不同类型的语言意义：蕴涵与会话含义的对比、涵义与指称的对比、词汇意义与句子意义的对比，以及组合性意义与非组合性意义的对比。每一组对比都区分出意义理论必须解释的一个不同层面。"},{"en":"The take-away is that what does this mean is a complicated question. Linguistic meaning has many layers, and each layer provides important insight into how language users produce and understand meaning.","zh":"本章的要点在于，“这到底是什么意思”是一个复杂的问题。语言意义包含许多层面，每个层面都为我们理解语言使用者如何产生和解读意义提供了重要的洞见。"}]},"chapter-088-exercises-7":{"title":{"en":"7.15 Exercise your linguistics skills","zh":"7.15 锻炼你的语言学技能"},"summary":{"en":"This closing set of exercises applies the semantic tools of Chapter 7. The first asks students to list ten words denoting members of the vehicle category and to judge which are more or less prototypical, explaining the properties involved. The second imagines a debate opponent who denies that drip meaning ‘style’ is a real word because the Oxford English Dictionary lacks it. The third weighs the pros and cons of sense versus denotation, and of lexical meaning as definitions versus as concepts.","zh":"本节练习把第 7 章的语义学工具付诸实践。第一题要求列举十个表示 vehicle（交通工具）范畴成员的词，判断哪些是较典型、哪些是较不典型的成员，并说明决定典型程度的属性。第二题设想辩论中有人说，用 drip 表示“风格”不算真词，因为《牛津英语词典》没有这种用法，请思考如何回应。第三题讨论两种意义观的利弊：涵义与指称之别，以及词汇意义究竟是词库中的定义还是概念。"},"points":[{"en":"List ten words that denote members of the vehicle category and rank them by prototypicality.","zh":"列举十个表示 vehicle 范畴成员的词，并按照典型程度从高到低为它们排出顺序。"},{"en":"Explain which properties make something a more prototypical or a less prototypical member of the category.","zh":"说明究竟哪些属性使某物成为该范畴较典型或较不典型的成员。"},{"en":"Respond to a debate opponent who denies that drip meaning ‘style’ is a real word because the Oxford English Dictionary omits it.","zh":"回应辩论对手的说法：他因《牛津英语词典》未收录而否认表示“风格”的 drip 是真词。"},{"en":"Compare the advantages and disadvantages of thinking about linguistic meaning as sense versus as denotation.","zh":"比较把语言意义看作涵义与看作指称这两种思路各自的优点与缺点。"},{"en":"Compare lexical meaning as definitions stored in the lexicon with lexical meaning as concepts.","zh":"比较把词汇意义视为词库中储存的定义与视为概念这两种立场的得失。"}],"terms":[{"en":"prototype","zh":"原型","defEn":"The most representative member of a category, against which other members are judged.","defZh":"范畴中最具代表性的成员，其他成员以它为参照来判断典型程度。"},{"en":"category","zh":"范畴","defEn":"A class of things grouped together, such as the class of vehicles.","defZh":"归为一类的一组事物，例如 vehicle 这一由各类交通工具构成的范畴。"},{"en":"sense","zh":"涵义","defEn":"The meaning a word has within the language system and in relation to other words.","defZh":"词在语言系统内部所具有的意义，尤指它与其他词之间的意义关系。"},{"en":"denotation","zh":"外延/指称","defEn":"The set of things in the world that a word or expression refers to.","defZh":"词或表达式在现实世界中实际指称的对象集合，与涵义相对。"},{"en":"lexicon","zh":"词库","defEn":"The store of words and their meanings that a speaker possesses.","defZh":"说话者或语言使用者所掌握的全部词及其词汇信息的存储库，即词库。"},{"en":"concept","zh":"概念","defEn":"A mental representation that a word is taken to stand for.","defZh":"词所对应的心理表征，即人脑中对某一类事物所形成的概括性概念。"}],"examples":[{"text":"vehicle","noteEn":"The category whose ten members students list and rank by prototypicality.","noteZh":"作为样板的范畴，学生需列举其十个成员并按典型程度排序。"},{"text":"drip to mean ‘style’","noteEn":"A disputed word use said to be missing from the Oxford English Dictionary.","noteZh":"一个有争议的词用法，据称未被《牛津英语词典》收录。"},{"text":"Oxford English Dictionary","noteEn":"The dictionary cited as an authority in the debate scenario.","noteZh":"辩论场景中被对手当作权威依据加以援引的词典。"},{"text":"Sense vs. denotation","noteEn":"One of the two contrasts that the advanced exercise asks students to evaluate.","noteZh":"高级题要求学生评价的两组对立之一，另一组是词汇意义的定义观与概念观。"}],"body":[{"en":"The exercises in this section apply the chapter's semantic concepts to concrete cases rather than reviewing them in the abstract. The first is a basic task about categorization: students take the category vehicle, list ten words that denote its members, and then decide which of those words name more prototypical vehicles and which name less prototypical ones. The follow-up question asks them to articulate the properties that make something a central rather than a marginal member of the category, so that an intuitive ranking is supported by explicit criteria. The task thus tests the idea that membership in a category comes in degrees.","zh":"本节练习把本章的语义学概念应用到具体案例上，而不是抽象地复述它们。第一题是基础题，关注范畴化：学生以 vehicle 这一范畴为对象，列举十个表示其成员的词，再判断其中哪些词指称较典型的交通工具、哪些指称较不典型的交通工具。后续问题要求学生说明，究竟是什么属性使某物成为该范畴的中心成员而非边缘成员，从而用明确的标准支持自己的直观排序。该题由此检验“范畴成员资格存在程度差异”这一观点，说明典型性并非全有或全无，而是可以分级的。"},{"en":"The second exercise poses a debate scenario of intermediate difficulty. Someone claims that drip, used to mean ‘style’, is not a real word, on the grounds that this use does not appear in the Oxford English Dictionary. Students must decide how to respond, which requires separating the question of whether a form is used by speakers from the question of whether a particular dictionary has recorded it. A dictionary documents past usage under editorial constraints, so its silence is not proof that a word does not exist.","zh":"第二题是中等难度题，设定了一个辩论场景。有人主张，用 drip 表示“风格”不算真词，理由是《牛津英语词典》中没有收录这种用法。学生需要考虑如何回应。回答这一问题时，需要把“某个形式是否为说话者所用”与“某部词典是否收录了它”这两个问题区分开来。词典主要记录词的历史使用情况，其收录与否受编纂体例和语料范围的限制，因此某词未被收录并不能证明它不存在；说话者实际使用的词汇远比任何一部词典的收录更为丰富。"},{"en":"The advanced exercise asks for a balanced discussion of two ways of thinking about linguistic meaning. The first contrast is between sense and denotation: sense concerns a word's meaning inside the language system, while denotation concerns what it refers to in the world. The second is between treating lexical meaning as definitions in the lexicon and treating it as concepts. For each pair, students should set out the advantages and disadvantages rather than simply choosing a side, since each position explains some facts well and leaves others unexplained.","zh":"第三题是高级题，要求对两种思考语言意义的方式作出利弊权衡。第一组对立是涵义与指称：涵义关注词在语言系统内部的意义及其与其他词的关系，指称则关注词与外部世界对象之间的对应。第二组对立是把词汇意义看作词库中的定义，还是看作概念：前者便于形式化和词典编纂，后者更能解释推理与范畴化。对每一组对立，学生都应列出各自的优点与缺点，而不是简单地选边站，因为每种立场都能解释部分语言事实，同时又留下另一些事实未能说明。"}]},"part-008-chapter-8-pragmatics":{"title":{"en":"Chapter 8: Pragmatics","zh":"第8章 语用学"},"summary":{"en":"This chapter studies sentential meaning through its relation to context, the domain called pragmatics. The first half traces how implicatures arise in conversation, examining Grice’s Cooperative Principle and cross-linguistic differences in conversational rules. The second half turns to illocutionary meaning and formal notions of context such as the Common Ground and the Question Under Discussion.","zh":"本章从语境如何影响意义、意义又如何影响语境的角度考察句子意义，这一研究领域称为语用学。前半部分追溯会话中会话含义的产生机制，评估格莱斯的合作原则，并比较不同语言与文化在会话规则上的差异；后半部分转向施为意义，引入共同背景、讨论中的问题等语境的形式化概念。"},"points":[{"en":"Pragmatics studies how context affects meaning and how utterances in turn affect context.","zh":"语用学研究语境如何影响意义，以及话语如何反过来影响语境。"},{"en":"The chapter opens with conversational logic and Grice’s Cooperative Principle, evaluated as a theory.","zh":"本章先讨论会话逻辑与格莱斯的合作原则，并将其作为理论加以评估。"},{"en":"It later analyses illocutionary meaning, comparing assertions with questions in discourse.","zh":"随后分析施为意义，比较断言与疑问在话语中的不同作用。"},{"en":"Formal notions such as the Common Ground and the Question Under Discussion sharpen the notion of context.","zh":"共同背景与讨论中的问题等形式化概念使语境这一概念更加清晰。"},{"en":"Learning goals include classifying implicatures and entailments, and appreciating neurodiversity and cultural diversity.","zh":"学习目标包括区分会话含义与蕴涵，并理解神经多样性与文化多样性。"}],"terms":[{"en":"pragmatics","zh":"语用学","defEn":"the study of how context affects sentential meaning","defZh":"研究语境如何影响句子意义的学科领域"},{"en":"Cooperative Principle","zh":"合作原则","defEn":"Grice’s foundational principle governing conversational contributions","defZh":"格莱斯提出、支配会话中话语贡献的基础性原则"},{"en":"illocutionary meaning","zh":"施为意义","defEn":"what an utterance does to the context in discourse","defZh":"话语在话语语境中所产生的行为效果意义"},{"en":"Common Ground","zh":"共同背景","defEn":"formal notion of the shared assumptions in a conversation","defZh":"会话中双方共同假定内容的形式化概念"},{"en":"Question Under Discussion","zh":"讨论中的问题","defEn":"formal notion of the question a discourse is addressing","defZh":"当前话语正在回答的那个问题的形式化概念"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"This chapter examines sentential meaning from the angle of context: how context shapes what a sentence conveys, and how producing a sentence changes the context in return. The study of that two-way relationship is pragmatics. Chapter 7 established that sentences give rise to implicatures that depend on the discourse context, and the present chapter begins by asking what conversational logic makes those implicatures arise in the first place.","zh":"本章从语境的角度考察句子意义：语境如何塑造句子所传达的内容，以及说出一个句子又如何反过来改变语境。研究这种双向关系的领域就是语用学。第7章已经说明，句子会产生依赖话语语境的会话含义，本章则首先追问：究竟是怎样的会话逻辑使这些会话含义得以产生。"},{"en":"The first half of the chapter therefore starts from the foundational work of the philosopher Paul Grice, above all the Cooperative Principle, and evaluates it as a theory while setting out the basic principles of conversational logic. Because such principles may not be shared everywhere, the discussion also considers what differences languages and cultures display in their conversational rules.","zh":"因此，本章前半部分从哲学家保罗·格莱斯的基础性工作出发，尤其是合作原则，在阐述会话逻辑基本原理的同时，把该原则作为一个理论来评估。由于这类原则未必为所有社群共有，讨论还考察不同语言与文化在会话规则上表现出的差异。"},{"en":"The latter half turns to illocutionary meaning, that is, what happens to the context when an utterance is made. Here the focus falls on the formal analysis of asserting versus asking a question in discourse. To make that analysis precise, the chapter introduces formal notions of context, notably the Common Ground and the Question Under Discussion, which help clarify what a context actually is and what it means for something to happen to it.","zh":"后半部分转向施为意义，即说出话语时语境中“发生”了什么。此处的重点是对话语中断言与提问的形式化分析。为使分析更精确，本章引入语境的形式化概念，特别是共同背景与讨论中的问题，它们有助于厘清语境究竟是什么，以及所谓语境“发生变化”到底意味着什么。"}]},"chapter-089-slug-8-1-at-issue-vs-non-at-issue-meaning":{"title":{"en":"8.1 At-issue vs. non-at-issue meaning","zh":"8.1 命题内容意义与非命题内容意义"},"summary":{"en":"The section separates the literal, surface meaning of a sentence from the other meanings it carries. The literal meaning is called at-issue meaning, while implicatures and presuppositions count as non-at-issue. Sentential negation supplies a diagnostic: negating the sentence cancels its at-issue meaning, may leave the implicature intact, and never removes the presupposition.","zh":"本节把句子的字面意义、表层意义与它携带的其他意义区分开来。字面意义称为命题内容意义，而会话含义与预设属于非命题内容意义。句子否定提供了一项诊断：否定会取消句子的命题内容意义，可能保留会话含义，却绝不会消除预设。"},"points":[{"en":"At-issue meaning is the literal, surface meaning, the main topic the sentence is discussing.","zh":"命题内容意义是字面的表层意义，即句子正在讨论的主要议题。"},{"en":"Implicatures and presuppositions are non-at-issue meanings rather than part of the surface content.","zh":"会话含义与预设属于非命题内容意义，而非表层内容的一部分。"},{"en":"Sentential negation targets the at-issue meaning and necessarily cancels it.","zh":"句子否定以命题内容意义为目标，并且必然将其取消。"},{"en":"An implicature may survive negation because it is compatible with the negated sentence.","zh":"会话含义可能在否定后仍然成立，因为它与否定句并不冲突。"},{"en":"A presupposition always survives negation and must still be assumed true.","zh":"预设总能经受否定，在否定句中仍必须被假定为真。"},{"en":"Other textbooks may use truth-conditional and non-truth-conditional meaning for these two notions.","zh":"其他教材可能用真值条件意义与非真值条件意义来指称这两个概念。"}],"terms":[{"en":"at-issue meaning","zh":"命题内容意义","defEn":"the literal surface meaning that is the main topic of discussion","defZh":"句子的字面表层意义，是当前讨论的主要议题内容"},{"en":"non-at-issue meaning","zh":"非命题内容意义","defEn":"meaning outside the surface content, such as implicature or presupposition","defZh":"表层内容之外的意义，如会话含义与预设"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"a context-dependent non-entailment suggested by a sentence","defZh":"句子依据语境所暗示、而非必然推出的意义"},{"en":"presupposition","zh":"预设","defEn":"what is already assumed true when a sentence is uttered","defZh":"说出句子时已被假定为真的那部分内容"},{"en":"sentential negation","zh":"句子否定","defEn":"negation of a whole sentence, as in it is not the case that","defZh":"对整个句子进行的否定，如“并非……的情况”"},{"en":"truth-conditional meaning","zh":"真值条件意义","defEn":"an alternative label for at-issue meaning in other textbooks","defZh":"其他教材中用来指称命题内容意义的另一种说法"}],"examples":[{"text":"Ode’imin and strawberry mean the same thing.","noteEn":"The sense or denotation interpretation of lexical meaning across Ojibwe and English.","noteZh":"该例说明奥吉布瓦语词与英语词可在涵义或指称上被解释为意义相同。"},{"text":"I said coffee is just as tasty as tea, but I didn’t mean it.","noteEn":"Here mean concerns the speaker’s sincerity and a commitment being retracted.","noteZh":"这里的 mean 涉及说话人的真诚，以及撤回先前作出的承诺。"},{"text":"What do you mean, Mounissa bought 10lbs of strawberries?","noteEn":"The question asks about the implicature rather than the compositional sense.","noteZh":"该问句追问的是会话含义，而不是句子的组合性字面意义。"},{"text":"It is not the case that Mounissa will buy the discounted strawberries.","noteEn":"Negation cancels the at-issue meaning while the presupposition and implicature survive.","noteZh":"否定取消了命题内容意义，而预设与会话含义仍然保留下来。"}],"body":[{"en":"Chapter 7 distinguished genuine linguistic meaning from other uses of the word mean, such as asking what a floating tea stalk means or noting, in Japanese, that imi-ga nai, there is no point. For words, meaning can be analysed either as sense or as denotation, so ode’imin and strawberry may share a sense or point to the same fruit. A further use appears when a speaker says they did not mean something: the issue there is sincerity and a commitment made in discourse, not the sense of the words.","zh":"第7章把真正的语言意义与 mean 一词的其他用法区分开来，例如追问立起来的茶梗意味着什么，或指出日语中 imi-ga nai、即没有意义。就词而言，意义既可以按涵义分析，也可以按指称分析，因此 ode’imin 与 strawberry 可以共有同一涵义，也可以指向同一种水果。mean 还有一种用法出现在说话人表示自己并非当真时：此时涉及的是真诚与话语中作出的承诺，而不是词本身的涵义。"},{"en":"A sentence can also mean more than what it literally states. Asking What do you mean, Mounissa bought 10lbs of strawberries? is unlikely to target the compositional content, which is clear enough: someone exchanged money for strawberries weighing ten pounds. What is really being questioned is the implicature, and since an implicature is not an entailment, it shifts with the context: given a love of jam, the sentence suggests jam-making, while elsewhere it might suggest strawberry lemonade.","zh":"句子还可能传达超出字面陈述的内容。问“What do you mean, Mounissa bought 10lbs of strawberries?”时，针对的通常不是组合性内容，因为这一内容已经足够清楚：某人花钱买了十磅草莓。真正被追问的是会话含义；由于会话含义不是蕴涵，它会随语境而变：若知道 Mounissa 喜欢果酱，该句便暗示她在做果酱，而在别的语境中也可能暗示她在做草莓柠檬水。"},{"en":"This textbook calls the literal surface meaning the at-issue meaning, since it is the main topic being discussed; issue here simply means topic, not problem. Whatever falls outside that surface content is non-at-issue meaning, and implicatures are one such type. In declaratives, negating with it is not the case that provides a diagnostic. Negation necessarily cancels the at-issue meaning and may leave an implicature standing, but it never removes a presupposition: the definite determiner the still presupposes that discounted strawberries exist.","zh":"本教材把字面的表层意义称为命题内容意义，因为它是当前讨论的主要议题；这里的“议题”只指话题，并不含“问题”那样的负面意味。表层内容之外的部分即非命题内容意义，会话含义就是其中一类。在陈述句中，用“并非……的情况”进行否定可作诊断：否定必然取消命题内容意义，可能让会话含义继续成立，却绝不会删除预设——定指限定词 the 仍然预设打折草莓的存在。"},{"en":"The pattern is therefore that negating a sentence makes its implicature possibly disappear, whereas its presupposition always survives. Some linguists use the labels truth-conditional meaning and non-truth-conditional meaning for these two categories, but this textbook prefers at-issue and non-at-issue, since presuppositions remain tied to truth in the sense that they must hold before the sentence is uttered.","zh":"由此可归纳出规律：否定一个句子，其会话含义可能随之消失，而预设却始终保留。有些语言学家用真值条件意义与非真值条件意义来指称这两类意义，但本教材更倾向使用命题内容意义与非命题内容意义，因为预设仍与真值相关——它必须在句子说出之前就成立。"}]},"chapter-090-slug-8-2-cross-community-differences-in-discourse":{"title":{"en":"8.2 Cross-community differences in discourse","zh":"8.2 话语中的跨社群差异"},"summary":{"en":"Conversational rules differ from one language community to another, so what counts as friendly, polite or honest varies. The author describes learning sarcasm in American English as a child and finding that it did not transfer to Japanese, a contrast supported by Ziv’s study. Anthropological work likewise shows that imperatives and requests are evaluated differently across cultures, which calls for suspending negative judgements.","zh":"不同语言社群的会话规则并不相同，因此什么算友好、礼貌或诚实也各不相同。作者回忆童年在美国英语中学会反讽，却发现这一策略在日语中行不通，Ziv 的研究支持了这一对比。人类学工作同样表明，祈使句与请求在不同文化中获得不同评价，因此应当避免由此产生负面评判。"},"points":[{"en":"Conversational rules vary across language communities, so politeness and friendliness are culture-specific.","zh":"会话规则因语言社群而异，礼貌与友好的标准具有文化特殊性。"},{"en":"The author learned sarcasm as a child immigrant and it failed in a Japanese conversation.","zh":"作者童年移民时学会反讽，但在一次日语对话中并未奏效。"},{"en":"Ziv found American students more sarcastic than Japanese students, supporting the anecdote.","zh":"Ziv 发现美国学生比日本学生更常使用反讽，印证了这一轶事。"},{"en":"Imperatives may be perceived as rude more often in Australia than in China.","zh":"祈使句在澳大利亚比在中国更容易被视为粗鲁。"},{"en":"What counts as being honest, and even whether fiction counts as a lie, can differ by community.","zh":"什么算诚实，甚至虚构是否算说谎，都可能因社群而异。"}],"terms":[{"en":"sarcasm","zh":"反讽","defEn":"saying blatantly false things to be funny, sassy or mean","defZh":"为逗趣、耍贫或贬损而故意说出明显虚假的话"},{"en":"imperative","zh":"祈使句","defEn":"a command form, as in cut down that branch","defZh":"表示命令的句式，如砍下那根树枝"},{"en":"politeness","zh":"礼貌","defEn":"a culturally variable evaluation of how an utterance comes across","defZh":"对某话语给人的感受所作的评价，因文化而异"},{"en":"Maxim of Quality","zh":"质量准则","defEn":"Grice’s principle that conversational contributions should be honest","defZh":"格莱斯提出的准则，要求会话中的话语应当诚实"},{"en":"discourse rule","zh":"话语规则","defEn":"a community-specific norm governing how conversation is conducted","defZh":"某一社群特有的、规范会话如何进行的准则"}],"examples":[{"text":"waa, kore cho: okaidoku-da-ne","noteEn":"The author’s Japanese sarcastic remark about expensive jewellery, met with confusion.","noteZh":"作者用日语对昂贵首饰说的反讽之语，却换来对方困惑的表情。"},{"text":"Why don’t you close the window?","noteEn":"A polite request in Canadian English whose Polish equivalent implies stubbornness.","noteZh":"在加拿大英语中是礼貌请求，其波兰语对应说法却暗示对方固执。"},{"text":"Dlaczego nie zamkniesz okna","noteEn":"The Polish literal equivalent, understood as blaming the addressee.","noteZh":"波兰语的逐字对应说法，会被理解为对听话人的责备。"}],"body":[{"en":"Anyone who has conversed across cultural backgrounds has probably met with miscommunication, because each language community follows its own conversational rules. Having immigrated to the United States from Japan at age six, the author found American rules strikingly different, and one thing that had to be learned explicitly was sarcasm. As a nine-year-old, it seemed that Americans said blatantly false things in order to be funny, sassy or mean.","zh":"与不同文化背景的人交谈过的人多半都遇到过误解，因为每个语言社群都遵循自己的会话规则。作者六岁从日本移民到美国，觉得美国的会话规则与日本差别极大，其中需要专门学会的一项就是反讽。在九岁的作者看来，美国人常为逗趣、耍贫或贬损而说出明显虚假的话。"},{"en":"That lesson was put to the test one summer while visiting a friend in Japan. Pointing at extremely expensive jewellery in a magazine, the author proudly tried the newly acquired tactic in Japanese, saying waa, kore cho: okaidoku-da-ne, roughly Wow, that is such a good deal! The result was a look of pure confusion. Such anecdotal experience is backed by research: Ziv (1988) found American students to be more sarcastic than Japanese students.","zh":"这一课在某个夏天拜访日本朋友时经受了检验。作者指着一本杂志里极其昂贵的首饰，得意地用日语使出刚学会的策略，说 waa, kore cho: okaidoku-da-ne，大意是“哇，这真是划算得很！”结果对方一脸茫然。这类个人经验也得到研究支持：Ziv（1988）发现美国学生比日本学生更常使用反讽。"},{"en":"Beyond sarcasm, judgements of politeness vary widely. Anthropological research shows that imperatives such as cut down that branch may be perceived as rude more often in Australia than in China. In Canadian English, Why don’t you close the window? can be perfectly polite, yet its literal Polish equivalent, Dlaczego nie zamkniesz okna, would suggest stubbornness on the addressee’s part.","zh":"除了反讽，对礼貌的判断也差异悬殊。人类学研究显示，诸如“cut down that branch”这类祈使句在澳大利亚比在中国更容易被视为粗鲁。在加拿大英语中，“Why don’t you close the window?”完全可以是很礼貌的请求，但其波兰语逐字对应说法“Dlaczego nie zamkniesz okna”却会暗示听话人固执。"},{"en":"Honesty is usually treated as a central conversational principle, yet what counts as being honest also differs: in some communities any falsehood, including fiction, is counted as a lie. Studying pragmatics therefore requires awareness of norms beyond one’s own. Unfamiliar discourse rules can cause surprise, and that is understandable, but such surprise should not harden into negative judgements about other cultures and languages, since all forms of language are valid.","zh":"诚实通常被视为会话的核心原则，但什么算诚实同样因社群而异：在某些社群中，任何虚假陈述，包括虚构，都被算作说谎。因此研究语用学必须意识到自身之外的规范。陌生的会话规则会带来意外之感，这无可厚非，但不该让这种意外固化为对其他文化与语言的负面评判，因为一切语言形式都是有效的。"}]},"chapter-091-slug-8-3-semantics-and-pragmatics-in-the-legal-domain":{"title":{"en":"8.3 Semantics and pragmatics in the legal domain","zh":"8.3 法律领域中的语义学与语用学"},"summary":{"en":"Meaning matters in law, where how a word or an utterance is interpreted can change lives. This section examines four cases: a 1960 contract dispute over chicken, a 2017 ruling about dawg, the perjury case Bronston v. United States, and an Israeli case about emojis. Together they show why both semantics and pragmatics matter.","zh":"意义在法律中至关重要：一个词或一句话如何被解释，可能改变人的命运。本节考察四个真实案例：1960 年围绕合同用词 chicken 的纠纷、2017 年关于 dawg 的裁定、伪证案 Bronston v. United States，以及以色列一起涉及表情符号的小额索赔案。这些案例共同表明，语义学与语用学在法律领域都不可或缺。"},"points":[{"en":"Words are not static: context fixes interpretation, and everyday ambiguity is usually harmless.","zh":"词语的意义并非固定不变，语境决定如何解释，而日常歧义通常无伤大雅。"},{"en":"In the chicken contract case the court favoured the defendant because the general reading had been used during negotiation.","zh":"在 chicken 合同案中，法院支持被告，因为谈判过程中曾出现过 chicken 的一般性用法。"},{"en":"The dawg case treated dog as genuinely ambiguous, setting aside dialect and its use as a form of address.","zh":"dawg 案把 dog 视为真正有歧义的词，却忽略了方言因素以及它作为称呼语的用法。"},{"en":"Bronston said nothing false, yet withholding information created an implicature; the Supreme Court ruled it was not perjury.","zh":"Bronston 所说的话并无虚假，但隐瞒信息产生了会话含义；最高法院裁定这不构成伪证罪。"},{"en":"In Dahan v. Haim the tenant was held accountable for the optimism conveyed by emojis in a text message.","zh":"在 Dahan v. Haim 案中，租客要为短信中表情符号所传达的乐观态度承担责任。"}],"terms":[{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"Meaning suggested by an utterance but not entailed by it, and cancellable.","defZh":"话语所暗示但并非由句子蕴涵的意义，它可以被取消，也可能随语境改变。"},{"en":"entailment","zh":"蕴涵","defEn":"A meaning that necessarily follows from a sentence being true.","defZh":"由句子为真而必然推出的意义，它不可能被取消，也不依赖语境。"},{"en":"ambiguity","zh":"歧义","defEn":"The property of having more than one possible interpretation.","defZh":"一个表达式具有不止一种可能解释的性质，例如 dog 既可指犬，也可用作称呼语。"},{"en":"perjury","zh":"伪证罪","defEn":"The crime of lying under oath while giving testimony in court.","defZh":"在法庭作证时宣誓后故意说假话所构成的犯罪，即通常所说的伪证罪。"},{"en":"reliance","zh":"信赖（reliance）","defEn":"A contract-law ground for suit when one party does not follow through on a deal.","defZh":"合同法上的一种起诉依据：一方未履行双方已达成的约定时，另一方可据此请求损害赔偿。"}],"examples":[{"text":"That cat is so cute!","noteEn":"Said of a digital drawing, where cat means 'drawing of a cat' rather than an animal.","noteZh":"面对一幅数码画作说出的一句话，其中 cat 意为「猫的画」，而非真正的猫。"},{"text":"Why don’t you just give me a lawyer dawg","noteEn":"Demesme's request for counsel, read by the court as possibly asking for a canine lawyer.","noteZh":"Demesme 请求律师时所说的话，法院却将其解读为可能在要一只犬类律师。"},{"text":"The company had an account there for about six months, in Zürich.","noteEn":"Bronston's literally true answer, which implicated that he personally had no such account.","noteZh":"Bronston 字面上完全属实的回答，却暗示他本人没有瑞士账户。"},{"text":"Good Morning 😊 interested in the house 💃👯‍♀️✌️☄️🐿 🍾 just need to discuss the details…When’s a good time for you?","noteEn":"The text message whose emojis the judge read as conveying optimism and intent to rent.","noteZh":"这条短信中的表情符号被法官解读为传达了乐观态度与租房意向。"}],"body":[{"en":"Linguistic meaning shapes our lives, and it matters greatly in law, from how a contract is read to how a courtroom statement is interpreted. Words are not static: context decides which interpretation is intended. Everyday speakers resolve this ambiguity without harm, but in a legal setting an interpretation can be life-changing.","zh":"语言意义影响着我们的生活，而它最重要的用武之地之一便是法律：从合同如何解读，到法庭陈述如何理解，皆在其中。词语的意义并非固定不变，语境决定说话人意图表达的是哪一种解释。日常交谈中，人们通常能无伤害地化解这类歧义；但在法律场景中，一种解释可能改变人的命运。"},{"en":"In Frigaliment Importing Co. v. B. N. S. Int’l Sales Corp. (1960), the dispute concerned chicken in a contract. The plaintiff understood it as young stewing chicken, while the defendant read it generally and sent mature frying chicken, which the plaintiff called a breach. Since chicken is used for young birds in the poultry trade, the court asked whether the general reading was also possible; it ruled for the defendant, because chicken had been used generally at least once during negotiation.","zh":"在 Frigaliment Importing Co. v. B. N. S. Int’l Sales Corp.（1960）一案中，争议围绕合同中的 chicken 一词。原告把它理解为「（年幼的）炖煮用鸡」，被告则取更笼统的意思，于是发来成熟肉鸡，原告认为这违反了合同。由于在家禽行业内 chicken 确实指幼鸡，法院必须判定笼统解释在此语境下是否同样可行；最终法院支持被告，理由是在谈判过程中 chicken 至少有一次被用作一般意义。"},{"en":"State of La. v. Demesme (2017) turned on dog. Questioned by police, Demesme asked for counsel, saying Why don’t you just give me a lawyer dawg. The prosecution treated this as ambiguous, possibly a request for a canine lawyer, and the Louisiana Supreme Court ruled for the state, so no lawyer was provided. Dawg was in fact a form of address like dude, and Demesme spoke African American English, linking the case to Chapter 2 on language and power.","zh":"State of La. v. Demesme（2017）一案的焦点是 dog。Demesme 在接受警方讯问时请求律师，说的是 Why don’t you just give me a lawyer dawg。控方认为这句话至少有歧义，甚至可能是在索要一只犬类律师；路易斯安那州最高法院最终支持州一方，Demesme 当时没有获得律师。事实上 dawg 是类似 dude 的称呼语，且 Demesme 讲非裔美国人英语，涉及语言与权力。"},{"en":"Bronston v. United States (1973) shows implicature at work. Asked whether he had ever had a personal Swiss bank account, Bronston answered that the company had an account there for about six months, in Zürich. His words contained no lie and did not entail that he lacked an account; the court merely inferred it. The Supreme Court held this was not perjury, since Bronston believed his answer true and the examiner should have asked follow-up questions. In Dahan v. Haim (2017), by contrast, an Israeli court held a tenant accountable for the optimism conveyed by emojis, after a landlord removed an apartment ad in reliance on the message.","zh":"Bronston v. United States（1973）展示了会话含义如何起作用。被问及是否曾拥有个人瑞士银行账户时，Bronston 回答公司在那里、在苏黎世有个账户，开了大约六个月。他的话中并无谎言，也不蕴涵他本人没有账户，法院只是从中推断而已。最高法院裁定这不构成伪证罪，因为 Bronston 真心认为回答属实。与之相对，在 Dahan v. Haim（2017）中，房东因信赖短信内容而下架出租广告，法庭判定租客要为表情符号传达的乐观态度负责。"}]},"chapter-092-slug-8-4-conversational-implicatures":{"title":{"en":"8.4 Conversational implicatures","zh":"8.4 会话含义"},"summary":{"en":"This section reviews the difference between entailments and implicatures. An entailment cannot be cancelled without contradiction, while an implicature can. Two contrasts show the point: cancelling an entailment yields a contradictory sentence, while cancelling an implicature leaves a perfectly acceptable one. Implicatures also shift with context, whereas entailments hold in every context, so implicatures are called conversational implicatures because they arise from conversational rules.","zh":"本节回顾蕴涵（entailment）与会话含义（implicature）之间的根本区别。蕴涵不可取消，一旦取消就会产生矛盾；会话含义则可以取消。取消蕴涵会得到自相矛盾的句子，取消会话含义则得到完全可接受的句子。会话含义还会随语境变化，同一个句子在不同语境中可以传达相反的含义，而蕴涵在任何语境下都成立。正因为这类含义源自会话规则，它们才被称为会话含义。"},"points":[{"en":"Implicatures are cancellable, so they are not entailments of the sentence that carries them.","zh":"会话含义可以被取消，因此它们并不是承载它的那个句子的蕴涵。"},{"en":"Cancelling an entailment produces contradiction, marked with the absurdity symbol, while cancelling an implicature does not.","zh":"取消蕴涵会产生矛盾，用矛盾符号标记；取消会话含义则不会。"},{"en":"The same sentence can carry opposite implicatures depending on the context in which it is uttered.","zh":"同一个句子在不同语境中可以传达完全相反的会话含义，由此可见其语境依赖性。"},{"en":"This is a book about the history of China always entails This is a book, in every context.","zh":"同一句子在一切语境下都蕴涵它是一本书，这一点不随语境改变。"},{"en":"Context-dependent implicatures are called conversational because they arise out of conversational rules.","zh":"依赖语境的会话含义之所以称为「会话的」，是因为它们源自会话规则。"}],"terms":[{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"Non-entailed meaning that a sentence may carry in a given context and that can be cancelled.","defZh":"句子在特定语境中可能带有、且可被取消的非蕴涵意义，它会随语境改变。"},{"en":"entailment","zh":"蕴涵","defEn":"A meaning guaranteed by the truth of the sentence, independent of context.","defZh":"由句子为真所保证的意义，与语境无关，也无法被取消，取消它会形成矛盾。"},{"en":"cancellable","zh":"可取消的","defEn":"Able to be denied without contradiction, as implicatures are.","defZh":"可以被否定而不产生矛盾的特性，会话含义便具有这一性质。"},{"en":"context","zh":"语境","defEn":"The surrounding situation and assumptions that shape what an utterance conveys.","defZh":"决定话语传达何种意义的周围情境与背景假设，会话含义尤其依赖它。"},{"en":"conversational implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"An implicature derived from conversational rules rather than from sentence meaning alone.","defZh":"由会话规则而非仅由句子意义推导出的会话含义，因而依赖语境。"}],"examples":[{"text":"This is a book about the history of China.","noteEn":"The sentence used throughout to contrast entailments with context-dependent implicatures.","noteZh":"贯穿本节用于对比蕴涵与依赖语境的会话含义的句子。"},{"text":"This is a book about the history of China, but it is not the case that this is a book.","noteEn":"Cancelling the entailment This is a book, which is contradictory and impossible.","noteZh":"取消蕴涵 This is a book 之后，句子自相矛盾，这样的说法根本无法成立。"},{"text":"This is a book about the history of China, but it is not the case that this book is long.","noteEn":"Cancelling the possible implicature that the book is long, which is perfectly acceptable.","noteZh":"取消「这本书很长」这一可能的会话含义，句子完全可以接受。"},{"text":"This is a book.","noteEn":"The entailment that survives in every context, whether Josh likes the book or not.","noteZh":"无论 Josh 喜不喜欢这本书，都在任何语境下成立的蕴涵。"}],"body":[{"en":"This section opens with a review of entailments versus implicatures. Implicatures are cancellable: they are not entailments, so they need not be true whenever the sentence is true. Adding but it is not the case that this is a book to This is a book about the history of China cancels an entailment, and the result is contradictory. Adding but it is not the case that this book is long cancels a possible implicature, and the result is perfectly fine.","zh":"本节开篇回顾蕴涵与会话含义之别。会话含义可以取消：它们不是蕴涵，因此句子为真时它们不必为真。在 This is a book about the history of China 后接上 but it is not the case that this is a book，是在取消一个蕴涵，结果自相矛盾。若接上 but it is not the case that this book is long，则是在取消一个可能的会话含义，结果完全正常。"},{"en":"Because an implicature is not an entailment, it can change with the context. Uttered when Danny knows that Josh is a history enthusiast, This is a book about the history of China implicates that Danny thinks Josh will like the book. In a context where world history has never been Josh's forte, the same sentence is much harder to read that way, and the more natural implicature is the opposite one. The context, not the sentence alone, decides which implicature is available.","zh":"由于会话含义不是蕴涵，它会随语境变化。当 Danny 知道 Josh 是历史爱好者时，说出 This is a book about the history of China 会隐含「Danny 认为 Josh 会喜欢这本书」。而在「世界历史从来不是 Josh 的长项」这一语境下，同一句话就很难作此解读，更自然的会话含义恰恰相反。决定采用哪一种会话含义的是语境，而不是句子本身。"},{"en":"The entailment, by contrast, never goes away. No matter what the context is, This is a book about the history of China entails This is a book, in the enthusiast scenario and in the uninterested scenario alike. This is the crucial difference: implicatures are context-dependent, entailments are not. It is also why implicatures of this kind are called conversational implicatures, since they arise out of conversational rules.","zh":"相比之下，蕴涵从不消失。无论在哪种语境下——无论 Josh 是爱好者还是毫无兴趣——This is a book about the history of China 都蕴涵 This is a book。这正是关键差异：会话含义依赖语境，蕴涵则不依赖。也正因这类含义源自会话规则，它们被称为会话含义，而会话规则正是下一节的主题。"},{"en":"This distinction matters because it explains how one sentence can convey something the speaker never literally said. Since an implicature is a non-entailment, it can be denied without contradiction, and a hearer who feels misled cannot point to anything the speaker actually stated. Entailments offer no such escape: denying one produces an impossible sentence, marked with the absurdity symbol. Keeping the two apart will matter when we see how implicatures are calculated in discourse. Entailments, by contrast, cannot vary with the context, because they follow from the sentence alone.","zh":"这一区分之所以重要，是因为它解释了同一个句子如何能够传达说话人从未明说的内容。既然会话含义不是蕴涵，它就可以在不产生矛盾的情况下被否认；听者即便觉得自己受到误导，也无法指出说话人究竟说过哪句不实之词。蕴涵则没有这样的余地：否认蕴涵会产生一个不可能的句子，书中用矛盾符号标记这种荒谬。把两者区分开来，在下一节考察会话含义如何在话语中被计算出来时尤为关键。蕴涵恰好相反，它不可能随语境而变，因为它只由句子本身决定。"}]},"chapter-093-slug-8-5-the-cooperative-principle":{"title":{"en":"8.5 The Cooperative Principle","zh":"8.5 合作原则"},"summary":{"en":"Grice's Cooperative Principle explains why implicatures arise: discourse participants assume that everyone is working toward a shared conversational goal, and this assumption is spelled out in four maxims, Quality, Quantity, Relation and Manner. The section illustrates each maxim and shows that what counts as cooperative varies across languages and communities.","zh":"Grice 的合作原则解释了会话含义为何产生：话语参与者假设大家都在为共同的会话目标而努力，这一假设具体化为四条准则——质量、数量、关联与方式。本节逐一举例说明各条准则，并指出「怎样才算合作」在不同语言、不同言语共同体乃至不同话语类型中都会有差异。"},"points":[{"en":"When Bo answers He fed the cat, Aya infers that Raj did not clean the litterbox.","zh":"当 Bo 回答 He fed the cat 时，Aya 便推断 Raj 没有清理猫砂盆。"},{"en":"The Cooperative Principle holds that discourse participants assume everyone acts to accomplish conversational goals.","zh":"合作原则认为，话语参与者都假设大家的行为是为了达成会话目标。"},{"en":"Grice described four maxims, stated as imperatives but meant to describe pragmatic knowledge, not prescribe good usage.","zh":"Grice 提出四条准则，它们以祈使句形式表述，但意在描述语用知识，而非规定人该如何说话。"},{"en":"Quality requires saying what you believe and have evidence for; in Mopan, even unintentional falsehood counts as tus.","zh":"质量准则要求说自己相信且有证据的话；在莫潘语中，即便无心的虚假也算作 tus（说谎）。"},{"en":"Quantity requires being as informative as needed; informativity is measured by entailment relations.","zh":"数量准则要求提供所需的信息量；信息量依据蕴涵关系来衡量。"},{"en":"Relation requires staying on topic and Manner requires clarity, brevity and order; politeness may be a further maxim.","zh":"关联准则要求切题，方式准则要求清晰、简洁、有条理；礼貌也可能构成又一条准则。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Cooperative Principle","zh":"合作原则","defEn":"Grice's proposal that discourse participants assume everyone is working toward conversational goals.","defZh":"Grice 提出的主张：话语参与者假设大家都在为达成会话目标而努力。"},{"en":"maxim","zh":"准则","defEn":"A general rule of conversational conduct whose assumption helps explain implicatures.","defZh":"会话行为的一般规则，假设人们遵守它有助于解释会话含义的产生。"},{"en":"Maxim of Quality","zh":"质量准则","defEn":"Say what you believe to be true, and only what you have sufficient evidence for.","defZh":"说自己认为真实的话，并且只说自己拥有充分证据的话，违反它即是说谎。"},{"en":"Maxim of Quantity","zh":"数量准则","defEn":"Be neither more nor less informative than the purpose of the conversation requires.","defZh":"信息量既不多于也不少于会话目的所需，而信息量由蕴涵关系来衡量。"},{"en":"Maxim of Relation","zh":"关联准则","defEn":"Make your contributions relevant to what is currently being discussed.","defZh":"使自己对话语的贡献与当前正在讨论的内容相关，即通常所说的切题。"},{"en":"Maxim of Manner","zh":"方式准则","defEn":"Be as clear, brief and orderly as possible when contributing to a conversation.","defZh":"在会话中尽可能清晰、简洁、有条理地表达，不让表达方式妨碍信息传递。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Aya: Did Raj feed the cat and clean the litterbox? Bo: He fed the cat.","noteEn":"The recurring exchange whose implicature each of the four maxims helps to explain.","noteZh":"贯穿本节的对话，四条准则各自都有助于解释其中产生的会话含义。"},{"text":"She is either in the house or at the market.","noteEn":"A Malagasy answer whose disjunction invites different inferences than it would in English.","noteZh":"马达加斯加语中的一个回答，其中的选言结构引发的推断与英语不同。"},{"text":"tus","noteEn":"The Mopan term for lying, applied even to a sincerely believed falsehood.","noteZh":"莫潘语中表示「说谎」的词，即使说话人真心相信虚假之事也照样适用。"},{"text":"When I was little, my favourite food was chicken nuggets.","noteEn":"Bo's off-topic reply, which violates the Maxim of Relation rather than Quality or Quantity.","noteZh":"Bo 跑题的回答，违反的是关联准则，而非质量或数量准则。"}],"body":[{"en":"The section begins with Aya asking whether Raj fed the cat and cleaned the litterbox; Bo answers only that he fed the cat, so Aya infers the litterbox was not cleaned. Grice proposed that such reasoning rests on the Cooperative Principle: the assumption that discourse participants act to accomplish conversational goals. The maxims' imperative wording describes pragmatic knowledge, not good usage.","zh":"本节从例子入手：Aya 问 Raj 是否喂了猫并清理了猫砂盆，Bo 只回答喂了猫，Aya 便推断猫砂盆没有清理。说话人制造会话含义，受话人作出推断。Grice 提出，这类推理建立在合作原则之上，即假设所有话语参与者都以达成会话目标的方式行事。准则以祈使句表述，是对语用知识的描述，而非规定性的劝告；而「怎样才算合作」在不同共同体和不同话语类型之间也有差异。"},{"en":"The Maxim of Quality says to say what you believe to be true and only what you have sufficient evidence for; a violation means overt lying. Failure to try is what counts as a violation, so a sincerely believed falsehood is not a lie in English. This varies across languages: in Mopan, a Mayan language of Central America, falsehoods are called tus whether or not the speaker knew they were false, and tus carries moral disapproval. Mopan speakers who learned that The Jungle Book was untrue classed it as tus.","zh":"质量准则要求说自己相信为真的话，并且只说自己有充分证据的话；违反它即是公然说谎。英语中只有「未加努力」才算违反，因此真心相信的假话并不算说谎。这一点在不同语言中并不一致：在莫潘语（Mopan，中美洲玛雅语族的一种原住民语言）中，不论说话人当时是否知道其为假，虚假一律称为 tus，而 tus 带有道德谴责意味。莫潘语使用者得知《森林王子》并非真事之后，把它归为 tus。"},{"en":"The Maxim of Quantity requires being as informative as needed and no more. Informativity is defined by entailment: if p entails q and the two are not the same sentence, p is more informative than q. Saying only He fed the cat when the litterbox was also cleaned, or listing every cat action, would both violate Quantity. Withholding information is not always uncooperative: in Malagasy, an Austronesian language of Madagascar, exclusive knowledge is highly valued.","zh":"数量准则要求提供所需的信息量，且不多不少。信息量由蕴涵关系来界定：若 p 蕴涵 q 且二者不是同一句子，则 p 比 q 信息量更大。若 Bo 明知猫砂盆也清理了却只说 He fed the cat，就违反了数量准则；而把与猫有关的所有动作一一列举，同样违反。不过，隐瞒信息并不总被视为不合作：在马达加斯加使用的南岛语系语言马达加斯加语（Malagasy）中，独占信息备受看重。"},{"en":"The Maxim of Relation asks speakers to stay on topic, so Bo's reply about chicken nuggets feels odd because the topic was childhood extracurricular activities; he was neither untruthful nor badly calibrated in quantity. The Maxim of Manner asks for clarity, brevity and order, which is why hand-washing instructions given out of sequence sound strange, and it covers word choice, ambiguity, speed and loudness. Manner is culturally variable, and Grice expected further maxims beyond the four. Politeness is a candidate: Pfister proposes not imposing on the addressee and approving their desires and actions.","zh":"关联准则要求说话人切题，因此 Bo 那句关于鸡块儿的回答显得突兀，因为话题本是童年课外活动；他的话既非不实，信息量也谈不上失调。方式准则要求清晰、简洁、有条理，这正是洗手步骤被打乱顺序时听起来古怪的原因，它还涵盖选词、歧义、语速与音量。方式准则存在文化差异，而 Grice 本人也认为四条准则之外可能还有更多准则。礼貌便是候选之一：Pfister 提议的礼貌准则要求不强加于受话人，并对受话人的愿望与行为表示认可。"}]},"chapter-094-slug-8-6-inferences-neurodiversity":{"title":{"en":"8.6 How inferences arise, and neurodiversity in inference making","zh":"8.6 推断如何产生，以及推断过程中的神经多样性"},"summary":{"en":"The Cooperative Principle assumes that conversation partners are rational and cooperative, and this good faith is what allows hearers to derive implicatures. The section shows how Quantity, Quality and Relation each yield different inferences from short answers, then reviews a study in which autistic and non-autistic adults interpreted the same dialogues differently while performing similarly on other linguistic tasks.","zh":"合作原则假定会话参与者理性且愿意合作，正是这种善意信任使听话人能够推导出会话含义。本节说明数量、质量与相关三条准则如何从简短回答中推导出不同含义，并介绍一项研究：自闭症成人与非自闭症成人对同一对话的推断存在差异，但在其他语言任务上表现相当。"},"points":[{"en":"Under the Cooperative Principle, hearers assume speakers are rational and cooperative, which licenses implicatures.","zh":"在合作原则下，听话人默认说话人理性且愿意合作，这一假设使会话含义得以产生。"},{"en":"Quantity, Quality and Relation each drive a different inference from the same short answer.","zh":"数量、质量与相关三条准则会分别从同一简短回答中推导出不同的会话含义。"},{"en":"Inference making shows individual variation; autism is one factor that shapes the strategies people use.","zh":"推断方式存在个体差异，自闭症是影响人们采用何种推断策略的因素之一。"},{"en":"In the study, autistic adults chose Yes or Don't know more often than non-autistic adults.","zh":"在该研究中，自闭症成人选择 Yes 或 Don't know 的比例高于非自闭症成人。"},{"en":"When forced to choose, autistic participants mostly gave the same answer as non-autistic participants.","zh":"当被迫在 Yes 与 No 之间选择时，自闭症参与者大多给出了与非自闭症参与者相同的回答。"},{"en":"Conversational expectations are often neurotypical-centric, so patience and giving information explicitly are recommended.","zh":"会话期待往往以神经典型者为中心，因此建议保持耐心，并尽量把重要信息明确说出来。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Cooperative Principle","zh":"合作原则","defEn":"The assumption that conversation partners are rational and cooperative unless circumstances suggest otherwise.","defZh":"假定会话参与者理性且愿意合作，除非出现特殊情况；它是推导会话含义的基础。"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"Meaning that a hearer infers from an utterance beyond what is literally said.","defZh":"听话人从话语中推导出的、超出字面意义的那部分含义。"},{"en":"Maxim of Quantity","zh":"数量准则","defEn":"The expectation that speakers give as much information as is required, and no more.","defZh":"要求说话人提供所需的信息量，既不少于所需，也不超出所需。"},{"en":"Maxim of Quality","zh":"质量准则","defEn":"The expectation that speakers say only what they believe true and have evidence for.","defZh":"要求说话人只说自己相信为真、且有充分证据支持的话。"},{"en":"neurodiversity","zh":"神经多样性","defEn":"The different ways in which people's brains function and the behaviours that result.","defZh":"人们大脑运作方式的差异，以及由这些神经认知差异所导致的行为差异。"},{"en":"neurotypical","zh":"神经典型","defEn":"A term for people whose neurological development and functioning fit some cultures' socio-political standards.","defZh":"用于描述神经发育与功能符合某些文化社会政治标准的多数人群，常与神经分化者相对。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Aya: Did Raj feed the cat and clean the litterbox? Bo: He fed the cat. (Aya infers: ‘He didn’t clean the litterbox.’)","noteEn":"Faith in the Maxim of Quantity makes this partial answer exclusive.","noteZh":"对数量准则的信任使这一部分回答具有排他性。"},{"text":"Aya: I wonder how the game today went. Bo: The Blue Jays won. (Aya infers: ‘Bo saw the results of the game.’)","noteEn":"The assumed reliable evidence behind the claim follows from Quality.","noteZh":"依据质量准则，听话人假定说话人对该说法有可靠证据。"},{"text":"Aya: Did you vacuum? Bo: The cat is sleeping. (Aya infers: ‘No, Bo did not vacuum.’)","noteEn":"Relation leads the hearer to construct a connection with the question.","noteZh":"相关准则促使听话人构建出与问题相关的联系。"},{"text":"Character 1: Could you hear what the police said? Character 2: There were lots of trains going past.","noteEn":"This is the stimulus dialogue used with autistic and non-autistic participants.","noteZh":"这是研究中用于测试自闭症与非自闭症参与者的对话材料。"}],"body":[{"en":"The Cooperative Principle rests on a basic assumption: in conversation, people extend good faith to one another and presume their partners are rational and cooperative unless something special is going on. That assumption is what allows hearers to derive implicatures. In the exchange where Bo answers that he fed the cat, Aya concludes that he did not clean the litterbox, precisely because she trusts him to obey all four maxims, especially the Maxim of Quantity.","zh":"合作原则建立在一个基本假设之上：在会话中人们彼此抱有善意，默认对方理性且愿意合作，除非出现特殊情况。正是这一假设使听话人能够推导出会话含义。在 Bo 回答「He fed the cat.」的那段对话里，Aya 之所以推断他没有清理猫砂盆，是因为她相信 Bo 遵守全部四条准则，尤其是数量准则。"},{"en":"Other maxims generate other inferences. If Bo says the Blue Jays won, Aya assumes he has reliable evidence, direct or indirect, because the Maxim of Quality forbids saying what you lack evidence for. If Bo answers a question about vacuuming with the remark that the cat is sleeping, Aya assumes the answer is relevant and works out a connection, concluding that he did not vacuum. The same faith drives hearers to link the chicken nuggets answer to the earlier question.","zh":"其他准则会催生别的推断。若 Bo 说蓝鸟队赢了，Aya 便假定他有可靠证据，无论是直接证据还是间接证据，因为质量准则禁止说出自己缺乏证据的话。若 Bo 面对「有没有吸尘」的提问只回答猫在睡觉，Aya 会假定这句话与问题相关，并自行建立联系，从而得出「他没有吸尘」的结论。同样的信任也促使听话人努力把鸡块这个答非所问的回答与前一个问题联系起来。"},{"en":"Not everyone draws these inferences identically. Beyond cross-linguistic and cross-cultural variation, there is individual variation depending on experience, and autism is one relevant factor. In Wilson and Bishop's study, autistic and non-autistic adults judged dialogues such as the police-and-trains exchange. Non-autistic adults usually answered No, whereas autistic adults were about 2.5 times likelier to answer Yes and about 6 times likelier to answer Don't know, while performing comparably on vocabulary, syntactic acceptability and literal comprehension tasks.","zh":"并非所有人都以相同方式作出这些推断。除跨语言、跨文化的差异之外，还存在取决于个人经验的个体差异，自闭症便是其中一个相关因素。在 Wilson 与 Bishop 的研究中，自闭症成人与非自闭症成人对「警察与火车」这类对话进行判断：非自闭症成人通常回答 No，而自闭症成人回答 Yes 的可能性约为前者的 2.5 倍，回答 Don't know 的可能性约为 6 倍；与此同时，他们在词汇、句法可接受性判断和字面意义理解等任务上表现相当。"},{"en":"When the Don't know option was removed, autistic participants who had chosen it answered No about 91% of the time, suggesting that they can reach the intended implicatures when constrained. Several reported frustration at lacking enough information, and one described exhausting anxiety about possible meanings built up over a lifetime of misunderstanding. What counts as adequate evidence may be stricter for some autistic people, and conversational expectations remain neurotypical-centric, so patience and explicit information are advisable.","zh":"当研究者取消 Don't know 这一选项后，原先选择它的自闭症参与者在约 91% 的情况下改答 No，这说明在被迫选择时他们同样能够得出预期的会话含义。也有参与者对信息不足表示不满，其中一位描述了因长期被误解而积累起来的焦虑：不断猜测各种可能含义令人精疲力竭。对部分自闭症者而言，「充分证据」的标准可能更严格，而会话期待仍以神经典型者为中心，因此在交际不畅时应保持耐心，并在传递重要信息时避免依赖会话含义。"}]},"chapter-095-slug-8-7-violating-vs-flouting-a-maxim":{"title":{"en":"8.7 Violating vs. flouting a maxim","zh":"8.7 违反准则与故意违反准则"},"summary":{"en":"A maxim can be violated or flouted, and the two have different consequences. Violating Quality breaks the discourse and forces repair, as when Bo first says he lives in Ottawa and then says Toronto. Flouting means deliberately appearing to violate a maxim in an obvious way in order to create an implicature, often signalled by intonation or facial expression.","zh":"违反准则与故意违反准则会造成不同的后果。违反质量准则会破坏话语进程、迫使双方加以修正，例如 Bo 先说住在渥太华、随后又说住在多伦多。故意违反则是刻意让对方明显察觉自己在违反准则，以产生会话含义，常借助语调或面部表情来提示。"},"points":[{"en":"When a maxim is violated, interlocutors sense that the speaker was uncooperative and that the discourse has gone wrong.","zh":"当准则被违反时，会话参与者会觉得说话人不合作，并认为话语本身出了问题。"},{"en":"A violation such as contradicting an earlier statement forces the facts to be straightened out before talk can continue.","zh":"与先前说法相矛盾之类的违反，必须先澄清事实，会话才能继续。"},{"en":"Flouting a maxim means appearing to violate it very obviously and deliberately in order to implicate something.","zh":"故意违反准则是指刻意、明显地表现出违反准则的样子，以便传达某种会话含义。"},{"en":"Intonation in spoken language and facial expression in spoken and signed language often signal flouting.","zh":"口语中的语调，以及口语和手语中的面部表情，往往可以提示说话人或打手语者正在故意违反准则。"},{"en":"Some people may find a flouted maxim as confusing as an outright violation, so the implicature may need saying aloud.","zh":"有些人可能觉得故意违反与直接违反同样令人困惑，此时需要把会话含义直接说出口。"}],"terms":[{"en":"violating a maxim","zh":"违反准则","defEn":"Breaking a conversational maxim in a way that makes the discourse anomalous and in need of repair.","defZh":"真正破坏某条会话准则，使话语变得异常、需要加以修正的行为。"},{"en":"flouting a maxim","zh":"故意违反准则","defEn":"Obviously and deliberately appearing to violate a maxim in order to create an implicature.","defZh":"明显地、刻意地表现出违反某条准则的样子，以产生会话含义。"},{"en":"Maxim of Quality","zh":"质量准则","defEn":"The expectation that speakers say only what they believe true and have evidence for.","defZh":"要求说话人只说自己相信为真、并有充分证据支持的话；故意说谎即违反此准则。"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"Meaning the hearer infers beyond what the speaker literally says.","defZh":"听话人从话语中推导出的、超出字面意义的那部分含义。"},{"en":"discourse repair","zh":"话语修复","defEn":"The clarification work needed to restore a conversation after something has gone wrong in it.","defZh":"当会话出现问题时，为使对话得以继续而进行的澄清与补救。"}],"examples":[{"text":"(Context: Aya and Bo met online. Aya lives in Vancouver. Bo lives in Ottawa.) Aya: Where did you say you live again? Bo: I live in Ottawa. Aya: So how is Ottawa? Do you like it? Bo: I live in Toronto.","noteEn":"Contradicting himself, Bo violates Quality and the discourse breaks down.","noteZh":"Bo 自相矛盾，违反质量准则，会话因此陷入破裂，必须澄清事实。"},{"text":"(Context: Aya and Bo are discussing what they did over the weekend. They both know there is no city named Toronto in Saskatchewan.) Aya: I visited Toronto over the weekend. Bo: Oh, Toronto, Ontario? Aya: No, Toronto, Saskatchewan. (Implicature: ‘Yes, of course it’s Toronto, Ontario’)","noteEn":"Aya's obviously false answer flouts Quality with a sarcastic intent.","noteZh":"Aya 明知为假却如此回答，以讽刺的方式故意违反质量准则。"}],"body":[{"en":"Earlier sections showed that violating a maxim makes a conversation anomalous. When the violation comes to light, discourse participants feel that the offender was being uncooperative and that something has gone wrong with the discourse itself, so the facts have to be straightened out before the conversation can continue. In the opening dialogue, Bo violates Quality: he says he lives in Ottawa and then, seconds later, that he lives in Toronto, and Aya is left wondering what is going on.","zh":"前几节已经表明，违反准则会使会话变得异常。当违反行为被察觉，参与者会觉得违规者不合作，并认为话语本身出了问题，因此必须先把事实澄清，对话才能继续。在本节开头的对话中，Bo 违反了质量准则：他先说自己住在渥太华，几秒后又说自己住在多伦多，Aya 因而不知所措。"},{"en":"Flouting works differently. Here the speaker acts as though she were violating a maxim, but does so in a way that is very noticeable to the addressee and clearly deliberate, in order to create an implicature. In the second dialogue Aya says she visited Toronto over the weekend when both participants know there is no city named Toronto in Saskatchewan, so her answer is obviously false to both of them; she flouts Quality and implicates that of course she means Toronto, Ontario. Intonation in spoken language and facial expressions in spoken and signed language often help signal that a maxim is being flouted.","zh":"故意违反则不同。此时说话人表现得好像在违反某条准则，但这种表现对听话人而言十分显眼，且明显出于有意，目的是产生会话含义。在第二段对话中，Aya 说自己周末去了多伦多，而双方都知道萨斯喀彻温省并没有叫多伦多的城市，因此这个回答对两人来说都明显为假；她故意违反质量准则，暗示自己当然是指安大略省的多伦多。口语中的语调和口语、手语中的面部表情，常常有助于提示说话人正在故意违反准则。"},{"en":"Not everyone experiences flouting in the same way. As noted in Section 8.6, communication by implicature may not be part of some people's native discourse strategies, so for them the second dialogue can seem just as confusing as the first. In that case the discourse has to be clarified by saying the implicature out loud, for instance by explaining that the remark was sarcastic and that Toronto, Ontario was meant all along. For other people, the flouted example simply does not feel as odd as the outright violation.","zh":"并非每个人都以相同方式体验故意违反。正如 8.6 节所指出的，依靠会话含义进行交流可能并不属于某些人的本族话语策略，因此对他们来说，第二段对话可能与第一段同样令人困惑。此时必须把会话含义直接说出来才能澄清，例如说明自己是在讽刺，指的当然是安大略省的多伦多。而对另一些人来说，故意违反的例子确实不像直接违反那样别扭。"}]},"chapter-096-slug-8-8-more-about-the-cooperative-principle":{"title":{"en":"8.8 More about the Cooperative Principle","zh":"8.8 关于合作原则的进一步讨论"},"summary":{"en":"Grice, a philosopher of language, developed the Cooperative Principle in the 1960s as a basic part of a system he called conversational logic. The section warns against treating perfect maxim-following as an ideal conversation, notes that other factors shape interpretation, and acknowledges that Grice's maxims were based largely on English and have been criticised for treating educated white English as the norm.","zh":"语言哲学家格赖斯在 20 世纪 60 年代提出合作原则，作为其「会话逻辑」体系的基础部分。本节指出，不应把严格遵守准则的对话视为「理想」对话：语篇体裁、互信、心理距离等因素同样参与解读。同时提醒，格赖斯的准则主要基于英语，学界批评其把受过教育的白人英语当作规范。"},"points":[{"en":"Grice developed the Cooperative Principle in the 1960s as a basic part of conversational logic.","zh":"格赖斯于 20 世纪 60 年代提出合作原则，它是会话逻辑体系的基础组成部分。"},{"en":"His aim was to show that ordinary language can be analysed logically, contrary to philosophers who dismissed it as ambiguous.","zh":"他的目标是证明日常语言可以被逻辑地分析，以回应当时认为自然语言过于含混、不合逻辑的哲学家。"},{"en":"A conversation that follows all four maxims perfectly is not necessarily ideal, natural or even comprehensible.","zh":"完全遵守四条准则的对话并不一定是理想的、自然的，甚至未必容易理解。"},{"en":"Genre, mutual trust and psychological distance are among the factors shaping interpretation, so some maxims may be prioritised.","zh":"语篇体裁、双方互信与心理距离都会影响解读，因此不同准则可能被赋予不同的优先级。"},{"en":"Grice's system is deliberately sketchy, based largely on English, and criticised for centring educated white English.","zh":"格赖斯的体系有意留有模糊之处，主要基于英语，并因以受过教育的白人英语为中心而受到批评。"},{"en":"Later theories of pragmatics reduce the maxims to one or two principles, such as Relevance and Informativeness.","zh":"后来的语用学理论把准则简化为一条或两条原则，例如「相关」与「信息量」。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Cooperative Principle","zh":"合作原则","defEn":"Grice's basic principle that discourse participants cooperate, on which his maxims depend.","defZh":"格赖斯提出的基本原则，即会话参与者彼此合作，其四条准则是这一原则的具体体现。"},{"en":"conversational logic","zh":"会话逻辑","defEn":"Grice's system for analysing everyday human discourse in a logical way.","defZh":"格赖斯建立的体系，用以对日常人类话语进行逻辑分析。"},{"en":"Maxim of Relation","zh":"相关准则","defEn":"The expectation that an utterance is relevant to the ongoing conversation.","defZh":"要求话语与当前对话相关；后格赖斯理论中的「相关」原则即由此发展而来。"},{"en":"Informativeness","zh":"信息量","defEn":"One of the principles to which post-Gricean theories reduce Grice's original maxims.","defZh":"后格赖斯理论用来取代原有四条准则的原则之一，常与「相关」并列。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"Paul Grice was a philosopher of language who developed the Cooperative Principle in the 1960s, partly in response to contemporaries who claimed that natural, ordinary language is too ambiguous and too illogical to analyse rigorously. His system, which he called conversational logic, allowed researchers to analyse everyday human discourse logically, and his work became foundational for the subfield of pragmatics.","zh":"保罗·格赖斯是一位语言哲学家，他在 20 世纪 60 年代提出合作原则，部分是为了回应当时一些哲学家的主张：自然语言过于含混、不合逻辑，无法严格分析。他把自己建立的体系称为会话逻辑，使研究者能够对日常话语进行逻辑分析；他的工作也成为语用学这一分支的奠基性成果。"},{"en":"One misconception is that a conversation in which participants follow Quantity, Quality, Relation and Manner perfectly is somehow ideal. Strict adherence may produce talk that is logical and even efficient, but whether it is ideal, natural or comprehensible is another matter. Many linguists, Grice included, have observed that other factors enter into interpretation, such as the genre of discourse, mutual trust and psychological distance between interlocutors; depending on context, some maxims may be prioritised and others deprioritised.","zh":"一个常见的误解是：参与者完美遵守数量、质量、相关和方式四条准则的对话就是「理想」对话。严格遵守准则也许能让对话合乎逻辑、甚至高效，但它是否理想、自然或容易被理解则是另一回事。包括格赖斯在内的许多语言学家都指出，解读话语还涉及其他因素，例如语篇体裁、双方的相互信任以及彼此的心理距离；视语境不同，某些准则可能被优先考虑，另一些则被置于次要位置。"},{"en":"As a philosopher, Grice did not aim to provide extensive empirical observations of human discourse; he wanted a general sketch of how conversational logic might work. Parts of his writing are intentionally vague, since many of his thoughts were introspections rather than a fully developed system, and his musings were based largely on English. He was never clear about whether the maxims applied to non-English conversations, so this textbook takes the view that the Cooperative Principle varies across languages and cultures, while acknowledging that Gricean pragmatics has contributed to treating educated, white English as a neutral norm; the call for more inclusive research practices is echoed here.","zh":"作为一名哲学家，格赖斯的目标并不是对人类话语作大量经验观察，而是勾勒会话逻辑可能如何运作。他的一些论述有意保持模糊，因为许多想法只是内省，而非完整成型的体系，而且主要以英语为据。他从未明确说明这些准则是否适用于非英语会话，因此本教材采取的观点是：合作原则存在跨语言和跨文化的差异。同时我们也承认，格赖斯式语用学助长了把受过教育的白人英语视为中性规范的印象，并在此呼应更包容研究实践的呼吁。"},{"en":"Grice's original work should not be treated as flawless. Many newer theories of pragmatics reduce his maxims to just one or two principles, such as Relevance and Informativeness. Readers who want to go further can consult Kearns for a short summary of post-Gricean approaches, or Huang for a more advanced overview of the history of pragmatics from past to present.","zh":"不应把格赖斯的原始论述视为毫无瑕疵。许多较新的语用学理论把他的多条准则缩减为一两条原则，例如「相关」与「信息量」。若想进一步了解，可参阅 Kearns 对后格赖斯研究路径的简短综述，或参阅 Huang 对语用学从过去到当代历史的、较进阶但易读的概览。"}]},"chapter-097-slug-8-9-illocutionary-meaning":{"title":{"en":"8.9 Illocutionary meaning","zh":"8.9 施为意义"},"summary":{"en":"Illocutionary meaning is the meaning of a sentence in terms of what the speaker or signer means in making an utterance. Using a dinner conversation between roommates, the section contrasts Bo's assertion with Aya's question, then shows that language is performative and that every speech act involves locutionary, illocutionary, and perlocutionary acts. Sentence form does not map one-to-one onto illocutionary meaning.","zh":"施为意义指说话人或手语者在作出话语时所表达的意思。本节以两位室友商量晚餐的对话为例，对比 Bo 的断言与 Aya 的疑问，进而说明语言具有施为性，每个言语行为都同时包含言说行为、施为行为和取效行为。句子形式与施为意义之间并不存在一一对应的关系。"},"points":[{"en":"Illocutionary meaning is what a speaker or signer means in making an utterance, not its literal content.","zh":"施为意义指说话人或手语者在作出话语时所表达的意思，而非句子的字面内容。"},{"en":"Assertions commit the speaker to the truth of the utterance, while questions ask about a proposition's truth value.","zh":"断言使说话人承诺该话语为真，疑问则就命题的真值向受话人发问。"},{"en":"Because language is performative, saying something can bring about conventional consequences such as christening or bequeathing.","zh":"语言具有施为性，说出话语可以产生命名船只、遗赠财物等约定俗成的后果。"},{"en":"Every speech act simultaneously involves a locutionary act, an illocutionary act, and a perlocutionary act.","zh":"每个言语行为都同时包含言说行为、施为行为和取效行为三个层面。"},{"en":"Sentence form does not map one-to-one onto illocutionary meaning, so You got first place! can congratulate or scold.","zh":"句子形式与施为意义并非一一对应，You got first place! 既可表示祝贺也可表示斥责。"}],"terms":[{"en":"illocutionary meaning","zh":"施为意义","defEn":"What a speaker or signer means by making an utterance in discourse.","defZh":"说话人或手语者在话语中作出某一言语时所表达的意思，区别于句子的字面意义。"},{"en":"speech act","zh":"言语行为","defEn":"Any linguistic expression whose purpose is to do something, in any modality.","defZh":"任何以做事为目的的语言表达，可出现在口语、手语等不同模态中。"},{"en":"performative","zh":"施为句","defEn":"An utterance made to do something and bring about a conventional consequence, often with a performative verb.","defZh":"为完成某行为并产生约定俗成后果而说出的话语，常含施为动词，如 I hereby name。"},{"en":"locutionary act","zh":"言说行为","defEn":"The expression of the literal, truth-conditional meaning of the sentence.","defZh":"表达句子字面意义即真值条件意义的行为层面，不涉及说话人的交际意图。"},{"en":"illocutionary act","zh":"施为行为","defEn":"What the speaker does in making an utterance, such as asserting or requesting.","defZh":"说话人在作出话语时所做的事，如断言、请求、承诺、祝贺等。"},{"en":"perlocutionary act","zh":"取效行为","defEn":"The actual effect the utterance has on the addressee.","defZh":"话语对受话人实际产生的效果，也称取效效果，取决于受话人的反应。"}],"examples":[{"text":"We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge.","noteEn":"Bo's utterance in the dinner conversation, which counts as an assertion.","noteZh":"晚餐对话中 Bo 说出的话语，表达他相信该句为真，属于一个断言。"},{"text":"Should we have spaghetti for dinner?","noteEn":"Aya's first utterance, which poses a question rather than an assertion.","noteZh":"Aya 的第一句话，提出的是一个疑问而非断言。"},{"text":"You're manspreading","noteEn":"Simultaneously an assertion and a request that the addressee move his legs.","noteZh":"说话人同时作出断言和请求，要求受话人把腿收拢一些。"},{"text":"You got first place!","noteEn":"Can count as congratulating in one context and as scolding in another.","noteZh":"在一种语境中表示祝贺，在另一种语境中则表示斥责。"}],"body":[{"en":"Illocutionary meaning concerns what a speaker means in making an utterance, not the literal content of the sentence. In the roommates' dinner conversation, Bo's utterance We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge has a sense built from its words, but he says it because he believes it and wants Aya to agree, which makes it an assertion. Aya's Should we have spaghetti for dinner? instead poses a question. Assertion versus question classifies sentences by their illocutionary meaning.","zh":"施为意义关注的是说话人在作出话语时所要表达的意思，而不是句子的字面内容。在 Aya 和 Bo 商量晚餐的对话中，Bo 说 We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge，这句话固然有由词汇意义组合而成的涵义，但他说它是因为相信它为真并希望 Aya 同意，这使他作出了一个断言；而 Aya 的第一句话 Should we have spaghetti for dinner? 则是提出疑问。断言与疑问正是按施为意义对句子所作的一种分类。"},{"en":"Language is performative: saying I name this ship the S.S. Anne christens the ship, and writing I bequeath my card collection to my child in a will gives rise to rights about future ownership. Austin held that to say something is to do something, and used performatives for utterances made to do something and bring about a conventional consequence, typically with verbs like I hereby name. Anything expressed in order to do something is a speech act, so even Did you break my bike? invites the addressee to supply information.","zh":"语言具有施为性：说出 I name this ship the S.S. Anne 便完成了命名，在遗嘱中写下 I bequeath my card collection to my child 会产生关于未来财产归属的权利。Austin 认为 to say something is to do something，并用施为句指称这类为做事并带来约定俗成后果而说出的话语，它们通常含有 I hereby name 之类的动词。凡以做事为目的的语言表达都称为言语行为，因此即便只是问一句 Did you break my bike?，也是邀请受话人提供信息的言语行为。"},{"en":"A speech act involves three simultaneous acts. In You're manspreading, the locutionary act gives the literal meaning, namely that the addressee, presupposed male, is sitting with his legs wide apart; the illocutionary act is what she does, asserting and likely also requesting; the perlocutionary act is the effect on the addressee, perhaps that he moves his legs. Form and illocutionary meaning are not one-to-one: You got first place! may congratulate or scold, while T-to-C movement in spoken English typically indicates a question.","zh":"一个言语行为同时包含三种行为。以 You're manspreading 为例：言说行为给出字面意义，即被预设为男性的受话人正把双腿大张着坐；施为行为是说话人所做的事，她既作出断言，也很可能在提出请求；取效行为则是话语对受话人实际产生的效果，例如他挪动双腿腾出空间。句子形式与施为意义并不一一对应：You got first place! 既可表示祝贺也可表示斥责；不过英语口语中的 T-to-C 移位通常表示正在提出问题。"}]},"chapter-098-slug-8-10-illocutionary-meaning-compositionality":{"title":{"en":"8.10 Thinking about illocutionary meaning compositionally","zh":"8.10 从组合性角度思考施为意义"},"summary":{"en":"Sentential meaning is compositional, but sentences are used inside conversations, so illocutionary meaning must be added to a proposition. The section distinguishes propositions from statements, noting that questions lack truth values yet still involve a proposition. It proposes silent ASSERT and INTERR morphemes in C that supply illocutionary force, and shows that Korean marks these forces overtly with -ta and -nunya.","zh":"句子意义具有组合性，但句子是在对话中使用的，因此必须为命题添加施为意义。本节区分命题与陈述，指出疑问句没有真值却仍然涉及命题；并设定位于 C 的隐性 ASSERT 与 INTERR 语素来提供施为语力，还说明韩语用显性语素 -ta 和 -nunya 标记这两种语力。"},"points":[{"en":"Sentential meaning is compositional, yet sentences are produced within conversations rather than in isolation.","zh":"句子意义具有组合性，但句子是在对话中产生而非孤立存在的。"},{"en":"A proposition is something that can be assigned a truth value; once it is given one, it is a statement.","zh":"命题是可以被赋予真值的东西；一旦被赋予真值，就成为陈述。"},{"en":"Questions have no truth value but still involve a proposition, asking whether it is true or false.","zh":"疑问句没有真值，却仍涉及某个命题，就其为真为假发问。"},{"en":"Silent illocutionary force morphemes ASSERT and INTERR sit in C and combine with their sister TP.","zh":"隐性施为语力语素 ASSERT 和 INTERR 位于 C，与其姐妹节点 TP 组合。"},{"en":"Korean has overt illocutionary morphemes -ta for assertion and -nunya for questions, surfacing sentence-finally.","zh":"韩语用显性语素 -ta 标记断言、-nunya 标记疑问，因中心语后置而出现在句末。"}],"terms":[{"en":"proposition","zh":"命题","defEn":"Something that can be assigned a truth value, such as p belonging to C.","defZh":"可以被赋予真值的东西，如 p∈C，即 Panks 属于猫的集合。"},{"en":"statement","zh":"陈述","defEn":"A proposition that has been given a truth value or truth condition.","defZh":"已被赋予真值或真值条件的命题，与尚未赋值的命题相对。"},{"en":"illocutionary force","zh":"施为语力","defEn":"The utterer's intended illocutionary meaning of a sentence.","defZh":"说话人意图表达的施为意义；只有受话人正确理解，施为行为才算成功。"},{"en":"ASSERT morpheme","zh":"断言语素","defEn":"A silent illocutionary force morpheme in C that turns a proposition into an assertion.","defZh":"位于 C 的隐性施为语力语素，使其姐妹 TP 所表命题成为断言。"},{"en":"INTERR morpheme","zh":"疑问语素","defEn":"A silent interrogative force morpheme in C that asks about a proposition's truth value.","defZh":"位于 C 的隐性疑问语力语素，与命题组合后使其成为疑问句。"},{"en":"head-final","zh":"中心语后置","defEn":"A language type in which the head follows its complements, as in Korean and Japanese.","defZh":"中心语位于补足语之后的语言类型，如韩语和日语，语力语素因此出现在句末。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Panks is a cat","noteEn":"The declarative version, an assertion built on the proposition p belongs to C.","noteZh":"陈述式版本，是基于命题 p∈C 并赋予真值的断言。"},{"text":"Is Panks a cat?","noteEn":"The interrogative version, which asks about the truth value of p belongs to C.","noteZh":"疑问式版本，就命题 p∈C 为真还是为假向受话人发问。"},{"text":"ku-ka seoul-e ka-ass-ta","noteEn":"Korean example glossed 'He went to Seoul', with the overt ASSERT morpheme -ta.","noteZh":"韩语例子，意为 He went to Seoul，带显性断言语素 -ta。"},{"text":"ku-ka seoul-e ka-ass-nunya?","noteEn":"Korean question glossed 'Did he go to Seoul?', with the overt INTERR morpheme -nunya.","noteZh":"韩语疑问句，意为 Did he go to Seoul?，带显性疑问语素 -nunya。"}],"body":[{"en":"Chapter 7 showed that sentential meaning is compositional: in Panks is a cat, the meaning of cat, the set of all cats, combines with the meaning of Panks to yield TRUE if and only if Panks is in that set. That suffices for a sentence considered in isolation, but sentences are part of a larger conversation, and you typically cannot decide alone whether one is true. This chapter therefore treats sentential meaning as collaborative and discourse-based, which largely concerns illocutionary meaning.","zh":"第七章已经说明，句子的意义具有组合性：在 Panks is a cat 中，cat 的意义（所有猫的集合）与 Panks 的意义组合，当且仅当 Panks 属于该集合时为真。这种分析对于孤立考察的句子够用，但句子是更大对话的一部分，你通常无法独自决定它是否为真。因此本章把句子意义视为协作性的、基于话语的，这主要涉及施为意义。"},{"en":"Two terms must be distinguished. Panks is a cat is the declarative version and Is Panks a cat? the interrogative version of the same proposition, that Panks is among the set of cats, or p∈C. A proposition is something that can be assigned a truth value; once it is given a truth value, it is a statement. The question is therefore not a statement, because questions have no truth value, yet it still asks whether p∈C is true or false. In this chapter a TP is simply assumed to be a proposition without a truth value yet.","zh":"有两个术语需要区分。Panks is a cat 是陈述式版本，Is Panks a cat? 则是同一命题的疑问式版本，即 Panks 属于猫的集合（p∈C）。命题是可以被赋予真值的东西，被赋予真值后称为陈述。疑问句没有真值，因而不是陈述，但它仍然就问 p∈C 为真还是为假发问。本章假定 TP 就是一个尚未具有真值的命题。"},{"en":"A proposition alone has no illocutionary meaning, so we posit a silent illocutionary force morpheme. English has a silent ASSERT in C, the head of CP, which adds illocutionary meaning to the proposition of its sister TP: asserting says roughly I believe p∈C is true; do you believe this too? Questions use INTERR instead, meaning roughly Either p∈C is true or p∈C is false; which do you think it is? Korean has overt morphemes for these forces, -ta for assertions and -nunya for questions, appearing sentence-finally because Korean is head-final.","zh":"命题本身没有施为意义，因此我们设定一个隐性语力语素。英语在 C（CP 的中心语）位置有一个隐性 ASSERT，为其姐妹节点 TP 所表示的命题添加施为意义：断言大致相当于我相信 p∈C 为真；你是否也相信？疑问则使用 INTERR，大致相当于 p∈C 要么为真要么为假；你认为哪一种？韩语用显性语素表达这两种语力：-ta 标记断言，-nunya 标记疑问；由于韩语是中心语后置语言，它们出现在句末。"}]},"chapter-099-slug-8-11-what-is-a-context":{"title":{"en":"8.11 What is a context?","zh":"8.11 什么是语境？"},"summary":{"en":"Conversation is a collaborative game in which participants try to determine which propositions are true. This section formalises the context as a collection of sets: the Common Ground of propositions all participants accept, the Question Under Discussion stack of current topics, and each participant's Discourse Commitment set. Illocutionary meaning places a proposition into one or more of these sets.","zh":"对话是一场协作性的游戏，参与者共同弄清哪些命题为真。本节把语境形式化地刻画为一组集合：所有参与者都接受的共同基础、记录当前话题的讨论中的问题堆栈，以及每个参与者的话语承诺集合。施为意义就是把命题放入这些集合中的一个或多个。"},"points":[{"en":"Conversation is a collaborative game in which participants try to determine which propositions are true.","zh":"对话是一场协作性的游戏，参与者共同设法确定哪些命题为真。"},{"en":"The context can be modelled as sets that track discourse information: Common Ground, QUD stack, and Discourse Commitment sets.","zh":"语境可刻画为记录话语信息的集合：共同基础、讨论中的问题堆栈和话语承诺集合。"},{"en":"The Common Ground holds propositions all discourse participants agree to be true, and the goal is to enlarge it.","zh":"共同基础包含所有话语参与者都同意为真的命题，话语游戏的目标就是不断扩大它。"},{"en":"The Question Under Discussion set is a stack of current topics, whose topmost issue is the one being discussed.","zh":"讨论中的问题集合是当前话题的堆栈，最上面的议题就是正在讨论的议题。"},{"en":"Each participant's Discourse Commitment set holds propositions they have publicly committed to; the Common Ground is their intersection.","zh":"每个参与者的话语承诺集合是其公开承诺为真的命题集合，共同基础则是这些集合的交集。"}],"terms":[{"en":"context","zh":"语境","defEn":"The state of affairs in the discourse when an utterance is made, modelled as sets of information.","defZh":"作出话语时话语中的事态，可形式化地刻画为一组记录信息的集合。"},{"en":"Common Ground (CG)","zh":"共同基础","defEn":"The set of propositions that all discourse participants agree to be true.","defZh":"所有话语参与者一致同意为真的命题集合，是话语游戏努力扩大的目标。"},{"en":"Question Under Discussion (QUD) stack","zh":"讨论中的问题堆栈","defEn":"A stack containing the questions or topics currently under discussion in the discourse.","defZh":"存放话语中正在讨论的问题或议题的堆栈，每提出一个问题就加入一个议题。"},{"en":"Discourse Commitment (DC) set","zh":"话语承诺集合","defEn":"The set of propositions a discourse participant has publicly committed to as true.","defZh":"某一话语参与者公开承诺为真的命题集合，也称公共信念集合。"},{"en":"at-issue","zh":"命题内容","defEn":"Being the topic of discussion; issue here simply means topic, not a problem.","defZh":"处于讨论议题之中；issue 在此仅指话题，并无问题、麻烦的负面含义。"},{"en":"discourse participant","zh":"话语参与者","defEn":"A person taking part in the conversation whose beliefs and commitments are tracked.","defZh":"参与会话的人，其公开承诺与信念由相应的话语承诺集合记录。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Is coffee better than tea?","noteEn":"A QUD on which participants fail to agree, so neither answer enters the CG.","noteZh":"参与者无法达成一致的 QUD，因此两个答案都不会进入 CG。"},{"text":"Coffee is better than tea","noteEn":"One competing answer, which does not get added to the Common Ground.","noteZh":"相互竞争的一个答案，不会被加入共同基础。"},{"text":"Tea is better than coffee","noteEn":"The other competing answer, which likewise does not get added to the CG.","noteZh":"另一个相互竞争的答案，同样不会被加入 CG。"}],"body":[{"en":"To see concretely what ASSERT and INTERR do with a proposition, consider why we have conversations. The big question a conversation always tries to answer is what the state of affairs in our world is. Participants are in a collaborative game, working out which propositions are true and which are false, and each player updates the context every time they make an utterance.","zh":"要具体了解 ASSERT、INTERR 对命题做了什么，不妨想想人为什么要对话。对话始终试图回答的大问题是我们这个世界处于什么状态；参与者在一场协作性游戏中共同弄清哪些命题为真、哪些为假，每个玩家每说出一句话就更新一次语境。"},{"en":"Informally, the context of an utterance is the state of affairs in the discourse when it is made: the topic, who is taking part, what has already been said. More formally, it is a collection of sets that track information. The Common Ground (CG) is the set of propositions all participants agree to be true, and the goal of the game is to increase it. Because discourse is collaborative, no one adds propositions singlehandedly; negotiation happens in the Question Under Discussion (QUD) set, which contains the topics being discussed. The QUD set is a stack: picture a stack of papers, each with a question on it, and every question asked adds an issue.","zh":"非正式地说，一句话语的语境就是它说出时话语中的事态：话题是什么、谁参与其中、已经说过什么。更形式化地说，语境是一组记录信息的集合。共同基础（CG）是所有参与者都同意为真的命题集合，话语游戏的目标就是扩大它。由于对话是协作性的，谁也不能单方面加入命题，协商发生在讨论中的问题（QUD）集合中，它包含正在讨论的话题。QUD 集合是一个堆栈：可以把它想成一摞纸，每张纸上写着一个问题，每提一个问题就加入一个议题。"},{"en":"Issue here just means topic of discussion, so something at-issue is the current topic. The topmost issue is what is being discussed, and once it is resolved it is removed from the stack. When participants cannot agree, as with Is coffee better than tea?, neither Coffee is better than tea nor Tea is better than coffee enters the CG. Each participant still has beliefs, tracked in their Discourse Commitment (DC) set; the CG is the intersection of all DC sets, such as DCA ∩ DCB. The context is therefore the CG, the QUD stack, and each participant's DC set, and illocutionary meaning places a proposition in these sets.","zh":"这里的 issue 只表示讨论的话题；说某事 at-issue，就是说它是当前的话题。最上面的议题是正在讨论的议题，一旦得到解决就被移出堆栈。当参与者无法达成一致，例如 QUD 是 Is coffee better than tea? 时，Coffee is better than tea 和 Tea is better than coffee 都不会进入 CG。但每个人仍有自己的信念，这由各自的话语承诺（DC）集合记录；共同基础可以看作所有 DC 集合的交集，如 DCA ∩ DCB。总之，语境就是共同基础、QUD 堆栈和每个参与者的 DC 集合的总和，施为意义就是把命题放入这些集合中。"}]},"chapter-100-slug-8-12-assertion":{"title":{"en":"8.12 Assertion","zh":"8.12 断言"},"summary":{"en":"An assertion, marked by the illocutionary operator ASSERT, does two things: it puts the proposition into the speaker's Discourse Commitment set, and it raises the issue whether that proposition is true at the top of the QUD stack. Because illocutionary meaning takes effect the moment an utterance is made, it cannot be cancelled, which produces Moore's Paradox.","zh":"断言由施为算子 ASSERT 标记，其作用有两方面：把命题放入说话人的话语承诺集，并把该命题是否为真这一问题置于讨论中的问题栈顶端。施为意义在话语说出的瞬间即已生效，无法被取消，因此一边断言一边否认自己的信念会产生摩尔悖论。"},"points":[{"en":"Uttering an assertion publicizes that the speaker believes the proposition to be true.","zh":"说出一个断言即公开表明说话人相信该命题为真。"},{"en":"Denying the asserted belief yields Moore's Paradox, which is odd but not strictly contradictory.","zh":"一边断言一边否认自己的信念会形成摩尔悖论，听起来怪异却并非严格矛盾。"},{"en":"ASSERT p adds p to the speaker's Discourse Commitment set.","zh":"ASSERT p 把命题 p 加入说话人／手语者的话语承诺集。"},{"en":"It also places the question is p true at the top of the QUD stack.","zh":"它同时把 p 是否为真这一问题置于讨论中的问题栈顶端。"},{"en":"Assertions invite agreement or disagreement, but they do not by themselves add p to the Common Ground.","zh":"断言会引来同意或反对，但仅凭断言并不能把 p 加入共同背景。"}],"terms":[{"en":"illocutionary meaning","zh":"施为意义","defEn":"The meaning an utterance carries by being performed, such as committing or asking.","defZh":"话语一经说出便产生的意义，如作出承诺或提出问题。"},{"en":"Moore's Paradox","zh":"摩尔悖论","defEn":"The oddity of asserting p while denying belief in p.","defZh":"断言 p 的同时又否认自己相信 p 所造成的怪异现象。"},{"en":"Discourse Commitment set","zh":"话语承诺集","defEn":"The set of propositions whose truth the speaker has publicly endorsed.","defZh":"说话人已公开认可其为真的命题所构成的集合。"},{"en":"Question Under Discussion (QUD) stack","zh":"讨论中的问题栈（QUD 栈）","defEn":"An ordered stack of the issues currently raised in a discourse.","defZh":"按顺序堆叠当前话语中所提出的诸问题的结构。"},{"en":"Common Ground","zh":"共同背景","defEn":"The propositions all discourse participants take to be true.","defZh":"所有话语参与者都视为真的命题集合。"},{"en":"at-issue meaning","zh":"命题内容意义","defEn":"The literal content of a sentence that is up for debate.","defZh":"句子中可供讨论、争辩的字面内容。"}],"examples":[{"text":"?? It's snowing, but I don't believe that it's snowing.","noteEn":"Asserting p while denying belief in p produces Moore's Paradox.","noteZh":"断言 p 却否认相信 p，由此产生摩尔悖论。"},{"text":"We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge.","noteEn":"Bo's assertion updates his Discourse Commitment set and the QUD stack.","noteZh":"博的断言更新了他自己的话语承诺集以及问题栈。"},{"text":"Yeah.","noteEn":"Aya's agreement shows that an assertion raises an issue for discussion.","noteZh":"阿雅的肯定回应表明断言提出了一个可供讨论的议题。"}],"body":[{"en":"An assertion carries illocutionary meaning contributed by the ASSERT morpheme. In asserting, the speaker publicizes a belief that the proposition is true. Trying to deny that belief while still asserting produces Moore's Paradox, as in ?? It's snowing, but I don't believe that it's snowing. The sentence is odd rather than plainly contradictory, since snow and a non-believer could coexist; but illocutionary meaning simply happens when the utterance is made, so it cannot be cancelled.","zh":"断言所携带的施为意义由 ASSERT 语素贡献。说话人作出断言，就是公开表明自己相信该命题为真。若一边断言、一边否认这一信念，就会形成摩尔悖论，例如「?? It's snowing, but I don't believe that it's snowing.」。这句话之所以怪异，并不是因为它严格自相矛盾——下雪与有人不相信下雪在概念上可以并存——而是因为施为意义在话语说出的瞬间就已发生，无法被取消。"},{"en":"In the dinner conversation, Bo's utterance decomposes as ASSERT [we have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge]. The proposition therefore lands in Bo's Discourse Commitment set.","zh":"在晚餐对话中，博说出的话语可分解为 ASSERT [we have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge]。该命题因此被放入博的话语承诺集。"},{"en":"An assertion does more than publicize a belief. Since the point of conversation is to add propositions to the Common Ground, Bo is seeking Aya's agreement, and his utterance raises an issue. Aya can reply Yeah, or in another context No we don't, which shows that assertions introduce a topic into the QUD stack: is p true? The literal content that is up for debate is the at-issue meaning. Believing something does not make it a fact, so asserting p does not automatically put p into the Common Ground; other participants must agree first.","zh":"断言不止于公开自己的信念。由于会话的目的就是把命题加入共同背景，博实际上是在争取阿雅的认同，因而这句话也提出了一个议题。阿雅可以回答 Yeah，在别的语境中也可以回答 No we don't，可见断言把命题作为讨论话题引入问题栈：p 是否为真？可供争论的字面内容就是命题内容意义。相信某事并不等于它是事实，所以断言 p 不会自动把 p 放入共同背景，必须先取得其他话语参与者的同意。"},{"en":"The illocutionary force of ASSERT is summarized as follows: ASSERT p puts p in the speaker's Discourse Commitment set, and puts is p true? at the top of the QUD stack. Replacing p with the proposition about tomato sauce and ground beef gives the analysis of Bo's utterance. The next section compares this with the illocutionary force of questions.","zh":"ASSERT 的施为力量可以概括为两步：ASSERT p 把 p 放入说话人／手语者的话语承诺集，并把 p 是否为真这一问题置于问题栈顶端。把 p 替换为关于番茄酱和牛肉末的命题，就得到对博那句话的分析。下一节将把这一分析与疑问的施为力量作比较。"}]},"chapter-101-slug-8-13-question":{"title":{"en":"8.13 Question","zh":"8.13 疑问"},"summary":{"en":"The interrogative operator INTERR places the issue whether p is true or whether the negation of p is true at the top of the QUD stack. Unlike ASSERT, a question does not add p to the speaker's Discourse Commitment set, because asking does not require any belief. In the Aya and Bo dialogue, Aya asks whether they can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients.","zh":"疑问施为算子 INTERR 把 p 为真还是非 p 为真这一问题置于问题栈顶端。与 ASSERT 不同，提问不会把 p 加入说话人的话语承诺集，因为提问并不要求任何信念。在阿雅与博的对话中，阿雅询问能否用那些食材做意大利肉酱面。"},"points":[{"en":"INTERR p asks whether p is true or whether its negation is true.","zh":"INTERR p 询问 p 为真还是 p 的否定为真。"},{"en":"A question raises a topic but commits the utterer to no particular proposition.","zh":"疑问提出话题，却不使说话人对任何具体命题作出承诺。"},{"en":"Unlike ASSERT, INTERR p leaves the speaker's Discourse Commitment set unchanged.","zh":"与 ASSERT 不同，INTERR p 不改变说话人的话语承诺集。"},{"en":"The QUD for an assertion is is p true, while for a polar question it is is p true or is not-p true.","zh":"断言的问题栈内容是 p 是否为真，极性问句则是 p 为真还是非 p 为真。"},{"en":"The contrast reflects the speaker's bias in assertions but openness in questions.","zh":"这一差别反映出断言中说话人有偏向，而提问时则持开放态度。"}],"terms":[{"en":"INTERR","zh":"疑问施为算子 INTERR","defEn":"The interrogative illocutionary operator that gives a clause its questioning force.","defZh":"赋予小句提问力量的疑问施为算子。"},{"en":"polar question","zh":"极性问句（是非问句）","defEn":"A question asking whether a proposition is true or false.","defZh":"询问某一命题为真还是为假的问句。"},{"en":"propositional negation","zh":"命题否定","defEn":"Negation of a whole proposition, written with the symbol ¬.","defZh":"对整个命题的否定，用符号 ¬ 表示。"},{"en":"Question Under Discussion (QUD) stack","zh":"讨论中的问题栈（QUD 栈）","defEn":"An ordered stack of the issues currently raised in a discourse.","defZh":"按顺序堆叠当前话语中所提出的诸问题的结构。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Can we make pasta bolognese with those ingredients?","noteEn":"Aya's polar question, analysed as INTERR we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients.","noteZh":"阿雅的极性问句，可分析为 INTERR we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients。"},{"text":"Should we have spaghetti for dinner?","noteEn":"The opening question of the dialogue raises the first issue on the stack.","noteZh":"对话开头的提问把第一个议题放上问题栈。"},{"text":"Is it snowing?","noteEn":"With a polar question the speaker is open to either p or its negation.","noteZh":"使用极性问句时，说话人对 p 及其否定两者都持开放态度。"}],"body":[{"en":"This section analyses the bolded question in the dialogue. Compositionally it breaks down as INTERR we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients, where the illocutionary operator acts on the proposition we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients. Unlike an assertion, a question does not commit the utterer to any particular proposition. Aya may ask whether they can make pasta bolognese without believing that they can; in this context it is natural to think she asks because she genuinely does not know. So INTERR p does not update the speaker's Discourse Commitment set.","zh":"本节分析对话中加粗的疑问句。按组合方式，它可分解为 INTERR we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients，其中的施为算子作用于命题 we can make pasta bolognese with those ingredients。与断言不同，疑问不使说话人对任何具体命题作出承诺。阿雅可以询问能否做意大利肉酱面，却并不相信真的能做；在这个语境中，自然的理解是她确实不知道答案。因此 INTERR p 不更新说话人的话语承诺集。"},{"en":"Questions, like assertions, do raise a topic for discussion. With a yes-no question the speaker asks whether the proposition is true or false. Since the issues in the QUD stack concern what should be added to the Common Ground, the question can be reframed as which one is true and should go in the Common Ground: we can make pasta bolognese, or we cannot.","zh":"与断言一样，疑问确实提出了讨论话题。用是非问句时，说话人询问该命题为真还是为假。由于问题栈中的议题归根结底是哪个命题应当加入共同背景，这个问题可以改写为：哪一个为真、应当进入共同背景——能做意大利肉酱面，还是不能做。"},{"en":"INTERR p puts is p true, or is not-p true? at the top of the QUD stack, where the symbol ¬ stands for propositional negation, meaning it is not the case that. Reading the formula with the pasta proposition gives it is not the case that we can make pasta bolognese, a long-winded way of saying we cannot make pasta bolognese. For ASSERT p the QUD is merely is p true?, which captures the intuition that someone asserting p is biased towards adding p to the Common Ground, whereas a polar question leaves the speaker open to either p or its negation.","zh":"INTERR p 把 p 为真还是非 p 为真置于问题栈顶端，其中符号 ¬ 表示命题否定，意为并非如此。把 p 代入意大利肉酱面的命题，¬p 读作「并非我们能做意大利肉酱面」，也就是「我们不能做意大利肉酱面」的冗长说法。而对 ASSERT p 来说，问题栈上只有 p 是否为真，这正好说明：断言 p 的人倾向于把 p 加入共同背景，而极性问句则让说话人对 p 或其否定都保持开放。"},{"en":"The comparison of the two operators shows that the difference in QUD is what distinguishes asserting from asking. The following section brings these pieces together to show how illocutionary meaning works across a whole conversation.","zh":"两个算子的对比表明，问题栈上的差异正是断言与提问的分界。接下来的部分将把这些环节整合起来，展示施为意义如何贯穿整段会话发挥作用。"}]},"chapter-102-slug-8-14-analysing-meaning-dynamically":{"title":{"en":"8.14 Analysing meaning dynamically","zh":"8.14 动态地分析意义"},"summary":{"en":"Illocutionary meaning can be modelled as updates to a context made up of the Common Ground, the QUD stack and the participants' Discourse Commitment sets. Tracking the Aya and Bo conversation step by step shows how each utterance changes these sets until the QUD stack is empty. This approach is called dynamic semantics; it also models disagreements, where commitment sets change but the Common Ground does not.","zh":"施为意义可以建模为对语境的更新，语境由共同背景、问题栈以及各参与者的话语承诺集构成。逐步追踪阿雅与博的对话，可以看出每次话语如何改变这些集合，直到问题栈清空。这种分析思路称为动态语义学；它同样能刻画意见不合的情形：话语承诺集改变而共同背景不变。"},"points":[{"en":"Every utterance changes the context, so discourse is a sequence of context updates.","zh":"每一次话语都会改变语境，因此话语是语境更新的序列。"},{"en":"An empty starting context contains no propositions, though trivial presuppositions may be taken for granted.","zh":"起始的空语境不含任何命题，但一些琐碎的预设可能已被视为理所当然。"},{"en":"Assertions update the speaker's commitment set and the QUD stack; questions update only the QUD stack.","zh":"断言更新说话人的话语承诺集与问题栈；疑问只更新问题栈。"},{"en":"The Common Ground grows when both participants commit to the same proposition, resolving the topmost question.","zh":"当双方都承诺同一命题时，共同背景随之扩展，最上层的问题得以解决。"},{"en":"Dynamic semantics examines meaning across sentences, including pronoun reference.","zh":"动态语义学跨句考察意义，包括代词指称等现象。"}],"terms":[{"en":"dynamic semantics","zh":"动态语义学","defEn":"A theory treating sentence meaning as an update to the context.","defZh":"把句子意义视为对语境之更新的理论。"},{"en":"context update","zh":"语境更新","defEn":"The change an utterance makes to the discourse context.","defZh":"一次话语给话语语境带来的改变。"},{"en":"Common Ground","zh":"共同背景","defEn":"The propositions shared and agreed on by discourse participants.","defZh":"话语参与者共同认可并共享的命题集合。"},{"en":"presupposition","zh":"预设","defEn":"A proposition already taken for granted before an utterance.","defZh":"在话语说出之前就已被视为理所当然的命题。"},{"en":"set theory notation","zh":"集合论记法","defEn":"Writing sets with curly braces, as in {p, q}.","defZh":"用花括号表示集合的写法，例如 {p, q}。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Should we have spaghetti for dinner?","noteEn":"Aya's opening question updates the QUD stack, producing Context 2.","noteZh":"阿雅开头的提问更新了问题栈，得到语境 2。"},{"text":"This bolognese sauce has no salt in it.","noteEn":"In the disagreement example, Aya asserts a proposition Bo rejects.","noteZh":"在意见不合的例子中，阿雅断言了一个博所否认的命题。"},{"text":"Yes it does! I totally put some in.","noteEn":"Bo's counter-assertion commits him to the opposite proposition.","noteZh":"博的反驳断言使他对相反的命题作出承诺。"},{"text":"{p, q}","noteEn":"Sets are written with curly braces in set theory notation.","noteZh":"在集合论记法中，集合用花括号书写。"}],"body":[{"en":"Illocutionary meaning can be analysed as updates to contexts. Starting from an empty context, in which the Common Ground, the QUD stack and both Discourse Commitment sets are empty, each utterance changes something. Trivial assumptions such as we are in the kitchen may already sit in the Common Ground, provided both take them for granted; these are the propositions usually called presuppositions. Sets are drawn as circles for convenience, though set theory notation writes them with curly braces, as in {p, q}.","zh":"施为意义可以被分析为对语境的更新。从空语境出发——共同背景、问题栈以及双方的话语承诺集均为空——每一次话语都会带来某种改变。像「我们在厨房里」这样的琐碎假设可能已经存在于共同背景中，只要双方都视之为理所当然；这类命题通常称为预设，此处暂不纳入。为便于观察，图中用圆圈表示集合，而集合论记法则用花括号书写，如 {p, q}。"},{"en":"Aya's first utterance, Should we have spaghetti for dinner?, is a question, so it updates the QUD stack, and as the only issue raised so far it sits at the top. The difference between this context and the previous one is exactly the illocutionary meaning of the question. This theory of meaning as context update is sometimes called dynamic semantics; it is dynamic because it looks at meaning cross-sententially, at how utterances relate to one another. It suits phenomena such as pronoun reference, and the framework used here is inspired by Farkas and Bruce (2010).","zh":"阿雅的第一句话 Should we have spaghetti for dinner? 是疑问，因此更新问题栈；由于它是目前为止唯一提出的议题，便位于栈顶。这一语境与此前语境的差别，正是该疑问的施为意义。把意义视为语境更新的理论有时称为动态语义学；它之所以是动态的，是因为它跨句考察意义，即关注不同话语之间如何相互关联。它适用于代词指称一类现象，本节采用的框架受到 Farkas 与 Bruce（2010）研究的启发。"},{"en":"Bo's assertion We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge updates both his Discourse Commitment set and the QUD stack, pushing the earlier question down, since only the topmost question can be addressed at a time. Aya's Yeah adds the proposition to her commitment set as well, so it enters the Common Ground, and the resolved question is removed from the stack. The conversation continues through further questions and assertions until the original issue is answered and the QUD stack is empty.","zh":"博的断言 We have tomato sauce and ground beef in the fridge 同时更新了他自己的话语承诺集和问题栈，并把先前的问题压到下方，因为每次话语只能处理最上层的问题。阿雅回答 Yeah 后，该命题也进入她的话语承诺集，于是进入共同背景，已解决的问题随之从栈中移除。会话继续经过若干提问与断言，直到最初的问题得到回答、问题栈清空。"},{"en":"The system also models disagreement. In the exchange about salt in the bolognese sauce, each participant's commitment set is updated, but because Aya and Bo never settle on the same proposition, the Common Ground stays unchanged. Discourse can therefore be seen as a sequence of updates to the context, driven by the illocutionary meaning of each contribution.","zh":"这一系统也能刻画意见不合。在关于肉酱面里有没有盐的对话中，双方的话语承诺集都被更新，但由于阿雅与博始终未能就同一命题达成一致，共同背景保持不变。因此，话语可以看作由各次贡献的施为意义所驱动的一连串语境更新。"}]},"chapter-103-chapter-8-summary":{"title":{"en":"8.15 Summary (and further questions to consider)","zh":"8.15 小结（以及值得进一步思考的问题）"},"summary":{"en":"This closing section reviews the kinds of meaning covered in the chapter, giving special weight to non-at-issue meaning. It lists at-issue meaning, entailment, implicature, presupposition, and illocutionary meaning, then invites readers to look for further kinds of meaning in imperatives and emojis, and to keep approaching the question What does that mean? like a linguist.","zh":"本节回顾本章讨论的各类意义，重点放在非命题内容意义上。文中依次列出命题内容意义、蕴涵、会话含义、预设与施为意义，并邀请读者继续追问祈使句和表情符号还带来哪些意义，同时希望读者今后遇到「这是什么意思」这一问题时，能像语言学家那样去分析。"},"points":[{"en":"At-issue meaning is the literal meaning of a sentence that is explicitly put up for debate.","zh":"命题内容意义是句子的字面意义，说话人把它明确摆出来供人讨论。"},{"en":"Non-at-issue meaning covers the meanings that a sentence does not explicitly assert, broadly construed.","zh":"非命题内容意义是广义上句子并未明确断言的那些意义。"},{"en":"Entailment is what must be true whenever the sentence is true; implicature is only suggested and depends on context.","zh":"蕴涵是句子为真时必然为真的内容；会话含义只是被暗示出来，且依赖语境。"},{"en":"Presupposition is a special entailment assumed true before the utterance and already in the Common Ground.","zh":"预设是一种特殊的蕴涵，在说出话语之前就被假定为真，并且已经存在于共同背景之中。"},{"en":"Illocutionary meaning is what you do in uttering, and it simply happens rather than being debated.","zh":"施为意义是说话时你所做之事，它是随着说出话语而自然发生的，并不供人争论。"},{"en":"The chapter invites further questions about imperatives, about emojis, and about other kinds of meaning still undiscovered.","zh":"本章还邀请读者继续思考祈使句、表情符号以及其他尚未被发现的意义类型。"}],"terms":[{"en":"at-issue meaning","zh":"命题内容意义","defEn":"The literal content of a sentence that is openly offered for debate.","defZh":"句子的字面内容，说话人把它明确提出来供听者讨论或质疑。"},{"en":"non-at-issue meaning","zh":"非命题内容意义","defEn":"Meaning that is not explicitly asserted, broadly understood.","defZh":"广义上指句子没有明确断言、却仍在传达的那部分意义。"},{"en":"implicature","zh":"会话含义","defEn":"A suggested, non-entailment meaning that depends on context.","defZh":"一种被暗示出来的意义，不是蕴涵，必须结合语境才能推出。"},{"en":"presupposition","zh":"预设","defEn":"A special entailment already assumed true before the utterance is made.","defZh":"一种特殊的蕴涵，在话语说出之前就已被假定为真，属于共同背景的一部分。"},{"en":"illocutionary meaning","zh":"施为意义","defEn":"What a speaker does in making an utterance, such as asserting.","defZh":"说话人在说出话语时所完成的行为意义，例如作出一个断言。"},{"en":"Common Ground","zh":"共同背景","defEn":"The shared assumptions that participants treat as true before an utterance.","defZh":"交际参与者在话语说出之前共同接受为真的那些假定。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Pet the cat!","noteEn":"An imperative that the chapter leaves as an open question for future analysis.","noteZh":"本章举出的祈使句例子，作者把它留作尚待分析的问题。"},{"text":"😂","noteEn":"The laughing-crying emoji, whose contribution to meaning the chapter asks readers to ponder.","noteZh":"哭笑不得的表情符号，本章请读者思考它对意义究竟有何贡献。"},{"text":"What does that mean?","noteEn":"The recurring question that the chapter shows to be far more complicated than it looks.","noteZh":"反复出现的那个问题，本章说明它远比表面上看起来复杂。"}],"body":[{"en":"Chapter 8 examined several distinct kinds of meaning, paying special attention to meanings that are not explicitly asserted. At-issue meaning is the literal content of a sentence that a speaker openly offers for debate. Alongside it sit the non-at-issue meanings, broadly understood as whatever a sentence communicates without asserting it outright. The chapter separated four such cases: entailment, which must hold whenever the sentence is true; implicature, a context-dependent suggestion that is not an entailment; presupposition, an assumption already in the Common Ground before the utterance; and illocutionary meaning, the act performed simply by making the utterance.","zh":"第 8 章考察了若干种不同的意义，尤其关注那些并未被明确断言的意义。命题内容意义是句子的字面内容，说话人把它公开摆出来供人讨论。与之相对的是非命题内容意义，广义上指句子在没有明确断言的情况下所传达的一切。本章区分出其中四种情况：蕴涵，即句子为真时必然成立的内容；会话含义，即依赖语境的暗示，而不是蕴涵；预设，即话语说出之前就已存在于共同背景中的假定；以及施为意义，即仅仅通过说出话语而完成的行为。"},{"en":"Taken together with Chapter 7, this material shows that the innocent question What does that mean? has no simple answer. A single utterance carries many layers of meaning at once, and each layer reveals part of the highly systematic way speakers package what they communicate. The chapter therefore closes by inviting readers to keep looking for further kinds of meaning beyond the ones catalogued here.","zh":"把这些内容与第 7 章合起来看，就会发现「这是什么意思」这个看似简单的问题其实没有简单答案。一句话同时承载着多个层面的意义，而每一层都揭示出说话人组织所传信息时高度系统化的一面。因此本章在结尾邀请读者继续寻找这里所列之外的其他意义类型。"},{"en":"That invitation points back to structures introduced earlier, such as the Common Ground, the Question Under Discussion stack, and the Discourse Commitment sets. It also points to sentence types treated only briefly, notably imperatives like Pet the cat!: what exactly does a command do to the context, and do the tools already introduced suffice to describe it? A final prompt asks what emojis contribute, wondering whether a laughing-crying emoji is an assertion, an implicature, or a presupposition. The possibilities for research, the chapter says, are limitless.","zh":"这一邀请指向前面介绍的几种结构：共同背景、讨论中的问题栈，以及话语承诺集合。它也指向本章只是简略带过的句式，特别是像 Pet the cat! 这样的祈使句：发出命令究竟对语境做了什么，已有的工具是否足以描写它？最后一个问题问的是表情符号贡献了什么意义，一个哭笑不得的表情到底算断言、算会话含义，还是算预设。作者说，可研究的方向是无穷的。"}]},"chapter-104-exercises-8":{"title":{"en":"8.16 Exercise your linguistics skills","zh":"8.16 练习语言学的技能"},"summary":{"en":"This section offers four graded exercises on pragmatics. They ask students to distinguish violating from flouting a maxim, to explain the lawyer dog case through the Cooperative Principle, to argue about the privileged status of Quality, and to represent a WH-question as a disjunction of answers at the top of the Question Under Discussion stack.","zh":"本节给出四道难度递进的语用学练习。它们分别要求学生区分违反准则与故意违反准则、用合作原则解释「律师狗」一案、论证质量准则是否具有优先地位，以及把特殊疑问句表示为讨论中的问题栈顶端由若干可能答案构成的析取。"},"points":[{"en":"Exercise 1, basic, contrasts violating a maxim with flouting it, using the proposed Maxim of Politeness.","zh":"练习 1 属于基础题，要求用学者提出的礼貌准则来对比违反准则与故意违反准则。"},{"en":"Exercise 2, intermediate, asks why the lawyer dog case feels unreasonable, relating it to the Cooperative Principle.","zh":"练习 2 属于中级题，要求结合合作原则解释「律师狗」一案为何让许多人觉得不合理。"},{"en":"Exercise 3, advanced, asks why Quality may be a privileged maxim and what its failure does to the others.","zh":"练习 3 属于高级题，追问质量准则为何可能是优先准则，以及它若不被遵守会带来什么后果。"},{"en":"Exercise 4, advanced, asks how a WH-question is represented as a disjunction of possible answers on the QUD stack.","zh":"练习 4 属于高级题，要求说明特殊疑问句如何表示为讨论中的问题栈上若干可能答案的析取。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Cooperative Principle","zh":"合作原则","defEn":"The assumption that speakers cooperate in conversation, which the maxims spell out.","defZh":"交际中假定说话人彼此合作的总体原则，各条准则正是对它的具体展开。"},{"en":"maxim","zh":"准则","defEn":"One of the conversational rules associated with the Cooperative Principle.","defZh":"与合作原则相联系的一条会话规则，如质量准则、礼貌准则等。"},{"en":"flouting","zh":"故意违反","defEn":"Openly breaking a maxim so that the hearer derives an implicature.","defZh":"公开地不遵守某条准则，从而让听者推导出会话含义。"},{"en":"violating","zh":"违反","defEn":"Quietly breaking a maxim, misleading the hearer rather than implicating.","defZh":"暗中不遵守某条准则，意在误导听者，而不是产生会话含义。"},{"en":"Quality","zh":"质量准则","defEn":"The maxim requiring speakers to say what they believe to be true.","defZh":"要求说话人只说自己认为真实的话、不说无根据之言的那条准则。"},{"en":"Question Under Discussion (QUD) stack","zh":"讨论中的问题（QUD）栈","defEn":"The ordered set of questions that a discourse is currently addressing.","defZh":"话语当前正在处理的问题按顺序排列而成的集合，新问题会置于栈顶。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Give me a canine lawyer","noteEn":"The reading the police attributed to Demesme in the lawyer dog case.","noteZh":"在「律师狗」一案中，警方对 Demesme 那句话所作的解释。"},{"text":"Is that a bean casserole?","noteEn":"A yes-no question whose QUD representation is a disjunction of two answers.","noteZh":"一个是非问句，它在问题栈上的表示是两个可能答案构成的析取。"},{"text":"Who brought the bean casserole?","noteEn":"A WH-question asked at a potluck attended by Aya, Bo, Cai, and Deo.","noteZh":"一个特殊疑问句，情境是 Aya、Bo、Cai 和 Deo 参加的聚餐。"}],"body":[{"en":"The first exercise is pitched at a basic level and draws on the discussion of maxims. Students must explain how violating a maxim differs from flouting one, then illustrate each with the Maxim of Politeness, a fifth maxim that some linguists have proposed. The exercise explicitly invites answers in the student's first language: the task is to imagine that politeness really is a maxim in one's own speech community and to work out what breaking it quietly, versus breaking it openly, would look like there.","zh":"第一题定位为基础难度，取材于关于准则的讨论。学生要说明违反准则与故意违反准则有何不同，再各举一例，所用的准则是有语言学家提出的第五条准则——礼貌准则。题目明确允许用学生的第一语言作答：设想礼貌确实是自己语言社群的准则，然后考虑暗中违反它和公开违反它分别会是什么样子。"},{"en":"The intermediate exercise returns to the lawyer dog case from Section 8.3, in which police read Demesme as meaning Give me a canine lawyer. Students are asked to explain why that interpretation strikes many people as unreasonable, and to connect their reasoning to the Cooperative Principle. The advanced exercise then turns to Grice's remark in Logic and Conversation that the maxims may be ranked by importance. Grice suggested Quality could be the most critical, claiming that the other maxims come into operation only on the assumption that Quality is satisfied. Students must argue why Quality might enjoy this privileged position, and consider what difficulties a discourse with a well-known liar creates for the remaining maxims.","zh":"中级题回到 8.3 节的「律师狗」一案，警方把 Demesme 的话理解为「给我一只犬类律师」。学生要说明为什么这种解读在许多人看来不合理，并把理由与合作原则联系起来。高级题接着讨论 Grice 在《逻辑与会话》中的说法：各条准则在重要性上可能有高低之分。Grice 认为质量准则可能最为关键，并称其他准则只有在质量准则得到满足的前提下才会起作用。学生需要论证质量准则为何可能享有这种优先地位，并思考当话语的参与者是个人所共知的骗子时，其余准则会遇到什么麻烦。"},{"en":"The last exercise concerns illocutionary meaning and the Question Under Discussion stack. It recalls that a yes-no question such as Is that a bean casserole? places at the top of the stack a question representable as a disjunction, namely whether That is a bean casserole is true or whether it is false, since only two answers are available. Students must extend this treatment to a WH-question, Who brought the bean casserole?, asked at a potluck attended by Aya, Bo, Cai, and Deo, and propose what the resulting disjunction of possible answers would look like.","zh":"最后一题涉及施为意义与讨论中的问题栈。题目提醒学生，像 Is that a bean casserole? 这样的是非问句会在栈顶放入一个可表示为析取的问题，即「That is a bean casserole 为真」或「That is a bean casserole 为假」，因为是非问句只有两种答案。学生要把这一分析推广到一个特殊疑问句 Who brought the bean casserole? 上，该句的情境是 Aya、Bo、Cai 和 Deo 参加的聚餐，并说明由此得到的可能答案析取会是什么样子。"}]}});
